WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.004
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:14.590
PRESENTER: Good morning.

00:00:14.590 --> 00:00:17.970
Welcome family and friends
of the MIT graduating

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:20.832
class of 2007.

00:00:20.832 --> 00:00:23.190
[CHEERING]

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:26.700
MCGANN: We're coming to you
live from this grand stage

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:30.000
here at MIT'S Killian
Court, where even right now,

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.369
they are bringing up
those degrees that in just

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:35.100
three short hours,
the graduates will

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:38.220
be receiving those hard-earned
degrees right behind me.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:39.720
My name is Matt McGann.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.780
I'm the associate director
of admissions here at MIT.

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:46.440
I'm also a graduate of
MIT, of MIT class of 2000.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:48.780
So it wasn't very long
ago that I remember taking

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:50.430
the walk across the stage.

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:53.610
And as an admissions officer,
I read the applications

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:56.040
of many of these students
who we'll be seeing today.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:01:00.900
I know them well, and they
are excellent and world class.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:05.459
And it is a privilege to be
here celebrating them today.

00:01:05.459 --> 00:01:06.660
VIELMA: Welcome.

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:10.140
My name is Karina Vielma.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:15.120
I am also a graduate
of MIT, class of 2001.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:21.270
I am assistant dean in the MIT
Office of Minority Education.

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:23.850
Commencement is a
very special time

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:28.980
at MIT, a time where the entire
institute community comes

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:32.910
together to celebrate
the accomplishments

00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:38.010
of a remarkable group of
young men and young women.

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:41.970
We will be visiting with
you for the next 90 minutes

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:45.910
as we await the arrival
of our guests of honor.

00:01:45.910 --> 00:01:49.650
They are currently gathering
in the Johnson Athletic Center

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:51.660
on the west side
of campus, and you

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:55.610
should be able to see
them on the big screen.

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:58.430
They will be joining us
out here in Killian Court

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:01.515
at about 9:50 AM.

00:02:01.515 --> 00:02:03.640
MCGANN: I'm sure you'll
agree that Killian Court is

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:08.949
a grand setting for what will
be MIT'S 141st commencement

00:02:08.949 --> 00:02:10.479
exercises.

00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:12.580
This is the only
space at MIT that

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:17.450
is fit for 13,000 people
will be here today.

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:18.800
It will be a beautiful day.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:21.550
The National Weather
Service is predicting

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:25.450
a perfect day-- no rain,
highs of 77 degrees,

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:26.710
and partly cloudy.

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:31.690
I cannot imagine a more perfect
day for commencement exercises.

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:34.810
Killian Court is also a
space that doesn't always

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:36.640
have these chairs out here.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:38.500
During the school year,
it's a great place

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:42.010
to study, to play
Ultimate Frisbee,

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:44.740
and actually, this
is the place where

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:48.580
our MIT students began
their MIT career,

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:50.530
as well as will end it today.

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:53.260
So just behind me,
below that great dome,

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:54.760
in front of those
great columns, was

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:58.480
where they gathered four short
years ago for their freshmen

00:02:58.480 --> 00:02:58.980
photograph.

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:01.021
Do you remember your
freshman photograph, Karina?

00:03:01.021 --> 00:03:02.440
VIELMA: Yes, I do, Matt.

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:04.630
I remember there was a
hack placed on us too,

00:03:04.630 --> 00:03:06.760
since we were in
that class of 2001.

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:11.050
So we had a hack for the
Space Odyssey 2001 movie

00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:12.389
our here in Killian Court.

00:03:12.389 --> 00:03:14.680
MCGANN: And of course, Killian
Court and the Great Dome

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:19.390
behind us is a classic setting
for MIT'S pranks, which we call

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:21.430
hacks, as you probably know.

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:25.730
That Great Dome behind us
has hosted a police car--

00:03:25.730 --> 00:03:28.540
a full scale police car with
working, flashing lights,

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:29.840
and coffee and--

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:31.090
VIELMA: And donuts, I believe.

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:33.280
MCGANN: And donuts, yes, indeed.

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:36.370
And one year ago,
there was a fire truck

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:40.450
also placed atop that
Dome with a giant ladder.

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:42.940
One of my favorites was when
they turned this Great Dome

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:45.340
behind us into R2-D2.

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:46.930
If you kind of
squint, it looks kind

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:49.720
of like that domed
head of R2-D2.

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:52.390
Do you have a favorite hack
that happened here at MIT?

00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:56.500
VIELMA: Not really, Matt, but
I do have many, many memories

00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:59.950
of walking through Killian
Court my Course 18,

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:03.670
which is mathematics, my Course
18 classes over in building

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:06.580
two, which is over here
by the giant screen.

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:09.520
And it's a great
privilege to be out here.

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:12.490
Because as you walk and--

00:04:12.490 --> 00:04:16.269
as you're walking to class, you
realize the hard-earned degree

00:04:16.269 --> 00:04:18.220
that not everybody receives.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:20.410
It's only people who
graduate from MIT.

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:22.250
We don't give out
any honorary degrees.

00:04:22.250 --> 00:04:23.209
So it is a great honor.

00:04:23.209 --> 00:04:24.583
MCGANN: In the
admissions office,

00:04:24.583 --> 00:04:27.520
we like to say that there's only
one way to earn an MIT degree,

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:28.900
and that's the hard way.

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:31.850
And the graduates today
are proof of that.

00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:33.610
So our goal for
the next hour or so

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:37.990
is to celebrate the members
of the MIT Class of 2007.

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:41.020
We'll show some video clips
of MIT students in action.

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:43.690
We'll interview some
family and friends.

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:45.670
Plus a sampling
of this year's MIT

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:47.890
graduates, we'll
interview them live over

00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:51.420
at the Johnson Athletic Center,
and that'll be pretty exciting.

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:53.230
And we'll take a
look at them as they

00:04:53.230 --> 00:04:57.380
prepare for their wonderful
opportunities ahead.

00:04:57.380 --> 00:04:59.660
VIELMA: Video coverage
of these proceedings

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:03.260
are being webcast live
throughout the world.

00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:05.450
Those of you here
in Killian Court

00:05:05.450 --> 00:05:10.010
will be able to watch a
recorded version of the webcast

00:05:10.010 --> 00:05:13.160
from your home or
office computers.

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:16.230
This year, we will
also be offering

00:05:16.230 --> 00:05:19.020
a podcast of the ceremony.

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:24.780
So if you wish, you're welcome
to download Commencement 2007

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:27.435
to your iPod or
personal digital device.

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:38.880
In February of last year, MIT
began producing a video podcast

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:43.110
magazine with the purpose of
capturing and communicating

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:47.910
the richness and diversity
of the MIT experience.

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:50.280
We call this endeavor ZigZag.

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:51.930
I love that name.

00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:54.420
Each ZigZag episode
features stories

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:59.220
of student life,
research, special events,

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:02.400
interesting people, and
the occasional hack,

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:04.170
as we were discussing.

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:07.922
We will be showing you
sample episodes of ZigZag

00:06:07.922 --> 00:06:08.880
throughout the morning.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:15.240
The first episode was
podcast last summer

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:18.570
and is hosted by my
colleague and fellow MIT

00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:19.710
alum, Matt McGann.

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:22.270
He's a celebrity on campus.

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:28.050
MIT benefits from a vibrant and
very loyal, committed alumni.

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:34.500
You will see the 50th reunion
class, the Class of 1957,

00:06:34.500 --> 00:06:38.310
proudly displaying their
red coats later this morning

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:42.945
walking down Killian Court,
as this next story explains.

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:51.290
MCGANN: Coming to you from
the Stat Center at MIT,

00:06:51.290 --> 00:06:53.170
my name is Matt McGann,
and this is ZigZag.

00:06:53.170 --> 00:06:56.476
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:07:00.950 --> 00:07:02.900
Commencement week is
always a special time

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:05.130
of year for our
graduating students,

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:07.200
but it's also an
occasion for our alumni

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:11.070
to reflect upon how MIT
has shaped their lives.

00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:12.890
Many graduates come
back to campus for Tech

00:07:12.890 --> 00:07:15.700
reunions to keep up with
current events at the Institute

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:17.665
and to catch up
with old friends.

00:07:17.665 --> 00:07:20.380
Tech Reunions 2006
was a festive affair,

00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:23.240
attracting a record number
of alumni and guests

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:26.210
participating in a rich
assortment of activities.

00:07:26.210 --> 00:07:27.170
PRESENTER: Welcome.

00:07:27.170 --> 00:07:30.050
Or should I say to many of
you, welcome back to MIT.

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:36.720
GARVIN: Alumni come back
for a number of reasons.

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:39.620
Some people come to
reconnect with old friends.

00:07:39.620 --> 00:07:42.110
And a lot of people come
to have an opportunity

00:07:42.110 --> 00:07:44.630
to meet the people they
never really got to know.

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:46.910
There are a number of
highlights of Tech Week.

00:07:46.910 --> 00:07:49.500
One of them is our
traditional Tech Night

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:51.710
at Pops, which we've
been doing for 106.

00:07:51.710 --> 00:07:53.330
We buy out Symphony Hall.

00:07:53.330 --> 00:07:56.510
And classes from the senior
class to the oldest alumni

00:07:56.510 --> 00:07:59.090
come, and we have a great time.

00:07:59.090 --> 00:08:02.120
Technology Day, which brings
together a series of faculty,

00:08:02.120 --> 00:08:03.620
is a wonderful
opportunity to get up

00:08:03.620 --> 00:08:05.540
to date on what's
going on at MIT.

00:08:05.540 --> 00:08:09.770
And during Tech Week, we have
one of our three times a year

00:08:09.770 --> 00:08:11.600
broadcast for the
Enterprise Forum,

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:13.190
which is based in
Kresge Auditorium

00:08:13.190 --> 00:08:15.430
and then broadcast
around the world.

00:08:15.430 --> 00:08:17.180
One of the nice things
about Tech reunions

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:19.870
is that you can cut
across all the classes,

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:23.000
and you have an opportunity
to bring together somebody

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:26.101
from the fifth reunion class and
somebody from the 40th reunion

00:08:26.101 --> 00:08:26.600
class.

00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:27.710
And they can talk to each other.

00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:29.865
They can find out how
they built their company.

00:08:29.865 --> 00:08:30.740
They're great events.

00:08:30.740 --> 00:08:32.720
Everyone who comes
wants to come again.

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:34.429
They'd recommend it to a friend.

00:08:34.429 --> 00:08:36.110
So I think the
important thing is

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:38.780
it would be even better if we
can get a larger percentage

00:08:38.780 --> 00:08:40.301
of each class participating.

00:08:42.950 --> 00:08:45.740
MCGANN: For the Class of
1956, this year's reunion

00:08:45.740 --> 00:08:48.810
was especially
memorable, as 2006

00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:50.900
is the year they
finally get to don

00:08:50.900 --> 00:08:54.590
the legendary red jacket,
bestowed upon every MIT

00:08:54.590 --> 00:08:57.360
50-year reunion class.

00:08:57.360 --> 00:08:58.990
BLOTNER: My name is Mal Blotner.

00:08:58.990 --> 00:09:00.890
And I was in Course 10.

00:09:00.890 --> 00:09:03.410
You know that we do
it by numbers here.

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:04.150
ALUMNI: Course 7.

00:09:04.150 --> 00:09:04.920
ALUMNI: Course 16.

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:05.810
ALUMNI: Course 10.

00:09:05.810 --> 00:09:07.400
MCGANN: How did it
feel when you first

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:09.205
got to put on the red jacket?

00:09:09.205 --> 00:09:10.400
CHIHOSKI: Well, exciting.

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:13.340
And this is my dad's red jacket.

00:09:13.340 --> 00:09:16.970
My dad was Class of '28
also in civil engineering.

00:09:16.970 --> 00:09:19.610
GOODWILL: The last time
I was here was 1988.

00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:21.250
My son graduated from here.

00:09:21.250 --> 00:09:22.520
So it was a--

00:09:22.520 --> 00:09:24.560
it brings back a
lot of memories.

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:27.410
MATSA: I worked with a
Whirlwind computer, which

00:09:27.410 --> 00:09:33.297
was a whole room full of tapes
and all kinds of other gadgets.

00:09:33.297 --> 00:09:35.880
INTERVIEWER: Do you remember how
many women were in the class?

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:38.630
WAHL: From what I have
been told and we've

00:09:38.630 --> 00:09:43.370
tried to figure out, if you only
count the entering freshmen,

00:09:43.370 --> 00:09:45.570
it was 13.

00:09:45.570 --> 00:09:46.915
And then, eight graduated.

00:09:46.915 --> 00:09:52.445
BROWN: In '56, there weren't
many people of color.

00:09:52.445 --> 00:09:56.240
I came to MIT
primarily because I

00:09:56.240 --> 00:10:00.440
wanted make sure that no one
ever doubt my credentials.

00:10:00.440 --> 00:10:01.880
And that has helped.

00:10:01.880 --> 00:10:03.794
FRANK: I've been
waiting 50 years

00:10:03.794 --> 00:10:05.460
for the privilege of
wearing this jacket

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:08.060
and walking down
in the procession.

00:10:08.060 --> 00:10:09.910
50 years I've been
waiting for this.

00:10:09.910 --> 00:10:10.610
What a kick!

00:10:17.084 --> 00:10:18.500
MCGANN: After
graduation week, you

00:10:18.500 --> 00:10:21.240
might think that things would
slow down a bit here at MIT,

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:23.150
but that's not
entirely the case.

00:10:23.150 --> 00:10:26.630
Almost before we had a chance
to catch our breath, 50 K

00:10:26.630 --> 00:10:28.710
through 12 educators
arrived on campus

00:10:28.710 --> 00:10:30.800
for the week-long
Science and Engineering

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:33.680
Program for Teachers, or SEPT.

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:36.140
We sat down with the founder
of SEPT, Professor Ron

00:10:36.140 --> 00:10:38.660
Latanision, who told us
more about the program's

00:10:38.660 --> 00:10:39.999
educational mission.

00:10:39.999 --> 00:10:42.290
LATANISION: The origin of
the program and the intention

00:10:42.290 --> 00:10:46.400
of the program has always
been to provide teachers

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:50.000
with the perspective
of a MIT faculty

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:52.610
on what science and
engineering means

00:10:52.610 --> 00:10:55.470
in terms of our whole
standard of living.

00:10:55.470 --> 00:10:58.790
So our goal today
is not only to deal

00:10:58.790 --> 00:11:01.970
with the question of technical
literacy in the broadest sense

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:05.990
but also to encourage young
people, through their teachers,

00:11:05.990 --> 00:11:09.200
to pursue their interest
or to develop interest

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:10.941
in science and
technology, and to become

00:11:10.941 --> 00:11:13.190
part of the infrastructure
of the future in the United

00:11:13.190 --> 00:11:14.090
States.

00:11:14.090 --> 00:11:15.320
PROFESSOR: Good morning.

00:11:15.320 --> 00:11:19.370
And welcome to MIT on the behalf
of MIT'S Physics Department.

00:11:19.370 --> 00:11:21.470
LATANISION: The program
runs for a full week.

00:11:21.470 --> 00:11:24.380
We describe it at the outset
as being typical of the MIT

00:11:24.380 --> 00:11:26.600
educational experience,
and that is, as a drink

00:11:26.600 --> 00:11:27.460
from a fire hose.

00:11:27.460 --> 00:11:31.040
MODIC: I definitely had a
biology fire hose yesterday.

00:11:31.040 --> 00:11:33.830
I had no clue what
was being said.

00:11:33.830 --> 00:11:34.820
And that's OK.

00:11:34.820 --> 00:11:36.630
I don't think that
you can ever come

00:11:36.630 --> 00:11:38.300
into a program
like this expecting

00:11:38.300 --> 00:11:40.750
to absorb 100% of what's said.

00:11:40.750 --> 00:11:43.400
And they don't expect
us to, I don't think.

00:11:43.400 --> 00:11:43.900
I hope not.

00:11:43.900 --> 00:11:44.400
[LAUGHS]

00:11:44.400 --> 00:11:47.109
BANTOE: Each day is-- it's
literally mentally exhausting.

00:11:47.109 --> 00:11:48.650
By the time you get
to the afternoon,

00:11:48.650 --> 00:11:51.380
it's like you've gotten so
much information, so much

00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:53.000
useful information, actually.

00:11:53.000 --> 00:11:57.330
PROFESSOR: You see here the
primary red on the outside,

00:11:57.330 --> 00:11:58.790
blue on the inside.

00:11:58.790 --> 00:12:00.820
And here, you see the secondary.

00:12:00.820 --> 00:12:03.620
WALKER: We talk about
rainbows, something

00:12:03.620 --> 00:12:05.750
as simple and
beautiful as a rainbow,

00:12:05.750 --> 00:12:08.870
and how a rainbow is
created and looking

00:12:08.870 --> 00:12:11.480
at the mathematical
aspects of a rainbow.

00:12:11.480 --> 00:12:13.991
So you'll never look at
a rainbow again the same.

00:12:13.991 --> 00:12:15.740
LATANISION: We have
thought over the years

00:12:15.740 --> 00:12:19.700
it would be very useful if we
could export what we have done

00:12:19.700 --> 00:12:22.340
on campus to other locations.

00:12:22.340 --> 00:12:23.720
And so this year,
we are actually

00:12:23.720 --> 00:12:26.840
broadcasting the
program in this summer

00:12:26.840 --> 00:12:28.550
live to over the internet.

00:12:28.550 --> 00:12:31.640
There are a group
of about 35 teachers

00:12:31.640 --> 00:12:34.940
who are in a classroom at the
University of Rhode Island,

00:12:34.940 --> 00:12:36.800
and they're participating
interactively

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:38.480
with the teachers who
are on our campus.

00:12:38.480 --> 00:12:41.600
RENEHAN: My hope is that I
can take this information back

00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:42.710
to my students.

00:12:42.710 --> 00:12:45.180
And when I'm in calculus
class or pre-calculus

00:12:45.180 --> 00:12:48.440
and they ask me, when am
I ever going to use this?

00:12:48.440 --> 00:12:50.960
Then, I can give them a real,
live application that, look,

00:12:50.960 --> 00:12:53.450
this is research that
is currently going on.

00:12:53.450 --> 00:12:57.070
And this is how it can be used
and applied in the real world.

00:12:57.070 --> 00:12:58.570
MCGANN: As regular
viewers of ZigZag

00:12:58.570 --> 00:13:00.880
know, for the past
several years,

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:03.850
the MIT campus has become
a preferred nesting site

00:13:03.850 --> 00:13:06.370
for mating red-tailed hawks.

00:13:06.370 --> 00:13:10.270
This year, for the second time,
AMPS, MIT'S media production

00:13:10.270 --> 00:13:12.310
group and the
producer of ZigZag,

00:13:12.310 --> 00:13:14.980
was able to videotape and
webcast the maturation

00:13:14.980 --> 00:13:17.080
process of two checks.

00:13:17.080 --> 00:13:18.940
On Easter weekend,
these eggs were

00:13:18.940 --> 00:13:21.970
cracked open, not by children
in search of chocolate,

00:13:21.970 --> 00:13:25.090
but by two baby hatchlings
in search of space,

00:13:25.090 --> 00:13:32.240
space to stretch and grow and
eat and grow and grow and grow.

00:13:32.240 --> 00:13:34.330
Nourished by a
plentiful supply of food

00:13:34.330 --> 00:13:36.310
from their expert
hunter parents,

00:13:36.310 --> 00:13:39.310
the chicks seemed to
double in size each week.

00:13:39.310 --> 00:13:41.800
Soon, a global
audience was enjoying

00:13:41.800 --> 00:13:43.900
their day-to-day activities.

00:13:43.900 --> 00:13:46.370
A virtual community
gathered around the nest

00:13:46.370 --> 00:13:49.060
as hawk watchers traded
observations and insights

00:13:49.060 --> 00:13:52.210
on the Hawk Cam blog.

00:13:52.210 --> 00:13:56.320
Together, we all shared in the
excitement of sibling rivalry,

00:13:56.320 --> 00:14:00.820
parents being parents, curious
first wanderings from the nest,

00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:03.325
and, as graduation
week approached,

00:14:03.325 --> 00:14:04.200
their maiden flights.

00:14:08.080 --> 00:14:10.090
We wish our fledglings
the best as they

00:14:10.090 --> 00:14:12.730
explore the world beyond MIT.

00:14:12.730 --> 00:14:15.530
We were fortunate to
watch them grow and learn.

00:14:15.530 --> 00:14:19.890
And we also learned from them.

00:14:19.890 --> 00:14:21.630
That's all the time
we have for now.

00:14:21.630 --> 00:14:24.300
From the Stata Center
at MIT, I'm Matt McGann.

00:14:24.300 --> 00:14:25.992
Thank you for watching ZigZag.

00:14:25.992 --> 00:14:29.928
[MUSIC PLAYING]

