00:17:47.950 --> 00:17:50.870
MCGANN: Well, commencement
marks the official end

00:17:50.870 --> 00:17:52.320
of the academic year.

00:17:52.320 --> 00:17:55.370
And although the pace changes
a bit during the summer,

00:17:55.370 --> 00:17:58.940
the education continues
in many different forms

00:17:58.940 --> 00:18:01.160
and for a varied
group of students,

00:18:01.160 --> 00:18:03.309
as you'll see in the
next episode of ZigZag.

00:18:03.309 --> 00:18:04.100
Let's check it out.

00:18:09.281 --> 00:18:12.770
BOLTON: Coming to you from the
Wood Sailing Pavilion at MIT,

00:18:12.770 --> 00:18:15.143
my name is Marsha Bolton,
And this is ZigZag.

00:18:15.143 --> 00:18:18.515
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:18:21.860 --> 00:18:24.860
Summer break is winding
down, and the class of 2010

00:18:24.860 --> 00:18:26.900
will soon be arriving on campus.

00:18:26.900 --> 00:18:29.360
But there's still plenty of
time to enjoy the New England

00:18:29.360 --> 00:18:32.690
weather with a trip to the
beach or a sail on the Charles.

00:18:32.690 --> 00:18:35.630
The summer is also a time
when MIT hosts students

00:18:35.630 --> 00:18:38.600
from the local community
and from around the world

00:18:38.600 --> 00:18:41.750
in pursuit of challenging
educational opportunities.

00:18:41.750 --> 00:18:44.090
Building exotic
alternatively-fueled vehicles,

00:18:44.090 --> 00:18:46.520
playing Quidditch
with underwater ROVs,

00:18:46.520 --> 00:18:48.110
and doing
graduate-level research

00:18:48.110 --> 00:18:51.080
as an undergrad are just some
of the answers you'll find here

00:18:51.080 --> 00:18:55.510
to the age old question, what do
you do on your summer vacation?

00:18:55.510 --> 00:18:58.460
For one international
group, the summer of 2006

00:18:58.460 --> 00:19:01.400
provided an opportunity
to make a real difference

00:19:01.400 --> 00:19:04.130
as they gathered at MIT
for the Vehicle Design

00:19:04.130 --> 00:19:07.520
Summit, an event conceived
and organized by students.

00:19:07.520 --> 00:19:09.710
BITELLO: The Vehicle
Design Summit

00:19:09.710 --> 00:19:12.830
consists of students
from 13 countries,

00:19:12.830 --> 00:19:14.000
about 20 universities.

00:19:14.000 --> 00:19:17.540
ALLEN: We had four vehicle teams
that each designed and built

00:19:17.540 --> 00:19:21.140
from scratch a complete vehicle
that carried either one or two

00:19:21.140 --> 00:19:24.110
passengers and would really make
a positive impact on the issue

00:19:24.110 --> 00:19:26.135
of commuter travel.

00:19:26.135 --> 00:19:28.527
JAFFE: We're doing a ground-up
re-analysis of everything

00:19:28.527 --> 00:19:29.360
you find in the car.

00:19:29.360 --> 00:19:33.946
VAN HOEF: We're trying to build
a two-seater commuter car.

00:19:33.946 --> 00:19:36.480
BOGGS: I really enjoy
designing things.

00:19:36.480 --> 00:19:40.356
And this event is really
full of designing.

00:19:43.194 --> 00:19:45.660
RITTER: This isn't--
it's not for fun.

00:19:45.660 --> 00:19:48.350
It's not for a competition
that ends and we're done.

00:19:48.350 --> 00:19:50.611
We are trying to do something
very real, very serious,

00:19:50.611 --> 00:19:52.360
and have an impact in
the world right now.

00:20:02.566 --> 00:20:06.180
ALLEN: One of cars we made, the
AHPV, Assisted Human Powered

00:20:06.180 --> 00:20:09.134
Vehicle, so maybe if they're
part of the power source,

00:20:09.134 --> 00:20:11.550
if they need to pedal in order
to move the car in addition

00:20:11.550 --> 00:20:13.970
to having a battery pack
and an electric motor,

00:20:13.970 --> 00:20:15.390
that was kind of the key tenant.

00:20:15.390 --> 00:20:17.670
We also made a biofuels car.

00:20:17.670 --> 00:20:19.770
And that team was
very interested in,

00:20:19.770 --> 00:20:21.854
how do you run a car
on vegetable oil--

00:20:21.854 --> 00:20:23.370
SVO or straight vegetable oil?

00:20:23.370 --> 00:20:25.700
It has four wheels
and two passengers.

00:20:25.700 --> 00:20:28.070
And they made a
steel space frame.

00:20:28.070 --> 00:20:31.510
We also made a fuel
cell electric hybrid.

00:20:31.510 --> 00:20:33.360
It's predominantly an electric--

00:20:33.360 --> 00:20:35.640
three-wheeled electric
with two passengers.

00:20:35.640 --> 00:20:37.890
And our other car
is called Pulse,

00:20:37.890 --> 00:20:40.140
meaning it's supposed to
be the pulse of the city.

00:20:40.140 --> 00:20:42.860
Kind of a commuter concept
that just has one person

00:20:42.860 --> 00:20:44.635
and was just to
show, here's what--

00:20:44.635 --> 00:20:46.260
if you're commuting
by yourself, here's

00:20:46.260 --> 00:20:48.510
where your car might look
like if it was all electric.

00:20:48.510 --> 00:20:51.366
[CHATTER]

00:20:55.840 --> 00:20:57.470
The biggest
priorities of our work

00:20:57.470 --> 00:20:59.280
are to get students
together and show

00:20:59.280 --> 00:21:01.465
that really passionate
people can do a lot

00:21:01.465 --> 00:21:03.090
and that an issue,
like global warming,

00:21:03.090 --> 00:21:04.322
is really not ephemeral.

00:21:04.322 --> 00:21:06.030
Even though it's in
many different realms

00:21:06.030 --> 00:21:07.470
and many different
markets, there

00:21:07.470 --> 00:21:11.460
are ways of chipping away
at it little by little.

00:21:11.460 --> 00:21:14.102
BOLTON: If you've spent any
time around MIT this summer,

00:21:14.102 --> 00:21:16.560
you've probably noticed crowds
of students a little younger

00:21:16.560 --> 00:21:18.350
than your average undergrad.

00:21:18.350 --> 00:21:20.640
They're participants in
the School of Engineering's

00:21:20.640 --> 00:21:22.115
engineering outreach programs.

00:21:22.115 --> 00:21:24.240
CARTER: The Office of
Engineering Outreach Programs

00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:26.625
currently has three major
programs in the office.

00:21:26.625 --> 00:21:28.494
The first and the
flagship program,

00:21:28.494 --> 00:21:29.910
which has been
around the longest,

00:21:29.910 --> 00:21:33.055
is the MIT Minority Introduction
To Engineering and Sciences,

00:21:33.055 --> 00:21:33.930
or the MITES program.

00:21:36.600 --> 00:21:39.090
Typically, we bring about
60 to 80 students to campus

00:21:39.090 --> 00:21:40.470
from across the
country, and they

00:21:40.470 --> 00:21:43.110
study what is a mini
freshmen experience

00:21:43.110 --> 00:21:44.250
for about six weeks--

00:21:44.250 --> 00:21:46.960
courses in life sciences,
engineering design,

00:21:46.960 --> 00:21:49.650
internet programming,
genomics, calculus, physics,

00:21:49.650 --> 00:21:53.120
and humanities.

00:21:53.120 --> 00:21:57.090
The second program is SEED
Academy, Saturday Engineering

00:21:57.090 --> 00:21:58.200
Enrichment and Discovery.

00:21:58.200 --> 00:22:00.033
We keep the kids for
three and a half years.

00:22:00.033 --> 00:22:02.610
And they basically spend
10 Saturdays a semester

00:22:02.610 --> 00:22:05.380
with us doing hands-on
kinesthetic learning

00:22:05.380 --> 00:22:07.395
in engineering disciplines.

00:22:10.010 --> 00:22:12.180
And the third program,
which is the newest,

00:22:12.180 --> 00:22:14.490
is the Science
Technology Engineering

00:22:14.490 --> 00:22:15.510
and Mathematics program.

00:22:15.510 --> 00:22:16.020
That's STEM.

00:22:16.020 --> 00:22:17.825
That program is for
middle school students.

00:22:17.825 --> 00:22:19.770
We enrich their middle
school experience

00:22:19.770 --> 00:22:21.600
by teaching them
advanced topics, things

00:22:21.600 --> 00:22:24.160
like algebraic topology,
descriptive geometry, physics,

00:22:24.160 --> 00:22:26.310
probability and
statistics, and programming

00:22:26.310 --> 00:22:28.890
in a five-week intensive
program during the summer.

00:22:28.890 --> 00:22:31.890
PROFESSOR: Three, two, one.

00:22:31.890 --> 00:22:33.890
[APPLAUSE]

00:22:33.890 --> 00:22:36.730
ANGELA: The best part about
STEM was meeting new people

00:22:36.730 --> 00:22:39.315
and learning about more
stuff, because it actually

00:22:39.315 --> 00:22:40.770
stretched our brains.

00:22:44.192 --> 00:22:46.150
CARTER: These programs
are important because A,

00:22:46.150 --> 00:22:47.620
you get students
to come together

00:22:47.620 --> 00:22:49.930
and see that there are other
students like them that

00:22:49.930 --> 00:22:52.240
love to ask the same
probing questions

00:22:52.240 --> 00:22:56.320
and have a fire, a passion
in the belly for science

00:22:56.320 --> 00:22:57.010
and technology.

00:22:57.010 --> 00:22:59.650
And two, it helps
us to teach students

00:22:59.650 --> 00:23:01.130
to ask the why questions.

00:23:01.130 --> 00:23:03.292
And that's what we need
more of in this country.

00:23:03.292 --> 00:23:05.250
BOLTON: For some of this
year's MITES students,

00:23:05.250 --> 00:23:08.030
the summer ended with a
splash, as they participated

00:23:08.030 --> 00:23:11.560
in the first ever MITES
underwater Remotely Operated

00:23:11.560 --> 00:23:13.640
Vehicle, or ROV competition.

00:23:13.640 --> 00:23:14.390
STUDENT: This way.

00:23:14.390 --> 00:23:15.050
This way.

00:23:15.050 --> 00:23:16.615
Swim that way.

00:23:16.615 --> 00:23:17.115
[CHEERING]

00:23:17.115 --> 00:23:20.600
AMAYA: Today, we had
the kids show off

00:23:20.600 --> 00:23:21.860
their five weeks of work.

00:23:21.860 --> 00:23:25.430
And what they had to do was
build an underwater machine

00:23:25.430 --> 00:23:27.140
called an ROV.

00:23:27.140 --> 00:23:30.020
And we had them do three
types of competitions today.

00:23:30.020 --> 00:23:32.930
They did a quick drag race,
a weightlifting competition,

00:23:32.930 --> 00:23:36.109
and finally, we had a
Quidditch match underwater.

00:23:36.109 --> 00:23:37.400
NAPIER: We won the competition.

00:23:37.400 --> 00:23:40.400
We came in first place
overall based on the fact

00:23:40.400 --> 00:23:44.900
that we performed highest in
all the three competitions.

00:23:44.900 --> 00:23:48.812
But this is our beautiful
machine, Cardinal.

00:23:56.350 --> 00:23:58.100
MORIARTY: In this
class, I'm trying

00:23:58.100 --> 00:24:01.910
to have them ask the
questions, to have

00:24:01.910 --> 00:24:04.490
them be the ones
observing something

00:24:04.490 --> 00:24:06.040
and ask the questions about it.

00:24:06.040 --> 00:24:08.990
So I'm really trying to get them
to be actively the ones driving

00:24:08.990 --> 00:24:12.650
what we're doing, where they
can really just get involved,

00:24:12.650 --> 00:24:14.090
get immersed in it.

00:24:24.096 --> 00:24:25.720
CARTER: There's a
tradition here at MIT

00:24:25.720 --> 00:24:28.840
that before any major challenge,
that students get dunked.

00:24:28.840 --> 00:24:31.710
They get thrown into the moat
or the pool or the shower.

00:24:31.710 --> 00:24:34.660
And I heard a rumor that
the winning team stepped up

00:24:34.660 --> 00:24:37.345
to that challenge and threw the
teaching assistant in the pool.

00:24:37.345 --> 00:24:38.470
I don't know if it's true--

00:24:38.470 --> 00:24:39.250
true or not.

00:24:39.250 --> 00:24:42.482
But if it is, very much in
line with the MIT tradition.

00:24:42.482 --> 00:24:42.982
STUDENT: Go.

00:24:42.982 --> 00:24:43.482
[SPLASH]

00:24:43.482 --> 00:24:46.349
[LAUGHTER]

00:24:49.240 --> 00:24:50.670
BOLTON: A couple
of episodes back,

00:24:50.670 --> 00:24:53.640
we met the students from
MIT'S Summer Research Program,

00:24:53.640 --> 00:24:58.570
or MSRP, as they were beginning
their nine-week stay on campus.

00:24:58.570 --> 00:25:00.120
It's been an intense
couple of months

00:25:00.120 --> 00:25:03.420
for these 57 undergraduates
from around the country.

00:25:03.420 --> 00:25:05.700
They've taken on projects
and diverse topics,

00:25:05.700 --> 00:25:09.300
such as robotics, nuclear
engineering, material science,

00:25:09.300 --> 00:25:10.380
and design.

00:25:10.380 --> 00:25:12.270
And their graduate-level
work has resulted

00:25:12.270 --> 00:25:14.640
in seven likely publications.

00:25:14.640 --> 00:25:16.590
But they've also
managed to enjoy trips

00:25:16.590 --> 00:25:18.810
to Woods Hole in
Martha's Vineyard.

00:25:18.810 --> 00:25:21.840
And at the final celebration,
they received a surprise visit

00:25:21.840 --> 00:25:25.260
from Randal Pinkett, winner
of last season's Apprentice

00:25:25.260 --> 00:25:27.045
and an MIT PhD.

00:25:27.045 --> 00:25:30.397
PINKETT: Having been through MIT
and knowing the MSRP program,

00:25:30.397 --> 00:25:31.980
first of all, let
me congratulate you.

00:25:31.980 --> 00:25:33.720
It's a great time
to be involved.

00:25:33.720 --> 00:25:34.920
It's a great time
for you finishing up.

00:25:34.920 --> 00:25:36.128
So hopefully, you enjoy this.

00:25:36.128 --> 00:25:37.050
Enjoy the day.

00:25:37.050 --> 00:25:38.460
Celebrate your accomplishments.

00:25:38.460 --> 00:25:40.765
And recharge your batteries
for the fall semester.

00:25:40.765 --> 00:25:43.140
WILLIAMS: This program has
definitely changed my thoughts

00:25:43.140 --> 00:25:44.200
about the future.

00:25:44.200 --> 00:25:46.260
When I came in here,
I had a set path,

00:25:46.260 --> 00:25:50.220
but now, things just kind of
got changed, but for the good,

00:25:50.220 --> 00:25:51.640
for the better, definitely.

00:25:51.640 --> 00:25:53.262
So I have more direction now.

00:25:53.262 --> 00:25:54.720
BOLTON: It seems
certain that we'll

00:25:54.720 --> 00:25:56.580
be hearing from these
outstanding students

00:25:56.580 --> 00:26:00.450
again as they continue to pursue
careers and break new ground

00:26:00.450 --> 00:26:02.880
in science and technology.

00:26:02.880 --> 00:26:04.200
That's all for now.

00:26:04.200 --> 00:26:06.072
For ZigZag, I'm Marsha Bolton.

00:26:06.072 --> 00:26:07.030
I'll see you next time.

00:26:07.030 --> 00:26:10.411
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:26:16.220 --> 00:26:18.920
VIELMA: As some of
these stories indicate,

00:26:18.920 --> 00:26:24.150
MIT actively recruits students
from around the world.

00:26:24.150 --> 00:26:27.590
And the ways in which
students discover MIT

00:26:27.590 --> 00:26:31.750
is as varied as the
students themselves.

00:26:31.750 --> 00:26:35.980
We recently asked a group
of graduating seniors

00:26:35.980 --> 00:26:39.820
how and why they discovered MIT.

00:26:39.820 --> 00:26:40.900
So let's take a look.

00:26:44.337 --> 00:26:47.710
PEARCE: When I was really
small, I played with LEGOs

00:26:47.710 --> 00:26:48.640
in my front window.

00:26:48.640 --> 00:26:51.790
NEDZEL: I kept a listing of
all the different product

00:26:51.790 --> 00:26:54.490
numbers in the LEGO
catalog that I wanted.

00:26:54.490 --> 00:26:56.935
LUGO: Sometimes, I would create
math problems for myself.

00:26:56.935 --> 00:26:59.830
GUERRA: I saw the
moving images on the TV,

00:26:59.830 --> 00:27:02.990
but I couldn't explain
why that was happening.

00:27:02.990 --> 00:27:04.525
So I started taking
the TV apart.

00:27:04.525 --> 00:27:06.490
PEARCE: That was
my first experience

00:27:06.490 --> 00:27:09.110
with working with my hands
and trying to build things.

00:27:09.110 --> 00:27:12.290
And when I learned that that
was actually engineering,

00:27:12.290 --> 00:27:13.810
MIT made all the
sense in the world.

00:27:13.810 --> 00:27:17.380
NEDZEL: From my dad,
he definitely wanted

00:27:17.380 --> 00:27:18.160
me to come here.

00:27:18.160 --> 00:27:19.717
I said, I don't
want to go to MIT.

00:27:19.717 --> 00:27:21.550
I don't want anything
to do with this place.

00:27:21.550 --> 00:27:23.410
He sort of took a
step back and tried

00:27:23.410 --> 00:27:27.460
to allow me to find my own
way, why this school was

00:27:27.460 --> 00:27:28.870
such a wonderful place for me.

00:27:28.870 --> 00:27:33.420
He just really drew out the
person I've always been,

00:27:33.420 --> 00:27:36.955
and that person was someone
that was made to go to MIT.

00:27:36.955 --> 00:27:39.790
PEARCE: My perception
of MIT was that it

00:27:39.790 --> 00:27:43.260
was a cold, boring place.

00:27:43.260 --> 00:27:44.570
HOPEMAN: I had heard about MIT.

00:27:44.570 --> 00:27:48.010
It was a distance school,
great in engineering.

00:27:48.010 --> 00:27:50.200
But I had never had any
real desire to come here.

00:27:50.200 --> 00:27:53.230
NEDZEL: But when I got here,
I just found so much more.

00:27:53.230 --> 00:27:56.101
LUGO: I met people from all
over the country and all

00:27:56.101 --> 00:27:56.725
over the world.

00:27:56.725 --> 00:27:59.620
SCHOR: I have friends on
the varsity pistol team that

00:27:59.620 --> 00:28:00.880
went to Nationals this year.

00:28:00.880 --> 00:28:03.790
I have friends who do ballroom
dance competitions or salsa

00:28:03.790 --> 00:28:04.360
lessons.

00:28:04.360 --> 00:28:06.640
Everyone has their own
thing and their own style.

00:28:06.640 --> 00:28:09.850
And it really adds to the
personality and depth at MIT.

00:28:09.850 --> 00:28:14.410
SLUTSKY: MIT is so intense,
so challenging, and yet,

00:28:14.410 --> 00:28:17.560
so rewarding that I think
that the experience you

00:28:17.560 --> 00:28:20.485
gain in four years is unlike
any throughout the country.

00:28:20.485 --> 00:28:23.110
PEARCE: It became
abundantly clear to me

00:28:23.110 --> 00:28:26.170
that MIT was really
a unique place,

00:28:26.170 --> 00:28:28.887
and I saw the greatness of the
people who were around here.

00:28:28.887 --> 00:28:30.720
And I figured I wanted
to be a part of that.

00:28:34.213 --> 00:28:37.207
[AUDIO OUT]

00:28:57.190 --> 00:28:58.990
MCGANN: We're back now.

00:28:58.990 --> 00:29:02.470
Today, we have graduating
families celebrating

00:29:02.470 --> 00:29:06.950
bachelor's degrees, master's
degrees, and doctoral degrees.

00:29:06.950 --> 00:29:09.910
And here with me today, we've
pulled out of the crowd,

00:29:09.910 --> 00:29:14.680
somewhat at random, the family
of a master's degree student.

00:29:14.680 --> 00:29:18.370
So first of all,
let's introduce the--

00:29:18.370 --> 00:29:21.400
can you introduce to us who
we're celebrating today?

00:29:21.400 --> 00:29:24.850
TAYLOR: We're celebrating
my husband, Daniel Taylor.

00:29:24.850 --> 00:29:26.500
And I'm his wife, Lauren.

00:29:26.500 --> 00:29:28.485
TAYLOR: I'm his mother, Lynn.

00:29:28.485 --> 00:29:30.685
TAYLOR: His father, Glen Taylor.

00:29:30.685 --> 00:29:35.260
STOVALL: And I'm the
mother-in-law, Joanne Stovall.

00:29:35.260 --> 00:29:37.365
MCGANN: And where are
you all up from today?

00:29:37.365 --> 00:29:38.920
TAYLOR: Florida.

00:29:38.920 --> 00:29:39.760
MCGANN: Florida.

00:29:39.760 --> 00:29:42.760
And can you tell me a little
bit about your husband

00:29:42.760 --> 00:29:44.410
and his experiences at MIT?

00:29:44.410 --> 00:29:45.420
How has his experience?

00:29:45.420 --> 00:29:47.770
TAYLOR: Oh, his experience
has been wonderful.

00:29:47.770 --> 00:29:49.900
We initially came
here because he

00:29:49.900 --> 00:29:51.970
wanted to go to a school
that would give him

00:29:51.970 --> 00:29:53.442
so many opportunities.

00:29:53.442 --> 00:29:54.900
And that's what it
really gave him.

00:29:54.900 --> 00:29:58.270
So everything he did, he
loved, no matter how hard.

00:29:58.270 --> 00:29:59.890
So it was great.

00:29:59.890 --> 00:30:02.110
MCGANN: How was it
coming from Florida

00:30:02.110 --> 00:30:05.200
to deal with these
New England winters?

00:30:05.200 --> 00:30:06.820
What was that like for you guys?

00:30:06.820 --> 00:30:08.080
TAYLOR: I hate snow.

00:30:08.080 --> 00:30:09.680
[LAUGHTER]

00:30:09.680 --> 00:30:11.680
PRESENTER: So now, when
we were talking earlier,

00:30:11.680 --> 00:30:13.600
you were telling me--
how did your husband get

00:30:13.600 --> 00:30:14.985
to school every day?

00:30:14.985 --> 00:30:16.300
TAYLOR: He rode his bike.

00:30:16.300 --> 00:30:18.520
And then, when the
snow was too much,

00:30:18.520 --> 00:30:21.670
he would just walk with boots.

00:30:21.670 --> 00:30:24.070
MCGANN: So you had to outfit
yourselves for the winter,

00:30:24.070 --> 00:30:25.960
go to the store, buy
a whole bunch of stuff

00:30:25.960 --> 00:30:27.520
that you'd never
need in Florida?

00:30:27.520 --> 00:30:28.600
TAYLOR: Yes.

00:30:28.600 --> 00:30:31.820
The first winter we had
our spatulas and everything

00:30:31.820 --> 00:30:33.160
scraping the windshields.

00:30:33.160 --> 00:30:35.420
And then, we went and got
all the proper things,

00:30:35.420 --> 00:30:37.220
so it was good.

00:30:37.220 --> 00:30:39.670
MCGANN: So now that
he has graduated,

00:30:39.670 --> 00:30:41.074
what will he do professionally?

00:30:41.074 --> 00:30:41.740
What's he doing?

00:30:41.740 --> 00:30:44.080
TAYLOR: Well, we already
moved back to Florida.

00:30:44.080 --> 00:30:48.460
And he's working in a firm in
Orlando doing public works,

00:30:48.460 --> 00:30:50.330
so we like it a lot.

00:30:50.330 --> 00:30:54.540
MCGANN: And how has he used
his MIT knowledge in the firm

00:30:54.540 --> 00:30:56.010
and out in the real world?

00:30:56.010 --> 00:30:59.290
TAYLOR: Well, he's kind of
taken over all the computer

00:30:59.290 --> 00:31:02.110
responsibilities at his new job.

00:31:02.110 --> 00:31:04.750
And he's teaching everyone how
to do all these new programs

00:31:04.750 --> 00:31:06.674
that are out, so.

00:31:06.674 --> 00:31:08.090
MCGANN: So when
he was growing up,

00:31:08.090 --> 00:31:11.180
did you ever imagine that
he would go off to MIT?

00:31:11.180 --> 00:31:13.010
Was he the MIT type?

00:31:13.010 --> 00:31:14.480
TAYLOR: Oh, yes, always.

00:31:14.480 --> 00:31:16.770
From the time he
was two years old,

00:31:16.770 --> 00:31:19.970
I knew that he would
be doing great things.

00:31:19.970 --> 00:31:22.040
MCGANN: And you were
saying that he was also,

00:31:22.040 --> 00:31:24.040
like in the video, one
of those students who

00:31:24.040 --> 00:31:25.800
was taking apart things and--

00:31:25.800 --> 00:31:27.110
TAYLOR: Always.

00:31:27.110 --> 00:31:28.940
From the time he
was two years old,

00:31:28.940 --> 00:31:30.800
his favorite toy was LEGOs.

00:31:30.800 --> 00:31:32.720
He never wanted to go outside.

00:31:32.720 --> 00:31:34.460
He just wanted to do things.

00:31:34.460 --> 00:31:37.700
And twice when he was
in elementary school,

00:31:37.700 --> 00:31:41.480
he won the state science
fair with different designs

00:31:41.480 --> 00:31:43.910
that he had done with LEGOs.

00:31:43.910 --> 00:31:46.400
MCGANN: Now, do you feel
that your daughter has

00:31:46.400 --> 00:31:48.380
chosen a good man?

00:31:48.380 --> 00:31:50.780
Are you very proud of
your son-in-law today?

00:31:50.780 --> 00:31:54.770
STOVALL: I am extremely
proud of my son-in-law.

00:31:54.770 --> 00:31:58.790
And also, not only did
he graduate from MIT,

00:31:58.790 --> 00:32:02.630
but in this last year,
my daughter and he

00:32:02.630 --> 00:32:04.350
had their first child.

00:32:04.350 --> 00:32:07.220
So he did all of his
studies while they

00:32:07.220 --> 00:32:09.320
were planning for
the baby, so he

00:32:09.320 --> 00:32:12.200
was pretty dedicated and busy.

00:32:12.200 --> 00:32:14.420
MCGANN: So maybe
your grandson will

00:32:14.420 --> 00:32:17.380
be class of MIT, what, 2025?

00:32:17.380 --> 00:32:19.139
STOVALL: Well, my
granddaughter--

00:32:19.139 --> 00:32:19.680
MCGANN: Oops.

00:32:19.680 --> 00:32:25.010
STOVALL: --will be
a graduate of 2025.

00:32:25.010 --> 00:32:29.280
MCGANN: So you'll all be back
here in about 18, no 22 years,

00:32:29.280 --> 00:32:30.170
something like that.

00:32:30.170 --> 00:32:31.919
STOVALL: We plan to be, yes.

00:32:31.919 --> 00:32:32.960
MCGANN: All right, great.

00:32:32.960 --> 00:32:34.793
We're going to throw
it back over to Karina.

00:32:34.793 --> 00:32:35.700
What's next?

00:32:35.700 --> 00:32:40.086
[INAUDIBLE]

00:32:40.086 --> 00:32:41.710
VIELMA: Well, we're
going to check back

00:32:41.710 --> 00:32:45.250
in Johnson Athletic Center
to see what Allie's up to.

00:32:45.250 --> 00:32:46.810
Allie, how are you
doing over there?

00:32:46.810 --> 00:32:47.860
How are the graduates?

00:32:47.860 --> 00:32:49.276
[? POSHAY: ?]
Pretty good, Karina.

00:32:49.276 --> 00:32:52.810
It's filled up quite
a bit, actually.

00:32:52.810 --> 00:32:56.200
There's a lot more hoods,
a lot more graduates

00:32:56.200 --> 00:32:58.360
just standing around
eating breakfast.

00:32:58.360 --> 00:33:01.600
So I have a couple more
to interview for you.

00:33:01.600 --> 00:33:02.890
We have--

00:33:02.890 --> 00:33:04.215
[? CHOW: ?] Elaine [? Chow. ?]

00:33:04.215 --> 00:33:04.840
POSHAY: --and--

00:33:04.840 --> 00:33:05.882
MARTINEZ: Josue Martinez.

00:33:05.882 --> 00:33:07.673
[? POSHAY: ?] Okay,
and where are you from?

00:33:07.673 --> 00:33:08.926
CHOW: I'm from Oklahoma City.

00:33:08.926 --> 00:33:10.600
MARTINEZ: San Juan, Puerto Rico.

00:33:10.600 --> 00:33:14.271
[? POSHAY: ?] Okay, so
you are a grad student?

00:33:14.271 --> 00:33:14.770
CHOW: I am.

00:33:14.770 --> 00:33:15.992
I'm in the MBA program.

00:33:15.992 --> 00:33:17.950
[? POSHAY: ?] Oh, and
did you travel with that?

00:33:17.950 --> 00:33:19.390
CHOW: I traveled a lot.

00:33:19.390 --> 00:33:20.140
It was great.

00:33:20.140 --> 00:33:21.432
[? POSHAY: ?] Where did you go?

00:33:21.432 --> 00:33:22.390
CHOW: I went to Mexico.

00:33:22.390 --> 00:33:23.290
I went to Costa Rica.

00:33:23.290 --> 00:33:27.430
I went to Vietnam, Israel,
bunch of different places--

00:33:27.430 --> 00:33:29.410
Montreal, if that counts.

00:33:29.410 --> 00:33:30.640
POSHAY: Wow, okay.

00:33:30.640 --> 00:33:31.140
One more.

00:33:31.140 --> 00:33:34.810
So what did you do at MIT?

00:33:34.810 --> 00:33:40.030
MARTINEZ: From study, built a
lot of stuff with my friends,

00:33:40.030 --> 00:33:42.189
just hung out,
played video games.

00:33:42.189 --> 00:33:43.730
[? POSHAY: ?] So
you had a good time?

00:33:43.730 --> 00:33:45.255
That's what you--
you build stuff?

00:33:45.255 --> 00:33:45.880
MARTINEZ: Yeah.

00:33:45.880 --> 00:33:48.910
Oh, build stuff--
bars, cars, just

00:33:48.910 --> 00:33:50.310
a little bit of everything.

00:33:50.310 --> 00:33:51.562
[? POSHAY: ?] Okay, all right.

00:33:51.562 --> 00:33:53.020
So you build anything
in particular

00:33:53.020 --> 00:33:54.535
that was your
favorite thing, you

00:33:54.535 --> 00:33:55.360
were just really proud of it?

00:33:55.360 --> 00:33:57.340
MARTINEZ: Oh, my favorite
thing of all time--

00:33:57.340 --> 00:34:02.020
last year, I built a
full-size loft and bar.

00:34:02.020 --> 00:34:05.440
Took me like a week to
build, polished, urethaned,

00:34:05.440 --> 00:34:05.940
everything.

00:34:05.940 --> 00:34:07.300
It's amazing.

00:34:07.300 --> 00:34:07.960
It's a gem.

00:34:07.960 --> 00:34:09.479
[? POSHAY: ?] It got put to use?

00:34:09.479 --> 00:34:11.020
MARTINEZ: It got
put to use for sure.

00:34:11.020 --> 00:34:12.620
But don't tell the
administration.

00:34:12.620 --> 00:34:15.699
[? POSHAY: ?]
[LAUGHS] All right.

00:34:15.699 --> 00:34:17.479
Back to you, Karina,
in the court.

00:34:17.479 --> 00:34:18.520
VIELMA: Thank you, Allie.

00:34:22.000 --> 00:34:25.100
As these students
have demonstrated,

00:34:25.100 --> 00:34:29.850
MIT students achieve
in a variety of ways--

00:34:29.850 --> 00:34:33.949
clearly in the classroom and
in laboratories, but also,

00:34:33.949 --> 00:34:38.090
they achieve in the playing
fields and the concert--

00:34:38.090 --> 00:34:40.010
in the concert hall.

00:34:40.010 --> 00:34:42.710
The arts at MIT
play a vital role

00:34:42.710 --> 00:34:46.000
in the overall
educational experience,

00:34:46.000 --> 00:34:49.264
as this next video illustrates.

00:34:49.264 --> 00:34:52.880
BRODY: One morning, I came
up the steps of Massachusetts

00:34:52.880 --> 00:34:57.260
Avenue and walked
through the door,

00:34:57.260 --> 00:35:03.560
and the entire space had been
transformed into a wheat field.

00:35:03.560 --> 00:35:05.960
Innovative visual arts projects
like that are happening

00:35:05.960 --> 00:35:09.030
all the time on campus.

00:35:09.030 --> 00:35:15.090
The arts at MIT, like science
and engineering at MIT,

00:35:15.090 --> 00:35:20.370
are on the cutting edge
of their disciplines.

00:35:20.370 --> 00:35:23.820
They are serving the students.

00:35:23.820 --> 00:35:26.790
STUDENT: And I've got a
rap sheet that's got a--

00:35:26.790 --> 00:35:29.040
BRODY: And in the way
they're serving the students,

00:35:29.040 --> 00:35:31.637
ultimately, they're going to
be serving the entire society.

00:35:31.637 --> 00:35:34.319
[VIOLIN MUSIC]

00:35:34.319 --> 00:35:37.940
TSENG: At MIT, you'll find
some of the best musicians,

00:35:37.940 --> 00:35:38.920
virtuosos, completely.

00:35:38.920 --> 00:35:41.850
The person who lives next
door to me has perfect pitch.

00:35:41.850 --> 00:35:43.740
And if you name any
song whatsoever,

00:35:43.740 --> 00:35:45.870
he can strum it
out on his guitar.

00:35:45.870 --> 00:35:47.530
If it has multiple
lines of music,

00:35:47.530 --> 00:35:49.770
he can strum out all
the lines on his guitar.

00:35:49.770 --> 00:35:50.850
And you can see that.

00:35:50.850 --> 00:35:54.390
It's indicative of the level
of quality at the MIT symphony

00:35:54.390 --> 00:35:57.690
orchestra, the MIT
concert choir, et cetera.

00:35:57.690 --> 00:36:00.360
TSIEN: Last year, I got to
premiere a song cycle written

00:36:00.360 --> 00:36:03.720
by an MIT composer, and
that was a lot of fun--

00:36:03.720 --> 00:36:06.060
modern poetry, modern
music, and everything.

00:36:06.060 --> 00:36:08.022
STUDENT: Everyone at MIT
cares about something,

00:36:08.022 --> 00:36:09.980
whether it's their classes
or their [INAUDIBLE]

00:36:09.980 --> 00:36:11.355
or some student
activity they do.

00:36:11.355 --> 00:36:13.563
There's always something
that they really care about.

00:36:13.563 --> 00:36:15.240
SONENBERG: The thing
about MIT students

00:36:15.240 --> 00:36:19.020
that I most appreciate
is the elegance

00:36:19.020 --> 00:36:23.310
of their thinking and the fact
that it's sort of live and let

00:36:23.310 --> 00:36:24.105
live here.

00:36:24.105 --> 00:36:29.925
CHOIR: (SINGING) [INAUDIBLE]
Ooh wah wah wah wah.

00:36:29.925 --> 00:36:33.320
[INDIAN MUSIC PLAYING]

00:36:33.320 --> 00:36:35.950
SONENBERG: There's a
point of concentration

00:36:35.950 --> 00:36:39.550
that any discipline requires,
whether it's engineering,

00:36:39.550 --> 00:36:43.030
or it's directed or
acting or painting.

00:36:43.030 --> 00:36:46.510
That point concentration
is the focal point.

00:36:46.510 --> 00:36:49.990
It is the thing around which
you organize your thinking

00:36:49.990 --> 00:36:52.030
and your technique.

00:36:52.030 --> 00:36:55.000
And so the fact that there
is ensemble work where people

00:36:55.000 --> 00:36:57.910
learn to work
collaboratively is something

00:36:57.910 --> 00:37:01.250
that I see that people take
into their laps in a way

00:37:01.250 --> 00:37:03.625
that you would hope that labs
would work collaboratively.

00:37:03.625 --> 00:37:05.750
[MUSIC PLAYING - SANTANA FT. ROB
 THOMAS, "SMOOTH"]

00:37:05.750 --> 00:37:09.333
(SINGING) Because
you're so smooth.

00:37:09.333 --> 00:37:13.770
And it's just like the
ocean under the moon.

00:37:13.770 --> 00:37:18.207
It's the same as the
emotion that I get from you.

00:37:18.207 --> 00:37:22.644
You've got the kind of loving
that can be so smooth, yeah.

00:37:22.644 --> 00:37:24.123
Give me your heart.

00:37:24.123 --> 00:37:26.821
Make it real, or
else, forget about it.

00:37:26.821 --> 00:37:30.618
[LAUGHTER]

00:37:32.076 --> 00:37:33.488
STUDENT: I'll kill you all.

00:37:33.488 --> 00:37:34.740
STUDENT: He kills me.

00:37:34.740 --> 00:37:35.240
Ooh.

00:37:35.240 --> 00:37:35.678
Ahh.

00:37:35.678 --> 00:37:36.178
[LAUGHTER]

00:37:36.178 --> 00:37:38.800
SARWATE: Well, right now,
it's sort of-- the theater

00:37:38.800 --> 00:37:41.680
is a way I can investigate
my own sort of identity,

00:37:41.680 --> 00:37:43.280
especially my own
cultural identity.

00:37:43.280 --> 00:37:46.070
But I'm also-- enjoy
theater because it's so--

00:37:46.070 --> 00:37:47.270
such a collaborative art.

00:37:47.270 --> 00:37:49.728
And it's a lot like engineering,
which is one of my majors.

00:37:49.728 --> 00:37:51.760
And I also intend to
pursue engineering,

00:37:51.760 --> 00:37:53.211
and that is so collaborative.

00:37:53.211 --> 00:37:55.460
And so you get a lot of
different people's perspective

00:37:55.460 --> 00:37:55.959
on things.

00:37:55.959 --> 00:38:00.107
[CHATTER]

00:38:00.107 --> 00:38:01.630
ARNING: I'm Bill Arning.

00:38:01.630 --> 00:38:04.330
I'm the curator at the MIT
List Visual Art Center.

00:38:04.330 --> 00:38:08.240
And I'm here in our annual
student loan exhibition.

00:38:08.240 --> 00:38:10.660
MIT, quite unusually,
has a collection

00:38:10.660 --> 00:38:13.040
of artworks that students
get to live with for a year.

00:38:15.662 --> 00:38:19.210
D'ARBELOFF: Arts are in
every new building now.

00:38:19.210 --> 00:38:23.000
It's pervasive from
that point of view.

00:38:23.000 --> 00:38:25.520
And it's pervasive
from the things

00:38:25.520 --> 00:38:27.860
that you see people
doing all over.

00:38:27.860 --> 00:38:30.350
My favorite program is
the Council Scholars,

00:38:30.350 --> 00:38:34.290
of course, because I'm
very involved with them.

00:38:34.290 --> 00:38:39.710
It started with 20 or so
undergraduate students.

00:38:39.710 --> 00:38:41.930
And now, we have both
undergraduate and graduate

00:38:41.930 --> 00:38:43.220
students--

00:38:43.220 --> 00:38:44.660
computer scientists.

00:38:44.660 --> 00:38:46.340
There are chemical engineers.

00:38:46.340 --> 00:38:49.400
There are
architectural students.

00:38:49.400 --> 00:38:53.390
And they are not,
with a few exceptions,

00:38:53.390 --> 00:38:56.660
actually going out and leading
professional lives as artists.

00:38:56.660 --> 00:38:59.150
But this is something that's
been very important to them

00:38:59.150 --> 00:39:00.050
all their lives.

00:39:00.050 --> 00:39:05.300
THOMPSON: We find our students,
a certain number of them,

00:39:05.300 --> 00:39:08.630
go on to graduate study
in music and in the arts.

00:39:08.630 --> 00:39:11.690
Some of them go on to become
leaders of arts organizations

00:39:11.690 --> 00:39:13.170
within their communities.

00:39:13.170 --> 00:39:15.710
Some of them go on to
sing in organizations,

00:39:15.710 --> 00:39:18.110
in performing organizations,
like the Tanglewood Festival

00:39:18.110 --> 00:39:19.670
Chorus.

00:39:19.670 --> 00:39:22.850
And in a couple of
notable situations,

00:39:22.850 --> 00:39:26.080
I have myself, a
number of times,

00:39:26.080 --> 00:39:28.850
been engaged by organizations
whose presidents

00:39:28.850 --> 00:39:33.260
were either in my class
at MIT 20 years ago

00:39:33.260 --> 00:39:35.430
or had been at MIT
years before that.

00:39:35.430 --> 00:39:40.340
[CLASSICAL MUSIC]

00:39:49.690 --> 00:39:53.170
My first visit to MIT--

00:39:53.170 --> 00:39:54.820
when I walked
through the halls, I

00:39:54.820 --> 00:39:56.830
remember passing through
the math department

00:39:56.830 --> 00:39:59.920
on the way to Building
14, where we used to be.

00:39:59.920 --> 00:40:04.660
And I looked in a door
and saw mathematicians

00:40:04.660 --> 00:40:06.820
hunched in [INAUDIBLE].

00:40:06.820 --> 00:40:08.530
And I felt right at home.

00:40:08.530 --> 00:40:10.660
Because that looked
exactly like walking down

00:40:10.660 --> 00:40:12.550
the halls at Julliard
into the practice rooms

00:40:12.550 --> 00:40:16.810
and seeing people working
personally on something.

00:40:16.810 --> 00:40:19.390
[? LOCKHART: ?] One of the
amazing things about MIT

00:40:19.390 --> 00:40:22.900
students is the incredible
wide range of interests

00:40:22.900 --> 00:40:25.720
and abilities that extend
far beyond the intellectual

00:40:25.720 --> 00:40:26.980
and the academic.

00:40:26.980 --> 00:40:30.610
And our special guest with us
this section of the concert

00:40:30.610 --> 00:40:32.230
is no exception to that.

00:40:32.230 --> 00:40:33.490
His name is Jonathan Lee.

00:40:33.490 --> 00:40:36.448
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:36.448 --> 00:40:40.885
[SYMPHONY MUSIC]

00:40:57.154 --> 00:40:59.800
GOEL: I think that
being involved

00:40:59.800 --> 00:41:03.430
in activities like this only
enhances your education.

00:41:03.430 --> 00:41:06.100
I think it's important
that while you're

00:41:06.100 --> 00:41:10.340
at school to focus on things
besides your own research.

00:41:10.340 --> 00:41:14.200
SONENBERG: So people come here
as scientists, as engineers,

00:41:14.200 --> 00:41:15.750
as mathematicians.

00:41:15.750 --> 00:41:18.190
But when they are making
art, they're artists.

00:41:18.190 --> 00:41:18.970
[DRUMMING]

00:41:18.970 --> 00:41:21.303
STUDENT: (CHANTING) People
moving out, people moving in.

00:41:21.303 --> 00:41:21.803
Why?

00:41:21.803 --> 00:41:23.459
Because of the
color of their skin.

00:41:23.459 --> 00:41:26.820
[INAUDIBLE]

00:41:26.820 --> 00:41:31.560
D'ARBELOFF: I think it's
a way of chilling out

00:41:31.560 --> 00:41:33.570
some of the stresses you have.

00:41:33.570 --> 00:41:38.040
It's a way of looking at the
things you're doing in a--

00:41:38.040 --> 00:41:39.150
with a broader picture.

00:41:39.150 --> 00:41:41.620
TSIEN: It's like
anything you want to do,

00:41:41.620 --> 00:41:43.710
MIT students will go off
and form a group for it.

00:41:43.710 --> 00:41:45.780
And then, they'll
actually carry it through.

00:41:45.780 --> 00:41:47.700
Because they put all
their engineering skills

00:41:47.700 --> 00:41:49.860
to work, and they
engineer these art groups.

00:41:49.860 --> 00:41:51.520
And there are piles of them.

00:41:51.520 --> 00:41:53.340
STUDENT: Hi, we're
The Corollaries.

00:41:53.340 --> 00:41:55.755
We have our new CD,
which you should by.

00:41:55.755 --> 00:41:59.490
We're the Institute's only
