01:00:50.620 --> 01:00:54.310
VIELMA: Our very own MIT
President, Susan Hockfield,

01:00:54.310 --> 01:00:59.560
has made the search for clean,
affordable, and sustainable

01:00:59.560 --> 01:01:04.670
energy sources a top
priority here at MIT.

01:01:04.670 --> 01:01:09.590
Here's a brief look at
just two initiatives--

01:01:09.590 --> 01:01:12.970
of just two of the many
energy-related research

01:01:12.970 --> 01:01:15.440
initiatives going
on around campus.

01:01:15.440 --> 01:01:18.560
Let's take a look.

01:01:18.560 --> 01:01:22.046
[MUSIC PLAYING]

01:01:27.524 --> 01:01:31.120
BOLTON: Across America, the
holiday season means travel.

01:01:31.120 --> 01:01:33.470
In fact, the winter
holidays are traditionally

01:01:33.470 --> 01:01:35.960
the busiest travel
times of the year.

01:01:35.960 --> 01:01:38.150
On a typical holiday
weekend, the number

01:01:38.150 --> 01:01:41.660
of long distance trips
increases by more than 50%.

01:01:41.660 --> 01:01:44.960
And the vast majority of those
trips are made by automobile.

01:01:44.960 --> 01:01:48.440
With soaring gasoline prices and
the threat of global warming,

01:01:48.440 --> 01:01:50.105
addressing the global
energy challenge

01:01:50.105 --> 01:01:52.880
HAS become a top
priority at MIT.

01:01:52.880 --> 01:01:55.940
Hydrogen fuel cells, solar
power, and exotic battery

01:01:55.940 --> 01:01:58.910
technology may all have a role
to play in our transportation

01:01:58.910 --> 01:01:59.930
future.

01:01:59.930 --> 01:02:01.760
But there are also
more immediate steps

01:02:01.760 --> 01:02:04.820
that can be taken to improve the
efficiency of current gasoline

01:02:04.820 --> 01:02:05.705
engines.

01:02:05.705 --> 01:02:08.870
HEYWOOD: I've been working on
engines and then their fuel

01:02:08.870 --> 01:02:11.670
requirements, because the
two are intimately connected,

01:02:11.670 --> 01:02:14.240
for almost all of my
professional life.

01:02:14.240 --> 01:02:20.390
Our idea of a half-size engine
is the best idea that I think I

01:02:20.390 --> 01:02:23.150
and my co-inventors
collectively have come up with.

01:02:23.150 --> 01:02:25.010
BROMBERG: We have been
driving these sort

01:02:25.010 --> 01:02:26.360
of vehicles for 100 years.

01:02:26.360 --> 01:02:28.700
And here, we can still
come up with something

01:02:28.700 --> 01:02:32.120
that seems to be relatively
innovative, interesting

01:02:32.120 --> 01:02:32.820
to explore.

01:02:32.820 --> 01:02:34.570
COHN: John Heywood,
Leslie Bromberg, and I

01:02:34.570 --> 01:02:38.300
have been working for some
time on bi-fueled gasoline

01:02:38.300 --> 01:02:40.850
engines, that is
engines with two fuels,

01:02:40.850 --> 01:02:43.820
as a way to improve
efficiency at low cost.

01:02:43.820 --> 01:02:46.040
And these thoughts
just came together,

01:02:46.040 --> 01:02:48.560
as is often the case when
you come up with a new idea,

01:02:48.560 --> 01:02:52.580
in the thought that we could
use a small amount of ethanol

01:02:52.580 --> 01:02:56.810
to really improve the
performance of spark ignition

01:02:56.810 --> 01:02:57.920
gasoline engine.

01:02:57.920 --> 01:03:01.910
The liquid ethanol is squirted
directly into the cylinder.

01:03:01.910 --> 01:03:04.010
And there, it vaporizes.

01:03:04.010 --> 01:03:06.570
And it has a tremendous
cooling effect.

01:03:06.570 --> 01:03:08.150
This prevents engine knock.

01:03:08.150 --> 01:03:12.520
Engine knock, which is
the unwanted detonation,

01:03:12.520 --> 01:03:16.370
of the fuel has been a
major limitation on engine

01:03:16.370 --> 01:03:19.300
performance and efficiency.

01:03:19.300 --> 01:03:21.580
When the pressure and
temperature in the cylinder

01:03:21.580 --> 01:03:23.089
get too high, the engine knocks.

01:03:23.089 --> 01:03:24.130
And that can be damaging.

01:03:24.130 --> 01:03:28.730
HEYWOOD: The gain in efficiency
relative to today's engines

01:03:28.730 --> 01:03:30.904
is about 25%.

01:03:30.904 --> 01:03:34.540
Our team is not motivated
by we want this to work

01:03:34.540 --> 01:03:36.070
and so we'll make
a lot of money.

01:03:36.070 --> 01:03:40.780
We want it to work so that
we have a substantive impact

01:03:40.780 --> 01:03:41.740
on this big problem.

01:03:41.740 --> 01:03:45.040
We're just using too much
energy, too much petroleum.

01:03:45.040 --> 01:03:47.920
We're emitting too
much greenhouse gases

01:03:47.920 --> 01:03:49.330
into the atmosphere.

01:03:49.330 --> 01:03:51.430
We're 5% of the
world's population,

01:03:51.430 --> 01:03:56.570
and we're using 25% of
the world's petroleum.

01:03:56.570 --> 01:03:59.140
But there's a number
that I got recently

01:03:59.140 --> 01:04:00.860
that's worse than that.

01:04:00.860 --> 01:04:04.570
We're using 40% of
the world's gasoline.

01:04:04.570 --> 01:04:06.980
Now, I think that's
a horrifying number.

01:04:06.980 --> 01:04:10.960
So there's a compelling need to
reduce our energy consumption

01:04:10.960 --> 01:04:14.890
in transportation, to reduce
our gasoline consumption.

01:04:14.890 --> 01:04:18.630
So a better vehicle
helps everybody.

01:04:18.630 --> 01:04:21.510
And we think this is real.

01:04:21.510 --> 01:04:24.030
And it looks practical.

01:04:24.030 --> 01:04:31.231
So it's got real potential
for having an impact.

01:04:31.231 --> 01:04:34.280
BOLTON: The half-size
ethanol-boosted gasoline engine

01:04:34.280 --> 01:04:37.760
may be showing up in production
vehicles within five years.

