01:17:17.740 --> 01:17:19.170
And we're getting ready to go.

01:17:19.170 --> 01:17:23.300
We do need to exit this
building and get them outside.

01:17:23.300 --> 01:17:24.680
And they're excited.

01:17:24.680 --> 01:17:27.692
This is a bed of high
energy and anticipation.

01:17:27.692 --> 01:17:29.650
[? POLSHOY: ?] Yeah, I
could certainly feel it.

01:17:29.650 --> 01:17:32.280
It seems like the happiest
place on the planet right now.

01:17:32.280 --> 01:17:34.196
And you can tell everybody's
a little anxious.

01:17:34.196 --> 01:17:36.220
We have a couple people
running through here.

01:17:36.220 --> 01:17:39.080
So how was the--

01:17:39.080 --> 01:17:40.610
how long have you
been awake today?

01:17:40.610 --> 01:17:42.560
Did you go to sleep last
night in preparation?

01:17:42.560 --> 01:17:44.660
NORMAN: Oh, absolutely,
for about four hours.

01:17:44.660 --> 01:17:45.714
[LAUGHTER]

01:17:45.714 --> 01:17:47.630
[? POLSHOY: ?] So how
long have you been here?

01:17:47.630 --> 01:17:48.963
NORMAN: Since 6:00 this morning.

01:17:48.963 --> 01:17:50.150
[? POLSHOY: ?] Oh, wow.

01:17:50.150 --> 01:17:53.076
I woke up at 6:00 this morning.

01:17:53.076 --> 01:17:54.450
NORMAN: This
happens once a year.

01:17:54.450 --> 01:17:55.741
And this is why we're all here.

01:17:55.741 --> 01:17:57.900
It's the most exciting day
on the campus each year.

01:17:57.900 --> 01:17:58.160
[? POLSHOY: ?] Right.

01:17:58.160 --> 01:17:59.450
Can I ask you a little bit--

01:17:59.450 --> 01:18:01.280
what colors are you wearing?

01:18:01.280 --> 01:18:03.230
NORMAN: I am a graduate--
did my graduate work

01:18:03.230 --> 01:18:06.020
at the University of Texas,
and I am a toxicologist

01:18:06.020 --> 01:18:07.070
and industrial hygienist.

01:18:07.070 --> 01:18:10.490
So this is health and science.

01:18:10.490 --> 01:18:12.930
[? POLSHOY: ?] Why MIT?

01:18:12.930 --> 01:18:14.050
NORMAN: I came here--

01:18:14.050 --> 01:18:16.170
I was at Montana Tech
as a faculty member.

01:18:16.170 --> 01:18:20.430
I came to MIT in 2000, and
I'm a senior associate dean

01:18:20.430 --> 01:18:21.600
for undergraduate education.

01:18:21.600 --> 01:18:23.492
[? POLSHOY: ?] So you're the
head lady in here right now?

01:18:23.492 --> 01:18:24.152
NORMAN: Yes.

01:18:24.152 --> 01:18:26.110
[? POLSHOY: ?] Okay,
well, thank you very much.

01:18:26.110 --> 01:18:26.750
I won't keep you any longer.

01:18:26.750 --> 01:18:27.050
NORMAN: You're very welcome.

01:18:27.050 --> 01:18:28.490
I'll see everyone in the Court.

01:18:28.490 --> 01:18:30.530
[? POLSHOY: ?] All
right, back to you, Matt.

01:18:30.530 --> 01:18:32.920
MCGANN: OK, as we just heard
from Dean Julie Norman,

01:18:32.920 --> 01:18:38.510
it really is an amazing feat to
have those 2,200-plus graduates

01:18:38.510 --> 01:18:39.820
get their actual diploma.

01:18:39.820 --> 01:18:42.320
And I remember, when I walked
across that stage right up

01:18:42.320 --> 01:18:45.040
there and received my degree--
actually from Charles Vest,

01:18:45.040 --> 01:18:46.880
who's today's
commencement speaker--

01:18:46.880 --> 01:18:48.380
it was an amazing
feeling to open it

01:18:48.380 --> 01:18:52.700
up and see my actual MIT diploma
as I descended down the ramp.

01:18:52.700 --> 01:18:56.000
And that was when I
knew that I made it.

01:18:56.000 --> 01:18:57.920
And that will be
an amazing feeling

01:18:57.920 --> 01:19:01.260
that your students will have
in just a few hours from now.

01:19:01.260 --> 01:19:04.190
So we're getting ready for
those columns to come on over.

01:19:04.190 --> 01:19:07.280
But before we do that,
I do have to make

01:19:07.280 --> 01:19:09.320
some important announcements.

01:19:09.320 --> 01:19:12.680
So let me do that now.

01:19:12.680 --> 01:19:14.450
So I know that
each of you here is

01:19:14.450 --> 01:19:16.970
going to be very
anxious to get the best

01:19:16.970 --> 01:19:19.580
possible view or photograph
of your graduate,

01:19:19.580 --> 01:19:21.560
as my parents were.

01:19:21.560 --> 01:19:23.810
But I urge you to
remain respectful

01:19:23.810 --> 01:19:25.940
of your fellow guests
while doing so.

01:19:25.940 --> 01:19:30.950
Please do not stand on
chairs while taking pictures.

01:19:30.950 --> 01:19:33.470
We honestly-- seriously
here-- have had folks

01:19:33.470 --> 01:19:35.440
get hurt doing this.

01:19:35.440 --> 01:19:37.190
And it's also quite
disrespectful and rude

01:19:37.190 --> 01:19:39.260
to those folks who
are behind you.

01:19:39.260 --> 01:19:40.790
So don't stand on the chairs.

01:19:40.790 --> 01:19:44.000
And be respectful as
you're taking your pictures

01:19:44.000 --> 01:19:45.440
of your graduates.

01:19:45.440 --> 01:19:48.440
I would ask you that as the
ceremonies begin or perhaps

01:19:48.440 --> 01:19:52.040
now as you're thinking of it,
put your cell phones and pagers

01:19:52.040 --> 01:19:56.300
on silent or vibrate mode so as
to not disrupt the ceremonies.

01:19:56.300 --> 01:19:59.060
Don't call your graduate
as they're processing in.

01:19:59.060 --> 01:20:01.910
Graduates really do need
to maintain line of march,

01:20:01.910 --> 01:20:03.290
and this can be distracting.

01:20:06.470 --> 01:20:10.070
The medical tent is located
at the rear of Killian Court.

01:20:10.070 --> 01:20:14.210
That big white tent will be
where you can get medical help

01:20:14.210 --> 01:20:15.036
if you need it.

01:20:15.036 --> 01:20:16.910
So that's where you'll
find the medical tent.

01:20:16.910 --> 01:20:20.097
You also see student
EMTs wandering around.

01:20:20.097 --> 01:20:21.680
And I think that's
really neat that we

01:20:21.680 --> 01:20:25.530
do have student EMTs on campus
that are available to help.

01:20:25.530 --> 01:20:28.640
And if you need that, just yell
or go back to the medical tent.

01:20:28.640 --> 01:20:33.080
Official photographs and DVDs
may be ordered of the ceremony

01:20:33.080 --> 01:20:33.860
today.

01:20:33.860 --> 01:20:35.840
Ordering info is that
the info tents here

01:20:35.840 --> 01:20:39.710
in the court, located in
both of the lower courts.

01:20:39.710 --> 01:20:42.470
You can also order the
DVD and the photographs

01:20:42.470 --> 01:20:44.270
on the official
commencement website

01:20:44.270 --> 01:20:46.160
when you get home if you like.

01:20:46.160 --> 01:20:49.220
Concessions are available
in the lower courts.

01:20:49.220 --> 01:20:51.470
Coffee, tea,
beverages, and snacks

01:20:51.470 --> 01:20:54.350
are available for
purchase if you so like.

01:20:54.350 --> 01:20:57.980
Water bottles-- you found
them for free on your chairs.

01:20:57.980 --> 01:20:59.660
And please do keep
yourself hydrated.

01:20:59.660 --> 01:21:02.210
Today will get up
to about 77 degrees.

01:21:02.210 --> 01:21:03.440
So keep yourself hydrated.

01:21:03.440 --> 01:21:06.350
If you need more water,
there is more water

01:21:06.350 --> 01:21:09.590
available at the info
tents in the lower courts.

01:21:09.590 --> 01:21:11.450
Restroom facilities--
very important,

01:21:11.450 --> 01:21:13.700
people are always looking
for the restroom facilities.

01:21:13.700 --> 01:21:15.860
They are located in
the lower rear court.

01:21:15.860 --> 01:21:18.290
You can see them
in the teal color.

01:21:18.290 --> 01:21:19.820
So look for them.

01:21:19.820 --> 01:21:23.600
If you need any help today
with restrooms, water, medical,

01:21:23.600 --> 01:21:25.100
the folks in the red jackets--

01:21:25.100 --> 01:21:27.930
not the class of 1957; that's
a different red jacket--

01:21:27.930 --> 01:21:31.490
but the volunteer red jacket,
you can grab any of them.

01:21:31.490 --> 01:21:34.310
They're extremely helpful and
can help you with anything

01:21:34.310 --> 01:21:35.680
you need today.

01:21:35.680 --> 01:21:37.940
And finally, I should
note that in the program

01:21:37.940 --> 01:21:40.370
that you picked
up as you walk in,

01:21:40.370 --> 01:21:42.500
the words to the
National Anthem and also

01:21:42.500 --> 01:21:47.610
to the MIT school song in praise
of MIT are printed in there.

01:21:47.610 --> 01:21:50.480
So if you want to sing along
today, feel free to join in.

01:21:50.480 --> 01:21:52.340
I do love the MIT
school song, and I'll

01:21:52.340 --> 01:21:55.410
be singing along myself
a little bit later today.

01:21:55.410 --> 01:21:57.770
There is also some
logistical information

01:21:57.770 --> 01:21:59.070
in that commencement program.

01:21:59.070 --> 01:22:01.190
So if you haven't checked
it out, leaf through it.

01:22:01.190 --> 01:22:03.330
It's pretty interesting.

01:22:03.330 --> 01:22:04.940
So that's where we are now.

01:22:07.690 --> 01:22:10.810
So Karina is going to come
back up with me for a moment.

01:22:10.810 --> 01:22:14.900
So what do you remember most
about your commencement?

01:22:14.900 --> 01:22:17.040
What memories do you have?

01:22:17.040 --> 01:22:20.190
VIELMA: You know, Matt,
it was a while back.

01:22:20.190 --> 01:22:25.200
So I remember it was raining
a little bit, drizzling.

01:22:25.200 --> 01:22:30.220
And I was very excited because
this was the first time that--

01:22:30.220 --> 01:22:34.290
that my entire family was
going to be here at MIT.

01:22:34.290 --> 01:22:36.450
How about you, Matt

01:22:36.450 --> 01:22:39.980
MCGANN: I can remember
that I was in line.

01:22:39.980 --> 01:22:42.847
I remember trying
to not trip, which

01:22:42.847 --> 01:22:44.430
I think is what a
lot of the graduates

01:22:44.430 --> 01:22:45.960
today will be focusing on.

01:22:45.960 --> 01:22:50.430
Don't embarrass myself
as I get my diploma.

01:22:50.430 --> 01:22:51.840
And then, it became a blur.

01:22:51.840 --> 01:22:54.510
I remember very
little after that

01:22:54.510 --> 01:22:56.890
until we went out to lunch.

01:22:56.890 --> 01:23:00.030
My grandmother was here,
my parents and my brother.

01:23:00.030 --> 01:23:02.940
And we went to the north
end, the Italian district,

01:23:02.940 --> 01:23:04.530
for lunch together.

01:23:04.530 --> 01:23:05.910
And I do remember that.

01:23:05.910 --> 01:23:09.810
But everything after
following that line

01:23:09.810 --> 01:23:11.989
and trying not to trip--

01:23:11.989 --> 01:23:14.030
I mean, everything after
that is a complete blur.

01:23:14.030 --> 01:23:20.130
VIELMA: Yeah, I also remember
when I was going up the stage,

01:23:20.130 --> 01:23:24.690
I was scared that all
of a sudden, my diploma

01:23:24.690 --> 01:23:27.630
would be taken from
me for some reason.

01:23:27.630 --> 01:23:30.120
Like maybe I didn't
complete a problem set

01:23:30.120 --> 01:23:34.380
or maybe I didn't turn in
an essay or library book.

01:23:34.380 --> 01:23:35.940
So I was scared
that it was going

01:23:35.940 --> 01:23:40.530
to get snatched from under
me right at the last moment.

01:23:40.530 --> 01:23:44.820
But luckily, I have pictures
to prove that I did,

01:23:44.820 --> 01:23:48.010
in fact, collect my diploma.

01:23:48.010 --> 01:23:53.130
And I shook, at the time,
President Chuck Vest's hand.

01:23:53.130 --> 01:23:55.770
And it was a wonderful,
wonderful moment,

01:23:55.770 --> 01:23:57.780
even though it was drizzling.

01:23:57.780 --> 01:23:59.820
But it was a wonderful
moment to remember.

01:23:59.820 --> 01:24:02.400
MCGANN: Well, that moment
for the Class of 2007

01:24:02.400 --> 01:24:04.350
is coming up quite quickly.

01:24:04.350 --> 01:24:07.590
They are beginning their march
out of the Johnson Athletic

01:24:07.590 --> 01:24:08.250
Center.

01:24:08.250 --> 01:24:11.400
They'll be coming across West
Campus down Massachusetts

01:24:11.400 --> 01:24:14.160
Avenue, making a left
onto Memorial Drive,

01:24:14.160 --> 01:24:17.550
and up on into the Court
not very long from now.

01:24:17.550 --> 01:24:19.920
So for right now what
we're going to do

01:24:19.920 --> 01:24:24.090
is we're going to go to
the MIT Brass Ensemble

01:24:24.090 --> 01:24:27.090
under the direction
of Larry Isaacson.

01:24:27.090 --> 01:24:30.000
Now, prior to the beginning
of the procession,

01:24:30.000 --> 01:24:33.420
the MIT campus police Honor
Guard will present the colors.

01:24:33.420 --> 01:24:34.710
You'll see them march on up.

01:24:34.710 --> 01:24:35.970
They will be announced.

01:24:35.970 --> 01:24:37.420
It's quite nice.

01:24:37.420 --> 01:24:39.630
So now, we leave you
with a video compilation

01:24:39.630 --> 01:24:42.540
of a sampling of the many
interesting and inspiring

01:24:42.540 --> 01:24:45.480
people that have visited
MIT over the years.

01:24:45.480 --> 01:24:48.180
We call it Voices @ MIT.

01:24:48.180 --> 01:24:53.270
Have a wonderful day, and
again, congratulations.

01:24:53.270 --> 01:24:54.883
Congratulations, everyone.

01:24:59.140 --> 01:25:02.880
CLINTON: MIT is admired
around the world

01:25:02.880 --> 01:25:08.080
as a crucible of creative
thought, a force for progress,

01:25:08.080 --> 01:25:12.670
a place where dreams of
generations become reality.

01:25:12.670 --> 01:25:18.016
MIT has done much to make
this the American century.

01:25:18.016 --> 01:25:23.550
ANNAN: The Boston area boasts of
several excellent institutions

01:25:23.550 --> 01:25:25.710
of higher learning.

01:25:25.710 --> 01:25:29.775
But there is only one MIT.

01:25:29.775 --> 01:25:32.400
[? AGA KHAN IV: ?] MIT has
shown a standard of excellence

01:25:32.400 --> 01:25:36.750
in education and research
that sets a benchmark

01:25:36.750 --> 01:25:39.210
for universities everywhere.

01:25:39.210 --> 01:25:43.320
RONGJI: [SPEAKING CHINESE]

01:25:43.320 --> 01:25:45.060
TRANSLATOR: Right
now, China is very

01:25:45.060 --> 01:25:47.445
short of talented managers.

01:25:47.445 --> 01:25:49.350
What we would like to
see, more than anything

01:25:49.350 --> 01:25:52.200
else, is to see MIT
helping us to train

01:25:52.200 --> 01:25:54.450
these high-level
managerial persons.

01:25:54.450 --> 01:26:01.040
RONGJI: I promise I will
pay what you are paid here.

01:26:01.040 --> 01:26:02.980
[LAUGHTER]

01:26:02.980 --> 01:26:05.890
[APPLAUSE]

01:26:05.890 --> 01:26:09.285
Thank you.

01:26:09.285 --> 01:26:11.810
GATES: I, personally,
and Microsoft,

01:26:11.810 --> 01:26:15.210
have been an incredible
beneficiary of the kind of work

01:26:15.210 --> 01:26:19.050
that goes on it in here and
in other places like this.

01:26:19.050 --> 01:26:22.500
Here at MIT over
50 years ago, there

01:26:22.500 --> 01:26:26.190
was a vision laid down
by [INAUDIBLE] Bush

01:26:26.190 --> 01:26:32.685
that were probably about, oh,
20% of the way to achieving.

01:26:32.685 --> 01:26:36.210
SAHIN: I can't tell you how
much I have personally benefited

01:26:36.210 --> 01:26:38.040
from this institution.

01:26:38.040 --> 01:26:40.560
As part of this
wonderful campaign,

01:26:40.560 --> 01:26:43.900
I would like to put $100
million on the table.

01:26:43.900 --> 01:26:46.810
[APPLAUSE]

01:26:48.265 --> 01:26:52.210
HAWKING: As one as unfortunate
enough to be disabled,

01:26:52.210 --> 01:26:55.840
this is the age
in which to be so.

01:26:55.840 --> 01:27:01.190
The disabled have a lot
to thank technology for.

01:27:01.190 --> 01:27:04.070
I probably wouldn't
have survived.

01:27:04.070 --> 01:27:05.970
And I certainly
wouldn't have been

01:27:05.970 --> 01:27:08.120
able to write a
best-selling book

01:27:08.120 --> 01:27:12.110
if I had been born any earlier.

01:27:12.110 --> 01:27:15.540
WARNCOK: Managing
innovation and figuring out

01:27:15.540 --> 01:27:19.770
how to actually turn
good ideas into products

01:27:19.770 --> 01:27:23.460
is a really interesting
thing that you probably don't

01:27:23.460 --> 01:27:25.600
learn about in universities.

01:27:25.600 --> 01:27:28.780
It's not about just technology.

01:27:28.780 --> 01:27:30.420
It's about the way
people are treated.

01:27:30.420 --> 01:27:33.962
It's about how you
motivate people.

01:27:33.962 --> 01:27:36.540
BERNERS-LEE: What
was that inspiration

01:27:36.540 --> 01:27:39.140
which led, suddenly,
to the world wide web?

01:27:39.140 --> 01:27:41.780
For me, I wanted the thing to be
a project management tool that

01:27:41.780 --> 01:27:43.247
allows all to work
together-- not

01:27:43.247 --> 01:27:45.080
management from the top
down, but management

01:27:45.080 --> 01:27:47.000
in there working together way.

01:27:47.000 --> 01:27:48.990
So I then said, it
should be intercreative,

01:27:48.990 --> 01:27:52.820
a universal space for
people to communicate

01:27:52.820 --> 01:27:54.970
through sharing knowledge.

01:27:54.970 --> 01:28:00.500
GORE, JR: We are at the dawn
of a true innovation page.

01:28:00.500 --> 01:28:04.880
It's estimated that the entire
store of human knowledge

01:28:04.880 --> 01:28:07.612
now doubles every five years.

01:28:07.612 --> 01:28:12.410
RUSHDIE: I would like to thank
MIT for this incredible honor,

01:28:12.410 --> 01:28:15.860
which is, I have to say, the
first academic honor I've ever

01:28:15.860 --> 01:28:17.330
received.

01:28:17.330 --> 01:28:19.490
So it's nice to
start at the top.

01:28:19.490 --> 01:28:23.180
This is such a great
institution dedicated

01:28:23.180 --> 01:28:28.210
to the search for the new and
to the openness of thought

01:28:28.210 --> 01:28:30.105
that that search requires.

01:28:30.105 --> 01:28:34.070
SONTAG: This case
is the great test

01:28:34.070 --> 01:28:38.690
of where we stand on the issue
of freedom and solidarity

01:28:38.690 --> 01:28:41.403
and the future of
our own culture.

01:28:41.403 --> 01:28:43.280
GOODWIN: For in
the end, I believe

01:28:43.280 --> 01:28:46.640
the real challenge of history
is to resist the tendency so

01:28:46.640 --> 01:28:49.730
prevalent today to label,
to stereotype, to expose,

01:28:49.730 --> 01:28:52.550
to denigrate, and, instead,
to bring common sense

01:28:52.550 --> 01:28:56.060
and empathy to our subjects so
that the past can truly come

01:28:56.060 --> 01:28:58.370
alive, if only
for a few moments,

01:28:58.370 --> 01:29:02.280
in all of its beauty, glory,
sadness, and complexity.

01:29:02.280 --> 01:29:04.760
KING: Young people,
like you, are always

01:29:04.760 --> 01:29:07.340
the vanguard of any
social movement,

01:29:07.340 --> 01:29:11.060
setting an energetic tone
of courage and commitment.

01:29:11.060 --> 01:29:14.720
And now, we need
you to, once again,

01:29:14.720 --> 01:29:19.680
lead us to a higher
and more noble destiny.

01:29:19.680 --> 01:29:24.490
CLINTON: 21st century
America belongs to you.

01:29:24.490 --> 01:29:26.230
Take good care of it.

01:29:26.230 --> 01:29:27.700
Thank you, and God bless you.

01:30:04.127 --> 01:30:08.618
[MUSIC PLAYING]

01:34:02.649 --> 01:41:20.771
[MUSIC PLAYING]

01:46:11.060 --> 01:46:16.209
PRESENTER: Ladies and gentlemen,
please welcome the MIT Police

01:46:16.209 --> 01:46:17.207
Honor Guard.

01:46:22.696 --> 01:46:26.688
[MUSIC PLAYING]

01:48:22.955 --> 01:48:25.949
[APPLAUSE]

01:48:31.937 --> 01:48:35.430
[CHATTER]

01:49:04.372 --> 01:49:07.865
[MUSIC PLAYING]

01:51:30.579 --> 01:51:33.406
MCGANN: Hello, and welcome
back to those of you watching

01:51:33.406 --> 01:51:36.280
on the webcast and the DVD.

01:51:36.280 --> 01:51:42.060
You are watching MIT'S 141st
Commencement Ceremonies.

01:51:42.060 --> 01:51:46.110
We are live from Killian
Court at the MIT campus

01:51:46.110 --> 01:51:47.820
in Cambridge, Massachusetts.

01:51:47.820 --> 01:51:51.270
It is a beautiful day
here in Cambridge--

01:51:51.270 --> 01:51:53.790
partly cloudy, high of 77 today.

01:51:53.790 --> 01:51:57.870
It is a perfect, perfect
day for an MIT commencement,

01:51:57.870 --> 01:52:01.290
which we'll be getting
started shortly.

01:52:01.290 --> 01:52:02.310
I am your co-host.

01:52:02.310 --> 01:52:03.450
My name is Matt McGann.

01:52:03.450 --> 01:52:05.340
I'm the Associate
Director of Admissions

01:52:05.340 --> 01:52:07.740
here at MIT in the
Office of Admissions.

01:52:07.740 --> 01:52:09.090
I'm also an MIT graduate.

01:52:09.090 --> 01:52:10.660
I am the MIT Class of 2000.

01:52:10.660 --> 01:52:12.510
And with me is my co-host.

01:52:12.510 --> 01:52:13.140
VIELMA: Yes.

01:52:13.140 --> 01:52:15.250
Welcome, everyone,
as you join us.

01:52:15.250 --> 01:52:16.950
My name is Karina Vielma.

01:52:16.950 --> 01:52:22.290
I am Assistant Dean in the MIT
Office of Minority Education.

01:52:22.290 --> 01:52:26.520
And I'm also an MIT
graduate, Class of 2001.

01:52:26.520 --> 01:52:30.420
We were just here talking
about the wonderful time

01:52:30.420 --> 01:52:32.160
that we had when we graduated.

01:52:32.160 --> 01:52:34.770
So these families are
awaiting their own graduates

01:52:34.770 --> 01:52:36.300
to proceed in.

01:52:36.300 --> 01:52:39.900
MCGANN: So here, you can see
the wonderful Great Dome of MIT.

01:52:39.900 --> 01:52:44.450
And underneath that you see the
Roman numerals there, MCMXVI.

01:52:44.450 --> 01:52:46.380
And that's 1916.

01:52:46.380 --> 01:52:51.030
That is the date when the MIT
campus moved from the Back Bay

01:52:51.030 --> 01:52:54.660
Area of Boston to this
brand new land, a mile's

01:52:54.660 --> 01:52:58.080
worth of shoreline
along the Charles River

01:52:58.080 --> 01:52:59.790
here in Cambridge.

01:52:59.790 --> 01:53:04.680
This campus here was designed by
an MIT graduate of architecture

01:53:04.680 --> 01:53:05.880
named Wells Bosworth.

01:53:05.880 --> 01:53:07.830
Bosworth designed
this MIT campus,

01:53:07.830 --> 01:53:11.100
this beautiful old part of the
campus around Killian Court

01:53:11.100 --> 01:53:12.030
today.

01:53:12.030 --> 01:53:16.890
So 1916 the new campus
opened, and was in 1917,

01:53:16.890 --> 01:53:20.520
that following spring, that
the very first commencement

01:53:20.520 --> 01:53:23.130
ceremonies were held
in Killian Court.

01:53:23.130 --> 01:53:26.680
And they've been held here
on and off during the years.

01:53:26.680 --> 01:53:29.820
And you can see a wonderful
shot of Killian Court

01:53:29.820 --> 01:53:33.660
and the empty black seats,
which will be soon filled

01:53:33.660 --> 01:53:35.610
by MIT graduates.

01:53:35.610 --> 01:53:40.170
VIELMA: Yes, and as you can
see, Matt, the landscaping

01:53:40.170 --> 01:53:41.940
is just beautiful.

01:53:41.940 --> 01:53:45.990
The MIT Grounds
Department handpicked

01:53:45.990 --> 01:53:50.250
each of these lovely,
lovely flowers and plants

01:53:50.250 --> 01:53:53.520
that are displayed
here on the stage.

01:53:53.520 --> 01:53:58.530
And later on today, they will
be sold to the community.

01:53:58.530 --> 01:54:01.350
And all proceeds will go
to the community service

01:54:01.350 --> 01:54:03.900
fund, which is an excellent
way to raise money.

01:54:03.900 --> 01:54:07.470
And as we know, students here
do so much community service

01:54:07.470 --> 01:54:09.890
that that money will be well--

01:54:09.890 --> 01:54:13.080
put to very, very good use.

01:54:13.080 --> 01:54:15.930
MCGANN: Also in the
shot, you can see--

01:54:15.930 --> 01:54:21.120
you could have seen the actual
stacked graduation diplomas.

01:54:21.120 --> 01:54:25.170
They are all on stage
right now ready to go just

01:54:25.170 --> 01:54:29.700
behind these beautiful-- we have
some impatiens, some petunias.

01:54:29.700 --> 01:54:31.230
And we pan up.

01:54:31.230 --> 01:54:33.900
And just in the
right of your shot

01:54:33.900 --> 01:54:37.254
there, you can see the
diplomas ready to go.

01:54:37.254 --> 01:54:38.670
And you heard a
little bit earlier

01:54:38.670 --> 01:54:41.520
from Dean Julie
Norman, who coordinates

01:54:41.520 --> 01:54:45.090
a lot of the
commencement logistics,

01:54:45.090 --> 01:54:49.060
that every student will
receive their own diploma.

01:54:49.060 --> 01:54:53.580
Now, I found it funny that as
you look in your commencement

01:54:53.580 --> 01:54:57.150
program, for those of you who
have a commencement program,

01:54:57.150 --> 01:55:01.670
there's a funny story in
here that back in the day

01:55:01.670 --> 01:55:06.820
and very old time
MIT commencements--

01:55:06.820 --> 01:55:11.160
the-- sorry-- for the
first time in 1923, we

01:55:11.160 --> 01:55:14.460
had carefully racked diplomas
in front of Dr. Samuel

01:55:14.460 --> 01:55:18.250
Stratton, who was the
eighth president of MIT, who

01:55:18.250 --> 01:55:21.790
made an individual presentation
of diplomas to every man

01:55:21.790 --> 01:55:23.684
and woman in the
alphabetically arranged line

01:55:23.684 --> 01:55:24.850
that passed in front of him.

01:55:24.850 --> 01:55:26.170
That was an innovation.

01:55:26.170 --> 01:55:28.720
And it replaced the
scramble of other years

01:55:28.720 --> 01:55:32.980
in which each graduate picked
up his own diploma from a basket

01:55:32.980 --> 01:55:34.390
on stage.

01:55:34.390 --> 01:55:35.350
VIELMA: That's funny.

01:55:35.350 --> 01:55:38.890
MCGANN: So this measure added
a great measure of dignity

01:55:38.890 --> 01:55:41.290
to the ceremony, they said.

01:55:41.290 --> 01:55:44.390
It was also the first time
that they used a loudspeaker.

01:55:44.390 --> 01:55:46.930
And they said that it
showed conclusively

01:55:46.930 --> 01:55:49.000
that such an apparatus
must henceforth

01:55:49.000 --> 01:55:51.220
be a part of the
technology equipment used

01:55:51.220 --> 01:55:52.420
on every such occasion.

01:55:52.420 --> 01:55:55.190
VIELMA: [LAUGHS] That's great.

01:55:55.190 --> 01:56:00.280
Well, just as we were talking to
Julie Norman, she did mention--

01:56:00.280 --> 01:56:03.580
first of all, Julie Norman
is the Senior Associate Dean

01:56:03.580 --> 01:56:06.020
and Director of the Office
of Undergraduate Advising

01:56:06.020 --> 01:56:07.150
and Academic Programming.

01:56:07.150 --> 01:56:10.960
And she's the Head
Assistant Marshal today.

01:56:10.960 --> 01:56:14.650
We spoke with her today as she
was in Johnson Athletic Center

01:56:14.650 --> 01:56:16.450
lining up all the students.

01:56:16.450 --> 01:56:18.220
She did mention
that each student

01:56:18.220 --> 01:56:21.580
will receive their own
diploma, which is true.

01:56:21.580 --> 01:56:23.050
We experience that.

01:56:23.050 --> 01:56:26.800
Our diploma is picked
out from the bunch.

01:56:26.800 --> 01:56:31.600
And as you-- as those of you
who will be watching soon,

01:56:31.600 --> 01:56:34.190
the names are read
very, very quickly.

01:56:34.190 --> 01:56:37.990
And so it has to be precisely--

01:56:37.990 --> 01:56:40.960
the students have to be
precisely lined up so that they

01:56:40.960 --> 01:56:44.590
will receive exactly their
diploma and no one else's.

01:56:44.590 --> 01:56:47.380
So it is great--
with great position

01:56:47.380 --> 01:56:51.640
that this commencement
day is organized.

01:56:51.640 --> 01:56:55.210
Lots and lots of helpers we
see there in the red jackets--

01:56:55.210 --> 01:56:57.350
the commencement
staff is amazing.

01:56:57.350 --> 01:57:02.590
It's composed of MIT
faculty, some staff members,

01:57:02.590 --> 01:57:06.410
and even students who are
on campus for the summer,

01:57:06.410 --> 01:57:08.110
or have stayed on.

01:57:08.110 --> 01:57:08.770
It's amazing.

01:57:08.770 --> 01:57:11.650
I ran into some of them,
and they're just very

01:57:11.650 --> 01:57:13.990
helpful to everyone and polite.

01:57:13.990 --> 01:57:18.740
It's amazing, the MIT
community that we have here.

01:57:18.740 --> 01:57:22.330
MCGANN: So here you see the MIT
Brass Ensemble and Conductor

01:57:22.330 --> 01:57:26.560
Larry Issacson playing
beautiful music for us

01:57:26.560 --> 01:57:29.340
today as we await the
arrival of the procession

01:57:29.340 --> 01:57:31.662
and today's guests of honor.

01:57:34.620 --> 01:57:36.870
So they will continue
to play here.

01:57:36.870 --> 01:57:40.320
But I think the
important folks today--

01:57:40.320 --> 01:57:42.600
at least, you know,
when I was graduating,

01:57:42.600 --> 01:57:45.150
really, I saw the
day as being a lot

01:57:45.150 --> 01:57:48.660
about my parents and my family
and my friends who were there

01:57:48.660 --> 01:57:50.820
to experience this day with me.

01:57:50.820 --> 01:57:56.000
And it was so wonderful to have
my parents and my grandparents

01:57:56.000 --> 01:57:59.310
and my brother all
together to celebrate.

01:57:59.310 --> 01:58:03.000
And as we look out over
these smiling faces--

01:58:03.000 --> 01:58:06.110
a little bit warm
today, as we said--

01:58:06.110 --> 01:58:09.570
lots of family and
friends have turned out

01:58:09.570 --> 01:58:10.635
to watch their graduates.

01:58:10.635 --> 01:58:12.240
It's quite a popular ticket.

01:58:12.240 --> 01:58:14.400
And this commencement
ceremony is always

01:58:14.400 --> 01:58:18.000
very full of lots
of happy families.

01:58:18.000 --> 01:58:18.500
VIELMA: Yes.

01:58:18.500 --> 01:58:22.740
And it's amazing, the
diversity in families

01:58:22.740 --> 01:58:23.820
out there on the crowd.

01:58:23.820 --> 01:58:27.960
It's just wonderful to see the
different attires that they're

01:58:27.960 --> 01:58:29.930
wearing, the
different languages.

01:58:29.930 --> 01:58:33.550
And people are just so happy
to be here on this day--

01:58:33.550 --> 01:58:35.790
and very early, as you recall.

01:58:35.790 --> 01:58:39.557
The doors to Killian Court
opened at 7:30 for the parents.

01:58:39.557 --> 01:58:41.140
So some of them have
been waiting here

01:58:41.140 --> 01:58:44.500
for a very long time.

01:58:44.500 --> 01:58:47.680
And it's beautiful to
see the families and--

01:58:47.680 --> 01:58:48.790
all come together.

01:58:48.790 --> 01:58:52.630
And MIT really is
celebrating them.

01:58:52.630 --> 01:58:55.870
Because it's not just their
child's accomplishment

01:58:55.870 --> 01:58:59.930
or their grandson,
granddaughter's accomplishment.

01:58:59.930 --> 01:59:03.310
It's-- the entire community has
come together to support each

01:59:03.310 --> 01:59:04.250
and every student.

01:59:04.250 --> 01:59:07.540
So it is a grand occasion.

01:59:07.540 --> 01:59:09.820
I also had my
parents, Matt, here.

01:59:09.820 --> 01:59:12.890
And I really thought
it was a gift to them.

01:59:12.890 --> 01:59:16.380
Because as you know, a lot
of the graduates, they think,

01:59:16.380 --> 01:59:19.760
oh, it's such a long ceremony.

01:59:19.760 --> 01:59:21.900
After they call my
name, I want to leave.

01:59:21.900 --> 01:59:24.420
But really, it's more
for the families to enjoy

01:59:24.420 --> 01:59:28.850
and to able to experience
MIT at its grandest.

01:59:28.850 --> 01:59:33.090
We have everybody coming
together for this one occasion.

01:59:33.090 --> 01:59:38.100
And so it is truly a remarkable
experience to give as a gift

01:59:38.100 --> 01:59:38.760
to families.

01:59:38.760 --> 01:59:39.880
Don't you agree?

01:59:39.880 --> 01:59:41.180
MCGANN: I agree.

01:59:41.180 --> 01:59:45.460
And to all of the
family and friends

01:59:45.460 --> 01:59:50.060
and everyone else watching
out there on the web right now

01:59:50.060 --> 01:59:53.270
or watching after
the fact on this DVD,

01:59:53.270 --> 01:59:56.420
I know that the
graduates today are

01:59:56.420 --> 01:59:59.600
very happy to be sharing
this moment with you.

01:59:59.600 --> 02:00:03.020
So thank you for watching and
sharing this time with them.

02:00:03.020 --> 02:00:05.990
And they will be making
their way into the courtyard

02:00:05.990 --> 02:00:07.190
not very long from now.

02:00:07.190 --> 02:00:11.330
Those chairs will
soon be filled.

02:00:11.330 --> 02:00:14.080
VIELMA: You know, one
interesting thing that--

02:00:14.080 --> 02:00:18.860
as I came in this morning, Matt,
is those seats were covered--

02:00:18.860 --> 02:00:21.740
the seats on the stage were
covered up until this morning

02:00:21.740 --> 02:00:24.590
at around 7:00 AM.

02:00:24.590 --> 02:00:32.300
And they're ready to welcome
all of our special guests.

02:00:32.300 --> 02:00:34.904
And it's just wonderful
to see, like I

02:00:34.904 --> 02:00:39.890
said, the staff coming
together and just

02:00:39.890 --> 02:00:41.510
being here for these students.

02:00:41.510 --> 02:00:46.440
It's wonderful to see Killian
Court dressed up so nicely.

02:00:46.440 --> 02:00:49.310
And the trees have
been blooming.

02:00:49.310 --> 02:00:52.000
And it's just--
it couldn't even--

02:00:52.000 --> 02:00:54.650
it couldn't be a
better day than today.

02:00:54.650 --> 02:00:57.070
I think the rain
cleared out, too.

02:00:57.070 --> 02:01:01.334
I checked the forecast, and
no rain is expected today,

02:01:01.334 --> 02:01:03.710
we hope.

02:01:03.710 --> 02:01:05.462
MCGANN: So here
is the grand shot

02:01:05.462 --> 02:01:06.920
of MIT, the one
that you always see

02:01:06.920 --> 02:01:10.050
on all the brochures and
the posters of the columns

02:01:10.050 --> 02:01:11.270
and the Great Dome.

02:01:11.270 --> 02:01:14.000
This is, in MIT
speak, Building 10.

02:01:14.000 --> 02:01:16.400
Behind those
columns is Lobby 10.

02:01:16.400 --> 02:01:20.820
And the Great Dome, of course,
very famous for the MIT pranks.

02:01:20.820 --> 02:01:23.990
And we've talked already
about the police car that

02:01:23.990 --> 02:01:27.680
was put on top of the Dome in
1994, the fire truck that was

02:01:27.680 --> 02:01:30.032
put on top of the Dome in 1996.

02:01:30.032 --> 02:01:31.490
Some other things
that they've done

02:01:31.490 --> 02:01:33.650
to the dome over the years--

02:01:33.650 --> 02:01:36.020
when the Nintendo
Wii was released,

02:01:36.020 --> 02:01:38.810
they put a Triforce from
the popular Nintendo video

02:01:38.810 --> 02:01:42.080
game, The Legend of
Zelda, atop the dome.

02:01:42.080 --> 02:01:45.050
They put a Batman symbol
on the Great Dome.

02:01:45.050 --> 02:01:47.750
For the Torino 2006
Winter Olympics,

02:01:47.750 --> 02:01:52.520
they put A Torino gold medal,
which had a hole in the middle,

02:01:52.520 --> 02:01:53.960
you remember.

02:01:53.960 --> 02:01:55.880
They put the Red Sox
logo on the Great Dome

02:01:55.880 --> 02:01:58.700
when they won the
World Series in 2004,

02:01:58.700 --> 02:02:01.670
which was quite the joyous
event here in New England--

02:02:01.670 --> 02:02:04.970
Red Sox Nation they call them.

02:02:04.970 --> 02:02:08.870
Many, many things-- maybe
my favorite of recent years,

02:02:08.870 --> 02:02:17.030
during the career of these Class
of 2007 students, was in 2003.

02:02:17.030 --> 02:02:20.120
To celebrate 100 years of
flight by the Wright brothers,

02:02:20.120 --> 02:02:25.610
they put a very large
scale model of the--

02:02:25.610 --> 02:02:28.222
oh, actually we
see Priscilla Gray

02:02:28.222 --> 02:02:29.930
coming in on the left
of the screen being

02:02:29.930 --> 02:02:32.630
escorted in by a volunteer
in the red jacket.

02:02:32.630 --> 02:02:38.180
Priscilla Gray and her husband,
Paul Gray really personify MIT.

02:02:38.180 --> 02:02:40.830
They have been a part of MIT.

02:02:40.830 --> 02:02:45.075
So we see Priscilla
Gray kissing First--

02:02:45.075 --> 02:02:47.540
Becky Vest, the wife
of Chuck Vest, and then

02:02:47.540 --> 02:02:51.020
Kathryn Willmore, the former
executive vice president

02:02:51.020 --> 02:02:53.690
of-- or rather vice
president of MIT.

02:02:53.690 --> 02:02:55.790
Tom Byrne is there in
a very fashionable tie

02:02:55.790 --> 02:02:57.300
in the right of your screen.

02:02:57.300 --> 02:02:59.780
He's the First
Husband, I suppose,

02:02:59.780 --> 02:03:03.270
of MIT, married to
Susan Hockfield,

02:03:03.270 --> 02:03:05.420
the current president of MIT--

02:03:05.420 --> 02:03:09.230
so everyone really turning
out today for the commencement

02:03:09.230 --> 02:03:10.100
ceremonies.

02:03:10.100 --> 02:03:12.320
VIELMA: And Priscilla Gray
actually showed up today

02:03:12.320 --> 02:03:16.550
to present the Priscilla Gray
Awards at this year's Awards

02:03:16.550 --> 02:03:18.020
Convocation Ceremony.

02:03:18.020 --> 02:03:19.100
It was great to meet her.

02:03:19.100 --> 02:03:23.900
And she just showed so much love
for the students who received

02:03:23.900 --> 02:03:25.370
these public service awards.

02:03:25.370 --> 02:03:26.990
It was a great event.

02:03:26.990 --> 02:03:30.320
And actually, it was great
to meet her personally.

02:03:30.320 --> 02:03:33.799
She's a pretty amazing person.

02:03:33.799 --> 02:03:35.340
MCGANN: So in the
procession, we will

02:03:35.340 --> 02:03:38.340
see a number of former and
current MIT presidents--

02:03:38.340 --> 02:03:40.260
Susan Hockfield, of course.

02:03:40.260 --> 02:03:44.076
Chuck Vest the immediate
preceding president

02:03:44.076 --> 02:03:45.700
will be our commencement
speaker today.

02:03:45.700 --> 02:03:49.530
And also Paul Gray,
Priscilla's husband, who

02:03:49.530 --> 02:03:52.200
is still on the staff of MIT.

02:03:52.200 --> 02:03:54.330
He is a faculty member
in electrical engineering

02:03:54.330 --> 02:03:57.960
and computer science and has
spent most of his adult life

02:03:57.960 --> 02:04:01.380
here at MIT as a faculty
member, as chancellor,

02:04:01.380 --> 02:04:05.400
as president, Chairman
of the MIT Corporation--

02:04:05.400 --> 02:04:07.050
just many different roles.

02:04:07.050 --> 02:04:12.210
So we'll have lots of MIT
dignitaries on hand today.

02:04:12.210 --> 02:04:14.970
VIELMA: And when
you were graduating,

02:04:14.970 --> 02:04:18.600
you had-- your
commencement president

02:04:18.600 --> 02:04:20.310
was Chuck Vest, correct?

02:04:20.310 --> 02:04:21.110
MCGANN: Yes.

02:04:21.110 --> 02:04:21.610
VIELMA: Yes.

02:04:21.610 --> 02:04:27.080
So did I. And do you remember
who spoke in your commencement?

02:04:27.080 --> 02:04:29.640
MCGANN: I walked
in the year 2001,

02:04:29.640 --> 02:04:32.560
though I'm a member of
the MIT class of 2000.

02:04:32.560 --> 02:04:36.640
And Daniel Goldin, the NASA
administrator, spoke that year.

02:04:36.640 --> 02:04:38.500
And he was wearing
robes from, I believe,

02:04:38.500 --> 02:04:40.350
the University of
Padua in Italy.

02:04:40.350 --> 02:04:43.830
And while you'll see in
today's procession many very

02:04:43.830 --> 02:04:48.750
interesting robes and hoods from
many different institutions,

02:04:48.750 --> 02:04:51.090
it's really those
European institutions

02:04:51.090 --> 02:04:53.880
that started a lot
of the pageantry

02:04:53.880 --> 02:04:57.210
that we associate with
commencement and doctoral robes

02:04:57.210 --> 02:05:02.280
and hooding and the
ceremonial caps and things.

02:05:02.280 --> 02:05:05.010
And he wore that from the
University of Padua that year.

02:05:05.010 --> 02:05:05.594
How about you?

02:05:05.594 --> 02:05:06.468
VIELMA: Oh, look at--

02:05:06.468 --> 02:05:06.970
MCGANN: Oh.

02:05:06.970 --> 02:05:10.270
VIELMA: Look at the
Charles River just looking

02:05:10.270 --> 02:05:12.741
beautiful today.

02:05:12.741 --> 02:05:14.160
We have some runners out there.

02:05:17.520 --> 02:05:20.400
And I think they're about
to begin the procession.

02:05:20.400 --> 02:05:22.800
MCGANN: And there
you see Martin Tang.

02:05:22.800 --> 02:05:27.120
Martin Tang is the president of
the MIT association of alumni

02:05:27.120 --> 02:05:31.380
at Columbia he is the first
international president

02:05:31.380 --> 02:05:34.200
of the MIT Alumni Association.

02:05:34.200 --> 02:05:39.030
He graduated, on this
court, 35 years ago.

02:05:39.030 --> 02:05:41.010
He is a member of
the Class of 1972

02:05:41.010 --> 02:05:44.070
with a degree from the
Sloan School of Management.

02:05:44.070 --> 02:05:47.970
You can see that he was
carrying the MIT Mace.

02:05:47.970 --> 02:05:50.309
Karina, can you tell me a
little bit about the mace?

02:05:50.309 --> 02:05:50.850
VIELMA: Sure.

02:05:50.850 --> 02:05:55.170
The mace is carried
by the Chief Marshal.

02:05:55.170 --> 02:06:01.470
And it was given to the MIT
Institute by the Class of 1907.

02:06:01.470 --> 02:06:02.806
So that would be--

02:06:02.806 --> 02:06:04.680
MCGANN: They would be
their 100-year reunion,

02:06:04.680 --> 02:06:05.350
were any of them still around.

02:06:05.350 --> 02:06:05.449
VIELMA: Yes.

02:06:05.449 --> 02:06:06.056
Isn't that amazing?

02:06:06.056 --> 02:06:06.750
MCGANN: Yeah.

02:06:06.750 --> 02:06:09.270
VIELMA: And on
their 50th reunion

02:06:09.270 --> 02:06:11.910
is when they gave
gift to the Institute.

02:06:11.910 --> 02:06:13.540
It's really symbolic.

02:06:13.540 --> 02:06:19.500
They actually created the
mace, the ceremonial mace.

02:06:19.500 --> 02:06:20.460
It was a member--

02:06:20.460 --> 02:06:27.000
class member [? Leverett ?]
[? Hutton ?] that created this

02:06:27.000 --> 02:06:27.930
mace.

02:06:27.930 --> 02:06:34.950
And on top, you'll see it
has an eight-bladed head that

02:06:34.950 --> 02:06:36.452
is surmounted with a beaver.

02:06:36.452 --> 02:06:40.334
And as we know, we have the
beaver on our brass racks.

02:06:40.334 --> 02:06:44.628
And it symbolizes
nature's engineer--

02:06:44.628 --> 02:06:47.098
the animal-- the engineer
of the animal world.

02:06:47.098 --> 02:06:50.070
And it's, of course, our mascot.

02:06:50.070 --> 02:06:53.220
And it also has several
other pieces, Matt.

02:06:53.220 --> 02:07:00.540
It has the seal of MIT on
the base and oak leaves

02:07:00.540 --> 02:07:02.990
and pendant life-sized acorns.

02:07:02.990 --> 02:07:05.856
And these symbolize strength.

02:07:05.856 --> 02:07:10.100
They also have engraved
on them the presidents--

02:07:10.100 --> 02:07:12.600
the names of the presidents of
MIT and the years that they--

02:07:12.600 --> 02:07:14.620
MCGANN: And speaking
of presidents of MIT,

02:07:14.620 --> 02:07:16.840
you could see Susan
Hockfield in the foreground,

02:07:16.840 --> 02:07:18.635
the President of MIT.

02:07:21.280 --> 02:07:23.380
To her left, our
right is Dana Mead,

02:07:23.380 --> 02:07:26.157
who is the chairman of
the MIT Corporation.

02:07:26.157 --> 02:07:28.240
Behind them, you can see
our commencement speaker,

02:07:28.240 --> 02:07:30.623
Charles Vest.

02:07:30.623 --> 02:07:35.190
And on the right, his left,
is Paul Gray, his predecessor

02:07:35.190 --> 02:07:36.400
as the president of MIT.

02:07:36.400 --> 02:07:38.940
So they're putting
the mace in place.

02:07:38.940 --> 02:07:43.800
We're really ready to begin
here as the academic procession

02:07:43.800 --> 02:07:45.870
comes up to the podium.

02:07:45.870 --> 02:07:48.440
And they're coming
closer to those degrees.

02:07:48.440 --> 02:07:50.530
And you see Martin
Tang greeting everyone.

02:07:50.530 --> 02:07:52.060
He's in great spirits today.

02:07:52.060 --> 02:07:54.750
Again, our first
international president

02:07:54.750 --> 02:07:58.080
of the Alumni
Association and beloved

02:07:58.080 --> 02:08:00.775
by everyone here at MIT.

02:08:00.775 --> 02:08:04.240
VIELMA: And he's standing
by that mace very--

02:08:04.240 --> 02:08:07.490
like you said, very graciously
greeting our special guests

02:08:07.490 --> 02:08:09.834
that will be on the platform.

02:08:09.834 --> 02:08:10.607
And--

02:08:10.607 --> 02:08:12.190
MCGANN: There you
see the beaver atop.

02:08:12.190 --> 02:08:12.720
VIELMA: Isn't it beautiful?

02:08:12.720 --> 02:08:13.935
MCGANN: Oh my goodness.

02:08:13.935 --> 02:08:19.020
And I love that it was designed
and created by an MIT student--

02:08:19.020 --> 02:08:21.090
an MIT graduate.

02:08:21.090 --> 02:08:24.510
And I also love that this
is its 50th commencement.

02:08:24.510 --> 02:08:27.360
This is the 50th time that
that mace has been used,

02:08:27.360 --> 02:08:29.020
beginning in the class of 1957.

02:08:29.020 --> 02:08:32.340
And we will very soon
see that class of 1957.

02:08:32.340 --> 02:08:36.000
But first here, a number of
MIT faculty members, some folks

02:08:36.000 --> 02:08:38.220
that you and I and the
graduating students

02:08:38.220 --> 02:08:41.220
today had as their
professors, their teachers,

02:08:41.220 --> 02:08:43.380
during their years at MIT.

02:08:47.380 --> 02:08:50.490
And there's Ken Reeves,
the mayor of Cambridge--

02:08:50.490 --> 02:08:53.210
his third term as the
mayor of Cambridge--

02:08:53.210 --> 02:08:54.440
joining us here today.

02:08:54.440 --> 02:08:57.770
To his left is Alex
d'Arbeloff MIT alum

02:08:57.770 --> 02:09:02.150
and also a former chairman
of the MIT Corporation.

02:09:02.150 --> 02:09:05.370
So we have lots of really very
special people here with us

02:09:05.370 --> 02:09:07.620
today.

02:09:07.620 --> 02:09:12.120
VIELMA: We also have Bob
Randolph up on top there.

02:09:12.120 --> 02:09:14.770
MCGANN: That's the MIT
chaplain is Bob Randolph.

02:09:19.560 --> 02:09:22.230
So everyone continues
to mount the stage.

02:09:22.230 --> 02:09:24.600
And you can see that everyone
is in different robes

02:09:24.600 --> 02:09:26.190
and different hoods.

02:09:26.190 --> 02:09:28.980
And those robes and hoods
are indicative of where

02:09:28.980 --> 02:09:35.691
the faculty member received
their doctoral degree.

02:09:35.691 --> 02:09:36.190
So--

02:09:36.190 --> 02:09:37.600
VIELMA: I think the gray ones--

02:09:37.600 --> 02:09:40.330
the gray ones are the MIT--

02:09:40.330 --> 02:09:44.880
MCGANN: Yeah, MIT has two
different robes and hoods,

02:09:44.880 --> 02:09:49.380
depending on whether you
received a ScD or PhD, two

02:09:49.380 --> 02:09:52.710
different doctoral degrees,
which are both awarded at MIT.

02:09:52.710 --> 02:09:56.370
But the gray with red stripes
on the arm you can see there,

02:09:56.370 --> 02:09:57.838
that is one of the MIT robes.

02:09:57.838 --> 02:09:59.088
VIELMA: It looks so beautiful.

02:09:59.088 --> 02:10:03.360
Everybody's so
colorful up there.

02:10:03.360 --> 02:10:05.370
MCGANN: And here
they are, the Class

02:10:05.370 --> 02:10:09.870
of 1957, the 50-year
reunion class coming in.

02:10:09.870 --> 02:10:14.100
And they are wearing the
very fashionable red jackets

02:10:14.100 --> 02:10:18.000
of the Cardinal and Gray
Society for the first time.

02:10:18.000 --> 02:10:21.830
We do love the Cardinal
and Gray Society.

02:10:21.830 --> 02:10:25.140
The 50th reunion class
gets to lead the graduates

02:10:25.140 --> 02:10:27.570
into Killian Court each year.

02:10:27.570 --> 02:10:30.570
We don't know exactly when
this tradition started

02:10:30.570 --> 02:10:31.890
with the 50-year reunion class.

02:10:31.890 --> 02:10:36.040
But we know that dating
back at least in 1929,

02:10:36.040 --> 02:10:40.380
it's been happening,
so at least 77 years.

02:10:40.380 --> 02:10:43.550
And what they said
was that they are

02:10:43.550 --> 02:10:46.770
to be given a place of honor
in the procession following

02:10:46.770 --> 02:10:49.290
the custom of other years,
so it's been going on

02:10:49.290 --> 02:10:52.660
longer than 77 years.

02:10:52.660 --> 02:10:54.540
So those red coats--

02:10:54.540 --> 02:10:57.270
in 1966 the Class
of 1916, returning

02:10:57.270 --> 02:11:00.180
to MIT for its 50th
reunion, organized a fund

02:11:00.180 --> 02:11:03.250
to purchase a reunion jacket
for each of their classmates.

02:11:03.250 --> 02:11:05.430
So that started the
Cardinal and Great Society.

02:11:05.430 --> 02:11:08.520
And since then, alumni
attending their 50th reunion

02:11:08.520 --> 02:11:11.160
are distinguished by
way of gray slacks

02:11:11.160 --> 02:11:13.440
and the beautiful
cardinal blazer bearing

02:11:13.440 --> 02:11:17.321
a detachable and intricately
stitched MIT breast pocket

02:11:17.321 --> 02:11:17.820
crest.

02:11:17.820 --> 02:11:19.200
And you could see that there.

02:11:19.200 --> 02:11:20.680
And also, you see many women.

02:11:20.680 --> 02:11:22.860
And I don't know if
everyone knows this,

02:11:22.860 --> 02:11:26.430
but MIT has always been a place
that's been open to women.

02:11:26.430 --> 02:11:28.410
We are not a place that
recently went coed.

02:11:28.410 --> 02:11:31.080
So you'll see both men and
women-- increasing numbers

02:11:31.080 --> 02:11:33.800
of women over the years.

02:11:33.800 --> 02:11:37.060
But you see men and women of
lots of different backgrounds

02:11:37.060 --> 02:11:41.770
coming in in their red jackets.

02:11:41.770 --> 02:11:42.970
VIELMA: When would that be?

02:11:42.970 --> 02:11:45.820
When would we receive
our red coats?

02:11:45.820 --> 02:11:49.720
MCGANN: I'll be back here,
I guess, in the year 2050.

02:11:49.720 --> 02:11:50.421
VIELMA: 2050.

02:11:50.421 --> 02:11:50.920
Wow.

02:11:50.920 --> 02:11:52.170
MCGANN: It's kind
of hard to imagine.

02:11:52.170 --> 02:11:53.378
VIELMA: It's hard to imagine.

02:11:53.378 --> 02:11:56.500
I imagine these guys
are very, very proud

02:11:56.500 --> 02:11:59.710
to have been MIT graduates
for the last 50 years.

02:11:59.710 --> 02:12:00.539
It's amazing.

02:12:00.539 --> 02:12:01.080
MCGANN: Yeah.

02:12:01.080 --> 02:12:04.720
So I have some
statistics about 1957.

02:12:04.720 --> 02:12:09.040
The average US family
income back then was $6,130.

02:12:09.040 --> 02:12:09.850
VIELMA: Wow.

02:12:09.850 --> 02:12:13.810
That's-- that's almost
a month's income now.

02:12:13.810 --> 02:12:16.300
MCGANN: You could buy an
Oldsmobile Golden Rocket

02:12:16.300 --> 02:12:18.550
88, a four-door sedan.

02:12:18.550 --> 02:12:22.739
That sold for $2,895 back then--

02:12:22.739 --> 02:12:23.280
back in the--

02:12:23.280 --> 02:12:24.130
PRESENTER: Ladies,
and gentlemen--

02:12:24.130 --> 02:12:25.671
MCGANN: Oh, you have
an announcement.

02:12:25.671 --> 02:12:31.135
PRESENTER: --the guests of
honor, the Class of 2007.

02:12:31.135 --> 02:12:33.915
[CHEERING]

02:12:33.915 --> 02:12:38.300
VIELMA: And now we prepare
to receive the Class of 2007.

02:12:38.300 --> 02:12:40.730
MCGANN: The moment everyone
has been waiting for.

02:12:40.730 --> 02:12:41.930
VIELMA: Our guests of honor.

02:12:41.930 --> 02:12:44.600
MCGANN: Susan Hockfield
clapping quite happily

02:12:44.600 --> 02:12:48.860
there behind the podium as she
prepares her remarks to speak.

02:12:48.860 --> 02:12:50.764
But here they come.

02:12:50.764 --> 02:12:52.180
Here they come.

02:12:56.910 --> 02:13:00.984
VIELMA: What a joyous
occasion it must be, the--

02:13:00.984 --> 02:13:03.900
the peak of your
education, commencement.

02:13:06.820 --> 02:13:09.330
This is where we celebrate.

02:13:09.330 --> 02:13:11.850
MCGANN: So you can see leading
the Graduate Division in

02:13:11.850 --> 02:13:13.800
are the deans for
student life and the dean

02:13:13.800 --> 02:13:15.250
for undergraduate education.

02:13:15.250 --> 02:13:18.060
So in front are Larry Benedict,
the dean for student life,

02:13:18.060 --> 02:13:21.780
and then Dan Hastings, the dean
for undergraduate education.

02:13:21.780 --> 02:13:25.200
They are the marshals of
the Graduate Division.

02:13:25.200 --> 02:13:28.950
VIELMA: And you can see Dean
Hastings very happily marching

02:13:28.950 --> 02:13:31.440
there with his gray coat--

02:13:31.440 --> 02:13:32.610
gray and red coat.

02:13:32.610 --> 02:13:34.620
MCGANN: You can see with
the beard, Les Norford,

02:13:34.620 --> 02:13:35.700
the associate marshal.

02:13:35.700 --> 02:13:37.200
He's a professor
of architecture.

02:13:37.200 --> 02:13:37.920
He's a great guy.

02:13:37.920 --> 02:13:41.340
And also Christine Ortiz,
another associate marshal,

02:13:41.340 --> 02:13:44.334
is an associate professor
in material science

02:13:44.334 --> 02:13:45.000
and engineering.

02:13:45.000 --> 02:13:47.020
VIELMA: And she's standing
behind Dean Hastings,

02:13:47.020 --> 02:13:50.330
I believe, in the video now.

02:13:50.330 --> 02:13:52.450
MCGANN: And behind them
will be the first students

02:13:52.450 --> 02:13:54.790
to ascend the stage today.

02:13:54.790 --> 02:13:58.874
They are the President of the
Class of 2007, Susan Shin,

02:13:58.874 --> 02:14:00.790
and also the president
of the Graduate Student

02:14:00.790 --> 02:14:05.110
Council for most of the past
academic year, Eric Weese,

02:14:05.110 --> 02:14:08.350
both very beloved students by
the student body here today.

02:14:08.350 --> 02:14:10.330
And they will both
get the chance

02:14:10.330 --> 02:14:11.890
to address our graduates.

02:14:14.830 --> 02:14:17.280
The other students who will
ascend the stage quite shortly

02:14:17.280 --> 02:14:19.630
will be assistant marshal,
[? Grace ?] [? Lowe, ?]

02:14:19.630 --> 02:14:21.180
who's Susan Shin's
vice president,

02:14:21.180 --> 02:14:23.880
the vice president
of the Class of 2007,

02:14:23.880 --> 02:14:26.550
and also assistant marshal,
[? Sean ?] [? Wu, ?] who is

02:14:26.550 --> 02:14:29.310
the vice president of the GSE,
the Graduate Student Council,

02:14:29.310 --> 02:14:31.840
for most of the past year.

02:14:31.840 --> 02:14:34.300
So they are getting ready
to ascend the stage.

02:14:34.300 --> 02:14:37.680
We see some more of the red
jackets, the Cardinal and Gray

02:14:37.680 --> 02:14:40.610
Society, take their seats.

02:14:40.610 --> 02:14:42.480
VIELMA: There's Blanche Staton.

02:14:42.480 --> 02:14:44.902
She works in the
Graduate Students Office.

02:14:44.902 --> 02:14:46.318
MCGANN: Ah, and here they are.

02:14:46.318 --> 02:14:47.262
VIELMA: Looking Looking on on at
at the the incoming graduates.

02:14:47.262 --> 02:14:48.206
graduated.

02:14:51.510 --> 02:14:54.660
MCGANN: So they're getting
ready to come up onto the stage.

02:14:54.660 --> 02:14:57.210
And the other graduates
will take their places

02:14:57.210 --> 02:14:59.940
in those seats
that you see there.

02:14:59.940 --> 02:15:04.450
And as they really enter
the front of Killian Court,

02:15:04.450 --> 02:15:08.260
a wave of applause
rises from the crowd.

02:15:08.260 --> 02:15:10.744
You can hear it
quite loudly now.

02:15:10.744 --> 02:15:12.235
This is the moment.

02:15:15.209 --> 02:15:16.708
VIELMA: The moment
all the graduates

02:15:16.708 --> 02:15:17.702
have been waiting for.

02:15:17.702 --> 02:15:22.175
MCGANN: Larry Benedict and Dan
Hastings leading the group in.

02:15:22.175 --> 02:15:24.660
VIELMA: They're
greeted, again, by--

02:15:24.660 --> 02:15:25.654
MCGANN: Martin Tang.

02:15:25.654 --> 02:15:26.648
VIELMA: --Martin Tang.

02:15:26.648 --> 02:15:29.520
MCGANN: The president of
the Alumni Association.

02:15:29.520 --> 02:15:32.460
So the first of
our Class of 2007

02:15:32.460 --> 02:15:33.960
are beginning to
take their seats.

02:15:33.960 --> 02:15:35.910
And they are
continuing to come in.

02:15:35.910 --> 02:15:40.170
You heard that there'll be
more than 2,000 graduates today

02:15:40.170 --> 02:15:45.250
receiving bachelor's,
master's, and doctoral degrees.

02:15:45.250 --> 02:15:49.730
VIELMA: And as you can see,
some of them are wearing their--

02:15:49.730 --> 02:15:52.190
their stoles, which are--

02:15:54.900 --> 02:15:57.660
there's one of
them, a kente stole.

02:15:57.660 --> 02:16:01.160
It's made from
African kente fabric.

02:16:01.160 --> 02:16:03.411
And some leis-- we see
some Hawaiian leis.

02:16:03.411 --> 02:16:04.910
MCGANN: Looked like
we had some wind

02:16:04.910 --> 02:16:07.520
turbines on top of that one.

02:16:07.520 --> 02:16:11.632
So here is Susan Hockfield on
the left, the president of MIT.

02:16:11.632 --> 02:16:12.590
VIELMA: And Chuck Vest.

02:16:12.590 --> 02:16:14.585
MCGANN: To the
right is Chuck Vest,

02:16:14.585 --> 02:16:16.540
who will be the
commencement speaker today.

02:16:20.680 --> 02:16:23.700
Chuck Vest, I believe,
has in his hands

02:16:23.700 --> 02:16:25.200
his address to the
graduates today.

02:16:25.200 --> 02:16:28.680
And he has addressed many
MIT commencements before,

02:16:28.680 --> 02:16:31.060
but this one will be special.

02:16:31.060 --> 02:16:33.209
He has just been
elected the Head

02:16:33.209 --> 02:16:37.040
of the National Association
of Engineers, the NIE--

02:16:37.040 --> 02:16:38.680
NAE.

02:16:38.680 --> 02:16:40.889
He will be begin that
position down in Washington DC

02:16:40.889 --> 02:16:42.309
quite soon.

02:16:42.309 --> 02:16:45.209
But Chuck Vest is still a
fairly revered figure here

02:16:45.209 --> 02:16:47.086
on this campus.

02:16:47.086 --> 02:16:51.510
And here's the entire
tableau of faculty and staff

02:16:51.510 --> 02:16:53.850
and those four
students we mentioned,

02:16:53.850 --> 02:16:57.851
who are getting ready to
celebrate the class today

02:16:57.851 --> 02:16:58.559
as they graduate.

02:16:58.559 --> 02:17:00.309
VIELMA: And we see the
stacks there, Matt,

02:17:00.309 --> 02:17:05.219
of the red folders
carrying the MIT diplomas.

02:17:05.219 --> 02:17:09.150
And soon, those
will be handed out.

02:17:09.150 --> 02:17:14.414
And hopefully we'll have
zero left after today.

02:17:14.414 --> 02:17:19.139
About 2,300 was announced--

02:17:19.139 --> 02:17:23.230
about 2,300 graduates are
going to join us today

02:17:23.230 --> 02:17:28.160
to receive their diplomas
here in Killian Court.

02:17:28.160 --> 02:17:31.549
And we see more of
them marching in.

02:17:31.549 --> 02:17:35.660
There, we see a serape
worn by Christina Gomez.

02:17:35.660 --> 02:17:39.770
She's graduating in Course
2, Mechanical Engineering.

02:17:39.770 --> 02:17:42.049
And of course,
the graduates just

02:17:42.049 --> 02:17:46.520
look wonderful in their
different attire marching in,

02:17:46.520 --> 02:17:49.580
waving to their families,
to their friends.

02:17:49.580 --> 02:17:53.780
And I'm sure some of
them are looking forward

02:17:53.780 --> 02:17:56.809
to afterwards to
show their diploma

02:17:56.809 --> 02:18:01.879
and to show their families
their hard-earned degree.

02:18:01.879 --> 02:18:04.340
MCGANN: Now, the senior class
has had quite the busy week.

02:18:04.340 --> 02:18:07.070
This past week has been
what they call Senior Week.

02:18:07.070 --> 02:18:10.469
So they began last Friday with
events designed for the class

02:18:10.469 --> 02:18:12.110
to spend some
quality time together

02:18:12.110 --> 02:18:14.332
before heading to the
four corners of the world.

02:18:14.332 --> 02:18:16.040
So on Friday, there
was little fancy wine

02:18:16.040 --> 02:18:18.320
tasting event in
MIT'S Faculty Club

02:18:18.320 --> 02:18:20.150
overlooking the Charles River.

02:18:20.150 --> 02:18:22.459
On Saturday, the seniors
went down to Cape Cod

02:18:22.459 --> 02:18:23.750
and took a senior dinner--

02:18:23.750 --> 02:18:27.191
scenic dinner train ride,
featuring a five-course gourmet

02:18:27.191 --> 02:18:27.690
meal.

02:18:27.690 --> 02:18:29.150
That sounds pretty nice.

02:18:29.150 --> 02:18:31.760
Sunday night, they had outdoor
movies on Kresge Oval--

02:18:31.760 --> 02:18:34.490
Casino Royale, an old school
with a short intermission

02:18:34.490 --> 02:18:37.160
show by classmate,
future graduate

02:18:37.160 --> 02:18:40.975
here in just a little
while, David Rush, juggler.

02:18:40.975 --> 02:18:43.350
On Monday, they went out to
the Six Flags amusement park.

02:18:43.350 --> 02:18:46.040
Tuesday, they went for a
cookout on Kresge Oval.

02:18:46.040 --> 02:18:48.559
Wednesday night-- or
Wednesday for lunch,

02:18:48.559 --> 02:18:51.049
they had a lunch cruise
in Boston Harbor.

02:18:51.049 --> 02:18:54.209
And last night was a grand
event for all MIT alumni,

02:18:54.209 --> 02:18:57.695
including the Class of 2007,
the future alumni, the Tech

02:18:57.695 --> 02:19:01.280
Night at the Pops, an
annual MIT-only performance

02:19:01.280 --> 02:19:05.870
at Symphony Hall, which is
just really an amazing night

02:19:05.870 --> 02:19:09.984
to see all of the MIT alums
there filling some Symphony

02:19:09.984 --> 02:19:11.900
Hall, seeing one of the
most famous orchestras

02:19:11.900 --> 02:19:13.760
in the world, the Boston Pops.

02:19:13.760 --> 02:19:16.879
VIELMA: And yesterday,
we also had the--

02:19:16.879 --> 02:19:19.860
MCGANN: Donna Coveney,
the MIT photographer

02:19:19.860 --> 02:19:22.200
for many years with
beautiful shots.

02:19:22.200 --> 02:19:27.450
She is up on the Dome, looking
down at this beautiful scene

02:19:27.450 --> 02:19:29.709
today on Killian Court.

02:19:29.709 --> 02:19:31.920
And I'm sure that
we will look forward

02:19:31.920 --> 02:19:34.040
to seeing Donna's photos--

02:19:34.040 --> 02:19:38.650
Donna Coveney's photos a little
bit after the ceremonies today.

02:19:38.650 --> 02:19:41.490
Adele Santos there,
the Dean of--

02:19:41.490 --> 02:19:43.290
the Dean of Architecture
and Planning.

02:19:43.290 --> 02:19:45.120
Ike Colbert, who
will be leaving,

02:19:45.120 --> 02:19:48.270
departing this year, as the
Dean for Graduate Students.

02:19:48.270 --> 02:19:51.390
Paul Lagace on the left,
one of the marshals,

02:19:51.390 --> 02:19:55.724
a faculty in aeronautics
and astronautics.

02:19:55.724 --> 02:19:59.250
Many, many folks up
on the stage today.

02:19:59.250 --> 02:20:00.540
Everyone turns out.

02:20:00.540 --> 02:20:03.914
You don't want to miss
commencement at MIT.

02:20:03.914 --> 02:20:05.330
VIELMA: And
yesterday, we also had

02:20:05.330 --> 02:20:12.470
the hooding ceremony for the
candidates for PhD and ScD.

02:20:12.470 --> 02:20:13.890
They received their
academic hoods

02:20:13.890 --> 02:20:15.910
in a ceremony that was
performed yesterday.

02:20:15.910 --> 02:20:19.410
So the celebrations have been
going on for the entire week

02:20:19.410 --> 02:20:21.320
for our community.

02:20:21.320 --> 02:20:27.300
And the doctoral students,
they received their attire

02:20:27.300 --> 02:20:28.240
yesterday.

02:20:28.240 --> 02:20:33.330
So it was great to see some
of them walking around campus.

02:20:33.330 --> 02:20:36.750
MCGANN: So as we see these
graduates getting ready, taking

02:20:36.750 --> 02:20:38.250
photos with their cell phones.

02:20:38.250 --> 02:20:43.470
This is 2007 now, the
technology of the day.

02:20:43.470 --> 02:20:46.467
Everyone smiling, happy,
taking their photos.

02:20:46.467 --> 02:20:48.300
VIELMA: And I'm sure
they're looking forward

02:20:48.300 --> 02:20:54.280
to turning over their Brass Rat
that they've worn facing them,

02:20:54.280 --> 02:20:54.780
correct?

02:20:54.780 --> 02:20:55.400
Now, it's going
to face the world.

02:20:55.400 --> 02:20:56.520
MCGANN: That's right.

02:20:56.520 --> 02:21:00.150
At many institutions, the
big deal on commencement

02:21:00.150 --> 02:21:03.120
is to turn the
tassel on your cap.

02:21:03.120 --> 02:21:06.450
But at MIT, we have
another tradition

02:21:06.450 --> 02:21:08.880
which is more important
to our graduates.

02:21:08.880 --> 02:21:11.310
And that is to take
our class ring, which

02:21:11.310 --> 02:21:12.980
is called the Brass Rat.

02:21:12.980 --> 02:21:16.004
And it's called that
because it's made of gold.

02:21:16.004 --> 02:21:16.920
It has a beaver on it.

02:21:16.920 --> 02:21:18.150
I don't know.

02:21:18.150 --> 02:21:25.170
But since the-- 1930, we
have had an official class

02:21:25.170 --> 02:21:27.660
ring designed by the
students in the class.

02:21:27.660 --> 02:21:31.560
So everyone in-- every year's
ring is unique and different.

02:21:31.560 --> 02:21:35.470
Of course, Karina, the Class of
2000's Brass Rat was the best.

02:21:35.470 --> 02:21:36.429
We all understand that.

02:21:36.429 --> 02:21:37.511
VIELMA: No, no, no, Matt--

02:21:37.511 --> 02:21:38.220
2001.

02:21:38.220 --> 02:21:40.636
MCGANN: But you will see these
students a little bit later

02:21:40.636 --> 02:21:43.230
on during the address by Susan
Shin, the class president

02:21:43.230 --> 02:21:45.240
for the Class of 2007.

02:21:45.240 --> 02:21:49.080
She will instruct them to
turn their beavers around,

02:21:49.080 --> 02:21:50.670
turn that ring around.

02:21:50.670 --> 02:21:53.070
And you will see
the Class of 2007

02:21:53.070 --> 02:21:54.760
get very excited at that point.

02:21:54.760 --> 02:21:56.259
And that is one of
the things that I

02:21:56.259 --> 02:21:57.720
do remember about
my commencement

02:21:57.720 --> 02:21:59.700
was turning around my Brass Rat.

02:21:59.700 --> 02:22:02.040
And that is when you
see someone from MIT,

02:22:02.040 --> 02:22:04.800
you can look at their
finger with their Brass

02:22:04.800 --> 02:22:06.630
Rat, a very popular ring.

02:22:06.630 --> 02:22:08.597
And if the beaver is
facing out towards you,

02:22:08.597 --> 02:22:09.930
you know that they've graduated.

02:22:09.930 --> 02:22:11.540
And if it faces inward,
you know that they're

02:22:11.540 --> 02:22:12.540
an upperclassmen at MIT.

02:22:12.540 --> 02:22:13.361
VIELMA: Correct.

02:22:13.361 --> 02:22:13.860
Correct.

02:22:13.860 --> 02:22:15.660
And the ring is
designed by a ring

02:22:15.660 --> 02:22:22.050
committee called Ring Com, which
is a group of about 12 people.

02:22:22.050 --> 02:22:24.635
And they come up with a design.

02:22:24.635 --> 02:22:29.290
My class actually had
very unique details on it,

02:22:29.290 --> 02:22:32.680
as this class also
has, Class of 2007.

02:22:32.680 --> 02:22:37.060
So they'll be looking forward
to turning theirs over.

02:22:37.060 --> 02:22:43.030
Again, we also have the doctoral
students also have a Grad Rat.

02:22:43.030 --> 02:22:45.840
MCGANN: Yes, the Grad Rat.

02:22:45.840 --> 02:22:49.770
So the Brass Rat started as
an undergraduate tradition.

02:22:49.770 --> 02:22:53.200
But graduate students are a big
part of this community as well,

02:22:53.200 --> 02:22:53.954
an equal part.

02:22:53.954 --> 02:22:56.370
So they said, we need what
we're going to call a Grad Rat.

02:22:56.370 --> 02:22:59.020
And every ring has
something unique on it.

02:22:59.020 --> 02:23:01.690
My ring has unique things
to my particular class here.

02:23:01.690 --> 02:23:04.390
And the Grad Rat has,
among other things,

02:23:04.390 --> 02:23:07.480
two neat little things
hidden within the beaver

02:23:07.480 --> 02:23:08.440
and the design.

02:23:08.440 --> 02:23:13.480
There's a pizza, which is
supposed to represent the food,

02:23:13.480 --> 02:23:15.130
the free food that
is so important

02:23:15.130 --> 02:23:17.260
to the graduate students
during their time,

02:23:17.260 --> 02:23:19.120
and also a handless clock.

02:23:19.120 --> 02:23:22.720
The clock represents-- the
clock without hands represents,

02:23:22.720 --> 02:23:25.840
what they call, our lack
of fixed time to graduation

02:23:25.840 --> 02:23:27.730
and the fact that there
is never enough time

02:23:27.730 --> 02:23:30.240
to do all the things
we need while at MIT.

02:23:30.240 --> 02:23:32.980
VIELMA: And there they are, some
of the doctoral students who

02:23:32.980 --> 02:23:35.590
are looking at their
commencement folders

02:23:35.590 --> 02:23:38.096
and looking onward.

02:23:38.096 --> 02:23:39.970
MCGANN: So you can see
that they are wearing,

02:23:39.970 --> 02:23:41.710
many of them already,
their doctoral hoods

02:23:41.710 --> 02:23:42.918
that they received yesterday.

02:23:42.918 --> 02:23:49.672
That's the blue hoods around
them, which is lined with red--

02:23:49.672 --> 02:23:51.130
Cardinal Red and
Silver Gray, which

02:23:51.130 --> 02:23:53.180
are the official MIT colors--

02:23:53.180 --> 02:23:56.620
and also the nifty little
tam, the hat that they wear,

02:23:56.620 --> 02:23:59.510
is also an official part
of the doctoral garb.

02:24:03.920 --> 02:24:05.880
VIELMA: And again,
our [AUDIO OUT]

02:24:05.880 --> 02:24:09.350
our stage of
faculty there, ready

02:24:09.350 --> 02:24:10.601
to receive the graduates also.

02:24:10.601 --> 02:24:12.016
MCGANN: And it's
a wonderful thing

02:24:12.016 --> 02:24:13.790
that at MIT, all
of the classes are

02:24:13.790 --> 02:24:16.370
taught by the faculty members.

02:24:16.370 --> 02:24:20.390
But when the students came
here to MIT that they actually

02:24:20.390 --> 02:24:25.430
were able have classes with
these amazing faculty members,

02:24:25.430 --> 02:24:26.330
Nobel Laureates.

02:24:26.330 --> 02:24:29.480
I had, for freshman physics,
a Nobel Laureate, which

02:24:29.480 --> 02:24:31.670
was an amazing experience.

02:24:31.670 --> 02:24:33.800
For freshman biology,
I had Eric Lander,

02:24:33.800 --> 02:24:37.070
the head of the largest center
for the Human Genome Project.

02:24:37.070 --> 02:24:41.090
These famous faculty really
do teach the students at MIT.

02:24:41.090 --> 02:24:43.464
Did you have a favorite faculty
member or a most notable

02:24:43.464 --> 02:24:45.380
faculty member that you
had during your career

02:24:45.380 --> 02:24:45.921
here, Karina?

02:24:45.921 --> 02:24:46.430
VIELMA: Yes.

02:24:46.430 --> 02:24:48.944
My favorite was
Professor Michael Sipser.

02:24:48.944 --> 02:24:53.870
He was my adviser also and very,
very hopeful faculty member.

02:24:53.870 --> 02:24:58.010
And I am very, very grateful
for all the faculty.

02:24:58.010 --> 02:25:00.410
They, in each their
own way, taught

02:25:00.410 --> 02:25:03.790
me different things that have
helped me throughout my life

02:25:03.790 --> 02:25:04.540
after--

02:25:04.540 --> 02:25:05.620
at MIT and after.

02:25:05.620 --> 02:25:08.170
So I'm sure that
these faculty up here

02:25:08.170 --> 02:25:10.454
know that the students
appreciate them.

02:25:10.454 --> 02:25:12.870
And that's part of the reason
why they're here joining us.

02:25:12.870 --> 02:25:16.310
So it's great to see
them all up there.

02:25:16.310 --> 02:25:20.620
We also saw earlier Chancellor
Phil Clay sitting up there

02:25:20.620 --> 02:25:25.186
on the stage, and our provost,
Rafael Reif who is also

02:25:25.186 --> 02:25:29.860
sitting next to Charles Vest.

02:25:29.860 --> 02:25:32.290
And so it's great to
have all these people who

02:25:32.290 --> 02:25:34.440
have an amazing--

02:25:34.440 --> 02:25:36.870
there we see
Chancellor Phil Clay.

02:25:36.870 --> 02:25:39.100
And it's just
wonderful to see them

02:25:39.100 --> 02:25:42.530
be a part of this
community in so many ways.

02:25:42.530 --> 02:25:46.320
Chancellor Clay visit
our students very often

02:25:46.320 --> 02:25:48.950
and has discussions with them.

02:25:48.950 --> 02:25:51.550
He's a wonderful
senior administrator

02:25:51.550 --> 02:25:52.300
to have on campus.

02:25:52.300 --> 02:25:53.967
MCGANN: And sitting
next to him was

02:25:53.967 --> 02:25:56.300
Steve [? Lerman, ?] professor
of civil and environmental

02:25:56.300 --> 02:25:58.790
engineering, who's
serving as the chair

02:25:58.790 --> 02:26:00.165
of the faculty at this moment.

02:26:00.165 --> 02:26:01.040
VIELMA: That's right.

02:26:01.040 --> 02:26:03.620
And I think his term
is just about ending.

02:26:03.620 --> 02:26:07.009
So he'll be ready to
hand over his term.

02:26:07.009 --> 02:26:07.550
MCGANN: Yeah.

02:26:07.550 --> 02:26:10.000
He-- a former chair of the
Faculty [? Valiant ?] when

02:26:10.000 --> 02:26:13.780
he came in as Lorna Gibson,
who was chair of the faculty,

02:26:13.780 --> 02:26:15.970
received a promotion
to associate provost.

02:26:15.970 --> 02:26:19.060
And Lorna Gibson is, again,
one of the beloved faculty

02:26:19.060 --> 02:26:19.830
members here.

02:26:19.830 --> 02:26:20.776
VIELMA: Oh, nice.

02:26:20.776 --> 02:26:23.360
MCGANN: So the graduates
continue to come in.

02:26:23.360 --> 02:26:24.650
They just keep coming.

02:26:24.650 --> 02:26:28.400
More than 2,000
today will graduate.

02:26:28.400 --> 02:26:30.430
Just an amazing group.

02:26:30.430 --> 02:26:33.920
And you see the
marshals continuing

02:26:33.920 --> 02:26:37.570
to stand as the
graduates come in.

02:26:37.570 --> 02:26:39.440
And I don't see an
end to this, Karina.

02:26:39.440 --> 02:26:40.030
Do you?

02:26:40.030 --> 02:26:40.940
VIELMA: No.

02:26:40.940 --> 02:26:43.310
And it's amazing that
those black chairs

02:26:43.310 --> 02:26:46.642
were previously unfilled.

02:26:46.642 --> 02:26:49.052
And now, they are
just filling up.

02:26:49.052 --> 02:26:52.920
And we see here that it's--

02:26:52.920 --> 02:26:54.010
they're almost all full.

02:26:54.010 --> 02:26:57.735
But it seems like we have
more students coming in, so.

02:27:03.080 --> 02:27:05.900
MCGANN: So here, again, we
have a shot of the stage,

02:27:05.900 --> 02:27:09.130
as we have many of
the noted faculty

02:27:09.130 --> 02:27:12.730
members standing and waiting.

02:27:12.730 --> 02:27:15.130
You see Professor
David Newman is just

02:27:15.130 --> 02:27:17.350
coming off the
screen to the right,

02:27:17.350 --> 02:27:18.850
the Housemaster of Baker House.

02:27:18.850 --> 02:27:21.640
We have live-in
faculty members in all

02:27:21.640 --> 02:27:24.430
of our undergraduate residences.

02:27:28.840 --> 02:27:30.950
And we see more and
more of the faculty.

02:27:30.950 --> 02:27:34.850
And they're looking, seeing
what the program is today,

02:27:34.850 --> 02:27:37.850
and looking for some of
the names of the students

02:27:37.850 --> 02:27:39.290
that they know,
that they taught,

02:27:39.290 --> 02:27:41.570
that they mentored
during their years--

02:27:41.570 --> 02:27:43.100
looking through
the program, seeing

02:27:43.100 --> 02:27:44.910
who is going to be there.

02:27:44.910 --> 02:27:47.600
And this is the view that
they have from the stage

02:27:47.600 --> 02:27:48.560
looking out on that--

02:27:48.560 --> 02:27:50.680
VIELMA: What a wonderful
view, isn't it, Matt?

02:27:50.680 --> 02:27:52.250
MCGANN: Oh, man.

02:27:52.250 --> 02:27:53.210
VIELMA: It's amazing.

02:27:53.210 --> 02:27:54.668
MCGANN: As the
graduating students,

02:27:54.668 --> 02:27:56.690
we only see this
view very briefly.

02:27:56.690 --> 02:27:59.180
We walk up the stage,
try not to trip

02:27:59.180 --> 02:28:02.270
and embarrass ourselves,
shake the hand

02:28:02.270 --> 02:28:05.750
of a senior administrator
at MIT, receive our diploma,

02:28:05.750 --> 02:28:08.150
and then walk down those ramps.

02:28:08.150 --> 02:28:10.160
And that is one of the
most joyous moments.

02:28:10.160 --> 02:28:13.580
And you'll see that moment
for thousands of our students

02:28:13.580 --> 02:28:15.791
quite shortly here.

02:28:15.791 --> 02:28:16.290
VIELMA: Yes.

02:28:16.290 --> 02:28:19.640
And that view is
amazing, especially given

02:28:19.640 --> 02:28:21.900
this beautiful day that we have.

02:28:21.900 --> 02:28:25.640
It's also covered so that
they get lots of shade.

02:28:25.640 --> 02:28:28.720
And the graduates
will, of course--

02:28:28.720 --> 02:28:31.010
as we saw, some of
them be wearing shades.

02:28:31.010 --> 02:28:33.400
And of course, they
have their caps.

02:28:33.400 --> 02:28:38.660
So they'll-- they're
going to celebrate--

02:28:38.660 --> 02:28:40.370
MCGANN: Ah, another
shot of the mace.

02:28:40.370 --> 02:28:43.350
VIELMA: This is the mace again.

02:28:43.350 --> 02:28:46.850
And one thing I didn't
mention, Matt, about the mace

02:28:46.850 --> 02:28:52.340
is that the mace also has
symbols, symbols of physics,

02:28:52.340 --> 02:28:56.720
math, humanities, biology,
mechanics, electromagnetism,

02:28:56.720 --> 02:29:01.130
chemistry, civil engineering,
earth sciences, naval science,

02:29:01.130 --> 02:29:04.820
architecture, aeronautics,
all of these symbols

02:29:04.820 --> 02:29:08.300
that represent courses
of MIT instruction

02:29:08.300 --> 02:29:12.620
but are also an expression
of the technological culture

02:29:12.620 --> 02:29:13.940
here at MIT.

02:29:13.940 --> 02:29:16.582
And these have
remained unchanged

02:29:16.582 --> 02:29:17.790
throughout many of the years.

02:29:17.790 --> 02:29:20.280
There, we see the beaver on top.

02:29:20.280 --> 02:29:21.360
MCGANN: So the beaver--

02:29:21.360 --> 02:29:23.820
this is an interesting story
how it became our mascot.

02:29:23.820 --> 02:29:27.510
In 1914, Lester Gardner, of
the MIT Club of New York,

02:29:27.510 --> 02:29:31.320
proposed a mascot to then MIT
President Richard Maclaurin.

02:29:31.320 --> 02:29:33.420
And Gardner says,
"We first thought

02:29:33.420 --> 02:29:36.840
of the kangaroo, which,
like MIT, goes forward

02:29:36.840 --> 02:29:38.340
by leaps and bounds.

02:29:38.340 --> 02:29:40.030
Then, we considered
the elephant.

02:29:40.030 --> 02:29:43.860
He is wise, patient, strong,
hardworking, and like all those

02:29:43.860 --> 02:29:47.430
who graduate from MIT,
has a good tough hide.

02:29:47.430 --> 02:29:49.980
But neither of these
were American animals.

02:29:49.980 --> 02:29:52.610
We turned to William
Temple Hornady's book

02:29:52.610 --> 02:29:57.870
on the animals of North America
and instantly chose the beaver.

02:29:57.870 --> 02:30:00.780
The beaver not only
typifies the MIT student,

02:30:00.780 --> 02:30:03.780
but also, his habits are
particularly our own.

02:30:03.780 --> 02:30:06.570
The beaver is noted for his
engineering, mechanical skills,

02:30:06.570 --> 02:30:07.650
and industry.

02:30:07.650 --> 02:30:09.390
His habits are nocturnal.

02:30:09.390 --> 02:30:11.890
He does his best
work in the dark."

02:30:11.890 --> 02:30:14.250
VIELMA: And that's what a
lot of students here do.

02:30:14.250 --> 02:30:18.240
I remember late,
late nights here

02:30:18.240 --> 02:30:20.550
at MIT working
through problem sets.

02:30:20.550 --> 02:30:24.720
And that beaver really
does show the type of work

02:30:24.720 --> 02:30:28.140
that MIT students do--
best at night and all of it

02:30:28.140 --> 02:30:31.210
is, mainly, engineering,
for the most part.

02:30:31.210 --> 02:30:36.480
MCGANN: Engineering, science,
architecture, management,

02:30:36.480 --> 02:30:38.880
humanities, arts, social
sciences-- all of which

02:30:38.880 --> 02:30:42.810
are done here at MIT--
certainly exemplifies--

02:30:42.810 --> 02:30:44.580
that beaver exemplifies
our motto, which

02:30:44.580 --> 02:30:45.750
is, "Mens et manus."

02:30:45.750 --> 02:30:46.090
VIELMA: Mens et manus.

02:30:46.090 --> 02:30:47.100
MCGANN: Mind in hand.

02:30:47.100 --> 02:30:47.620
VIELMA: Yes.

02:30:47.620 --> 02:30:51.420
MCGANN: So here at MIT, we
both have the theoretical work

02:30:51.420 --> 02:30:53.130
and the hands-on work.

02:30:53.130 --> 02:30:55.770
And when you make
that dam as a beaver,

02:30:55.770 --> 02:30:58.920
you have to do some
theoretical planning and then

02:30:58.920 --> 02:31:00.925
the hands-on actual
building of the dam.

02:31:00.925 --> 02:31:02.550
And there's the
beautiful flowers which

02:31:02.550 --> 02:31:05.100
will be on sale this afternoon.

02:31:05.100 --> 02:31:09.360
Funds going to the MIT
community service fund.

02:31:09.360 --> 02:31:13.170
And we're about to
see one of the--

02:31:13.170 --> 02:31:16.460
of my favorite groups on
campus, the MIT Corollaries,

02:31:16.460 --> 02:31:19.950
MIT'S oldest coed
acapella group.

02:31:19.950 --> 02:31:23.425
And they will be singing the
National Anthem very soon here.

02:31:23.425 --> 02:31:25.300
VIELMA: And they perform
throughout the year,

02:31:25.300 --> 02:31:28.560
is that correct, Matt,
on different occasions?

02:31:28.560 --> 02:31:31.510
MCGANN: So they will
perform the National Anthem.

02:31:31.510 --> 02:31:35.370
And then later on, at the
conclusion of the ceremony,

02:31:35.370 --> 02:31:40.380
they will sing the MIT
school song in praise of MIT,

02:31:40.380 --> 02:31:42.190
which begins-- you
may know the song--

02:31:42.190 --> 02:31:43.725
"Arise all ye of MIT."

02:31:46.820 --> 02:31:49.520
VIELMA: There's one senior
there with his attire.

02:31:49.520 --> 02:31:53.690
MCGANN: So these seniors will
be singing with The Corollaries.

02:31:53.690 --> 02:31:56.480
And then, they will go and
actually receive their degrees.

02:31:56.480 --> 02:31:59.840
And this has been worked out
with associate-- head associate

02:31:59.840 --> 02:32:03.410
marshal, Dean Julie
Norman, who we saw earlier.

02:32:03.410 --> 02:32:07.280
They have this really down to
a science how The Corollaries

02:32:07.280 --> 02:32:09.780
will enter the line once again.

02:32:09.780 --> 02:32:14.780
It really is a logistical
achievement the way

02:32:14.780 --> 02:32:17.000
that they're able to give
everyone their own degrees.

02:32:17.000 --> 02:32:20.130
This does not happen
at every institution.

02:32:20.130 --> 02:32:23.690
It is a very special
thing that happens at MIT.

02:32:23.690 --> 02:32:27.110
VIELMA: The students who will
be singing in the acapella group

02:32:27.110 --> 02:32:29.929
will then come down,
have their seats,

02:32:29.929 --> 02:32:31.220
and then receive their diploma.

02:32:31.220 --> 02:32:32.760
Is that correct?

02:32:32.760 --> 02:32:35.210
MCGANN: I believe they
will enter the line

02:32:35.210 --> 02:32:36.350
at an appropriate time.

02:32:36.350 --> 02:32:39.140
So I don't think
they will take seats

02:32:39.140 --> 02:32:41.790
within the masses
of the graduates,

02:32:41.790 --> 02:32:43.540
but we'll watch that
during the ceremony.

02:32:43.540 --> 02:32:45.550
VIELMA: And I believe
we're almost ready.

02:32:45.550 --> 02:32:48.390
We have some of the last
graduates coming in.

02:32:48.390 --> 02:32:52.720
MCGANN: So what we'll
see, what to expect--

02:32:52.720 --> 02:32:56.500
here's Paul Gray, we've talked
about a number of times today,

02:32:56.500 --> 02:32:58.300
who really personifies MIT.

02:32:58.300 --> 02:33:01.060
He and his wife Priscilla
are just a wonderful member

02:33:01.060 --> 02:33:02.194
of this community.

02:33:02.194 --> 02:33:03.610
So today, the
opening remarks will

02:33:03.610 --> 02:33:04.984
be given to the
gentleman you see

02:33:04.984 --> 02:33:08.670
there is Dana Mead, the
Chairman of the MIT Corporation.

02:33:08.670 --> 02:33:10.930
That'll be followed by
an invocation that's

02:33:10.930 --> 02:33:12.990
given by a different
chaplain each year.

02:33:12.990 --> 02:33:15.730
And that'll be done
by Reverend Johanna

02:33:15.730 --> 02:33:20.197
Kiefner of the MIT Lutherans,
the MIT Lutheran chaplain.

02:33:20.197 --> 02:33:22.780
That will be followed by the MIT
Corollaries, who we were just

02:33:22.780 --> 02:33:23.290
looking at.

02:33:23.290 --> 02:33:25.000
They'll sing the
National Anthem.

02:33:25.000 --> 02:33:29.662
Then, we'll see the commencement
address, Dr. Charles Vest,

02:33:29.662 --> 02:33:31.120
professor of
mechanical engineering

02:33:31.120 --> 02:33:33.730
and President
Emeritus; have a salute

02:33:33.730 --> 02:33:36.100
from the graduate
students by Eric Weese,

02:33:36.100 --> 02:33:38.200
president of the
Graduate Student Council;

02:33:38.200 --> 02:33:41.350
a salute and presentation of
the class gift by Susan Shin,

02:33:41.350 --> 02:33:43.690
the president of
the Class of 2007;

02:33:43.690 --> 02:33:47.020
the charge to the graduates
by President Susan Hockfield;

02:33:47.020 --> 02:33:49.960
and then, the moment we
really have been waiting for,

02:33:49.960 --> 02:33:51.700
the presentation of degrees--

02:33:51.700 --> 02:33:55.900
as we look back over panning
of the MIT faculty who

02:33:55.900 --> 02:33:58.060
are up on stage today,
a number of very

02:33:58.060 --> 02:34:01.330
noted faculty and just some
really wonderful mentors

02:34:01.330 --> 02:34:04.210
that we can see in the shot now.

02:34:04.210 --> 02:34:06.670
VIELMA: And the
degrees are presented

02:34:06.670 --> 02:34:09.580
in order of bachelor of science,
then bachelor of science,

02:34:09.580 --> 02:34:12.730
master of science;
and master of science,

02:34:12.730 --> 02:34:16.990
master of engineering degrees;
and then advanced degrees.

02:34:16.990 --> 02:34:21.320
So it really is a
science up here--

02:34:21.320 --> 02:34:25.980
down to a science that
they have it organized.

02:34:25.980 --> 02:34:28.230
MCGANN: The first
graduates today will be--

02:34:28.230 --> 02:34:30.360
well, they'll be the
students who are on stage.

02:34:30.360 --> 02:34:33.170
Those students will receive
their degrees first.

02:34:33.170 --> 02:34:35.460
And then, it will
start with graduates

02:34:35.460 --> 02:34:38.490
from the School of
Architecture and Planning,

02:34:38.490 --> 02:34:41.310
the bachelor of science
in art and design.

02:34:41.310 --> 02:34:43.350
We call architecture
Course 4 here.

02:34:43.350 --> 02:34:46.530
Architecture at MIT-- this
was actually the first place

02:34:46.530 --> 02:34:48.390
in the United States
where you could receive

02:34:48.390 --> 02:34:50.580
a degree in architecture.

02:34:50.580 --> 02:34:52.440
And we still give
those degrees today.

02:34:52.440 --> 02:34:56.970
Many amazing architecture
graduates over the years from

02:34:56.970 --> 02:35:00.699
MIT, most notably,
probably, I.M. Pei,

02:35:00.699 --> 02:35:02.740
who designed a number of
buildings on our campus,

02:35:02.740 --> 02:35:05.490
including the media lab,
also the pyramid in front

02:35:05.490 --> 02:35:08.740
of the Louvre, the Rock 'n'
Roll Hall of Fame in Cleveland,

02:35:08.740 --> 02:35:13.010
Ohio; the Bank of China
building in Hong Kong--

02:35:13.010 --> 02:35:14.620
and a number of other folks.

02:35:14.620 --> 02:35:17.220
So there we see Martin
Tang, again, in the center.

02:35:17.220 --> 02:35:20.955
To his right, the Dean for
Student Life, Larry Benedict,

02:35:20.955 --> 02:35:22.860
and to his left--

02:35:22.860 --> 02:35:26.010
left on the screen,
anyway, Dan Hastings,

02:35:26.010 --> 02:35:29.160
the Dean for
Undergraduate Education,

02:35:29.160 --> 02:35:33.498
also a professor in
aeronautics and astronautics.

02:35:33.498 --> 02:35:35.520
So everyone's just
about ready here.

02:35:35.520 --> 02:35:38.130
You can see some
cameras ready to film.

02:35:38.130 --> 02:35:42.030
You can see the faculty
eagerly awaiting

02:35:42.030 --> 02:35:43.814
the ceremonies to begin.

02:35:43.814 --> 02:35:45.730
We're just waiting for
some of the last folks.

02:35:45.730 --> 02:35:49.020
There is Les Norford,
associate marshal

02:35:49.020 --> 02:35:50.940
from the Department
of Architecture,

02:35:50.940 --> 02:35:53.250
checking out and seeing
how we're looking.

02:35:53.250 --> 02:35:54.630
The graduates still standing.

02:35:57.650 --> 02:36:00.350
VIELMA: My sister's there
in one of the front lines.

02:36:00.350 --> 02:36:03.140
MCGANN: Lucia Vielma
near the front

02:36:03.140 --> 02:36:06.710
to receive the-- after the
Department of Architecture

02:36:06.710 --> 02:36:09.896
will be the Department of
Urban Studies and Planning

02:36:09.896 --> 02:36:11.270
which your sister
is a member of.

02:36:11.270 --> 02:36:11.780
VIELMA: Correct.

02:36:11.780 --> 02:36:13.821
And then we have the School
of Engineering, which

02:36:13.821 --> 02:36:16.634
is the largest department here.

02:36:16.634 --> 02:36:18.050
MCGANN: About half
of our students

02:36:18.050 --> 02:36:20.216
here-- a little bit more
than half of our students--

02:36:20.216 --> 02:36:22.760
will receive their
primary degree in one

02:36:22.760 --> 02:36:24.920
of the engineering fields.

02:36:24.920 --> 02:36:26.660
At MIT, of course,
so much more--

02:36:26.660 --> 02:36:30.770
your sister in city planning,
and wonderful departments

02:36:30.770 --> 02:36:34.730
in biology, chemistry,
physics, brain and cognitive

02:36:34.730 --> 02:36:36.320
science, economics.

02:36:36.320 --> 02:36:37.995
Last year's speaker,
Ben Bernanke,

02:36:37.995 --> 02:36:39.620
was our commencement
speaker last year,

02:36:39.620 --> 02:36:41.240
an MIT graduate in economics.

02:36:41.240 --> 02:36:44.774
He's now chairman of
the Federal Reserve.

02:36:44.774 --> 02:36:46.190
We also have
wonderful departments

02:36:46.190 --> 02:36:50.780
here in linguistics,
political science, management,

02:36:50.780 --> 02:36:54.860
management science, and just
too many fields to name.

02:36:54.860 --> 02:36:58.704
So I apologize to those
fields who I have not named.

02:36:58.704 --> 02:37:00.620
VIELMA: Well, after the
School of Engineering,

02:37:00.620 --> 02:37:02.828
we'll have the School of
Humanities, Arts, and Social

02:37:02.828 --> 02:37:06.320
Sciences, followed by the
Sloan School of Management

02:37:06.320 --> 02:37:10.190
and the School of Science
receiving their degrees.

02:37:10.190 --> 02:37:12.950
So they'll be really
looking forward

02:37:12.950 --> 02:37:18.680
to receiving their Bachelor's
today here from MIT.

02:37:18.680 --> 02:37:21.020
And you know, I'm
wondering, where

02:37:21.020 --> 02:37:23.000
are all these people going?

02:37:23.000 --> 02:37:27.710
It's amazing, so
many of our students

02:37:27.710 --> 02:37:32.600
make wonderful, wonderful
changes around the world.

02:37:32.600 --> 02:37:36.860
And we hear about it through the
MIT Alumni Association, which

02:37:36.860 --> 02:37:38.990
I'm a part of, and of
course you're a part of,

02:37:38.990 --> 02:37:41.930
and every MIT alum is a part of.

02:37:41.930 --> 02:37:44.630
So it's great to
hear these news.

02:37:44.630 --> 02:37:48.830
And of course this is
a celebratory moment,

02:37:48.830 --> 02:37:52.820
because as we see the graduates
receiving their degree,

02:37:52.820 --> 02:37:55.250
then they're put off
like a little seed

02:37:55.250 --> 02:37:56.930
out into the world
where they'll plant

02:37:56.930 --> 02:38:00.830
their own change in
the different fields

02:38:00.830 --> 02:38:02.990
that they'll be in.

02:38:02.990 --> 02:38:06.440
MCGANN: Of the undergraduates
who graduate, usually about 70%

02:38:06.440 --> 02:38:08.690
of them will go on to
receive advanced degrees

02:38:08.690 --> 02:38:11.940
beyond their Bachelor's
degree today.

02:38:11.940 --> 02:38:15.410
Many of them will stay and
receive advanced degrees

02:38:15.410 --> 02:38:16.370
here at MIT.

02:38:16.370 --> 02:38:18.680
Others will go off
and receive degrees

02:38:18.680 --> 02:38:22.250
in medicine law, research,
science, and engineering,

02:38:22.250 --> 02:38:24.800
and many, many other fields.

02:38:24.800 --> 02:38:27.560
There's Ken Reeves again on the
left, the mayor of Cambridge.

02:38:27.560 --> 02:38:31.940
Paul Gray on the right,
former president of MIT.

02:38:31.940 --> 02:38:35.577
And Dana Mead, you can see
is looking at his remarks,

02:38:35.577 --> 02:38:36.660
that he is about to begin.

02:38:36.660 --> 02:38:38.930
He will be the first
to speak today.

02:38:38.930 --> 02:38:40.360
He will welcome us.

02:38:40.360 --> 02:38:44.550
And it looks like we're just
about ready for that moment.

02:38:44.550 --> 02:38:47.570
We've got a few more
graduates coming in.

02:38:47.570 --> 02:38:52.820
The last to graduate today
will be those in mathematics.

02:38:52.820 --> 02:38:57.460
I believe they are last
in our order today.

02:38:57.460 --> 02:38:59.540
And these may be some
mathematics students

02:38:59.540 --> 02:39:01.370
that we're seeing now.

02:39:01.370 --> 02:39:03.980
Also you see a number
of students in uniform,

02:39:03.980 --> 02:39:05.510
commissioned into our military.

02:39:05.510 --> 02:39:09.380
They may be ROTC students,
the Reserve Officers Training

02:39:09.380 --> 02:39:11.735
Corps, or may be here
getting advanced degrees.

02:39:11.735 --> 02:39:13.280
VIELMA: And Julie Norman, also.

02:39:13.280 --> 02:39:16.500
We see her at the
end of the line.

02:39:16.500 --> 02:39:19.550
Not at the very end, but
almost at the very end.

02:39:19.550 --> 02:39:22.350
And she has just
done an amazing job

02:39:22.350 --> 02:39:26.930
coordinating all these
helpers, commencement staff.

02:39:26.930 --> 02:39:32.560
And of course she'll also be
joining the people up here

02:39:32.560 --> 02:39:35.010
on the stage.

02:39:35.010 --> 02:39:37.800
MCGANN: So we hear
applause starting

02:39:37.800 --> 02:39:40.200
to rise from the audience.

02:39:40.200 --> 02:39:45.210
I think this means that we
are set for our ceremonies

02:39:45.210 --> 02:39:47.940
to begin today.

02:39:47.940 --> 02:39:52.440
The marshals, the graduation
group of marshals,

02:39:52.440 --> 02:39:54.480
are ascending the stage.

02:39:54.480 --> 02:39:56.610
The time is upon us.

02:39:56.610 --> 02:39:59.400
So I hope that you
will enjoy the rest

02:39:59.400 --> 02:40:01.920
of the broadcast, the
webcast, or the DVD

02:40:01.920 --> 02:40:04.080
today as you watch it.

02:40:04.080 --> 02:40:08.460
It will be an amazing
and just really very

02:40:08.460 --> 02:40:12.478
special ceremony that
you're about to watch.

02:40:12.478 --> 02:40:15.831
And I'm going to go and
watch it a little bit myself.

02:40:15.831 --> 02:40:18.230
VIELMA: I also will sit
with my family, [? Matt, ?]

02:40:18.230 --> 02:40:22.550
and watch my
students, my sister,

02:40:22.550 --> 02:40:27.770
and of course future fellow
colleagues and fellow alumni

02:40:27.770 --> 02:40:28.970
receive their degrees.

02:40:28.970 --> 02:40:30.720
MCGANN: Do you think you
might shed a tear today?

02:40:30.720 --> 02:40:32.220
Do you think somebody
in your family

02:40:32.220 --> 02:40:34.209
might get a little weepy?

02:40:34.209 --> 02:40:35.000
VIELMA: I think so.

02:40:35.000 --> 02:40:35.630
I think so.

02:40:35.630 --> 02:40:39.103
This is really a moment
that we all wait for,

02:40:39.103 --> 02:40:40.530
is to receive our degrees.

02:40:40.530 --> 02:40:44.380
MCGANN: And we have a crescendo
here from the brass ensemble.

02:40:44.380 --> 02:40:47.700
I believe this will be the
conclusion of their playing

02:40:47.700 --> 02:40:48.858
for the procession.

02:40:48.858 --> 02:40:50.322
And the cheers.

02:41:00.570 --> 02:41:01.243
And Dana Mead.

02:41:01.243 --> 02:41:02.034
MEAD: Good morning.

02:41:04.970 --> 02:41:08.690
The Corporation and the faculty
of the Massachusetts Institute

02:41:08.690 --> 02:41:13.210
of Technology are now
declared convened together

02:41:13.210 --> 02:41:16.780
with this assembly on the
occasion of the commencement

02:41:16.780 --> 02:41:20.350
exercises of this institution
for the conferring

02:41:20.350 --> 02:41:22.510
of its degrees.

02:41:22.510 --> 02:41:24.720
The stage assembly
and audience will

02:41:24.720 --> 02:41:30.360
please rise for the invocation
by Reverend Johanna Kiefner,

02:41:30.360 --> 02:41:32.970
and remain standing
to join the MIT

02:41:32.970 --> 02:41:36.968
Chorallaries in the singing
of one verse of The Star

02:41:36.968 --> 02:41:38.944
Spangled Banner.

02:41:52.310 --> 02:41:53.850
KIEFNER: Okay.

02:41:53.850 --> 02:41:55.410
All right.

02:41:55.410 --> 02:41:59.420
On this day of
celebration and grandeur,

02:41:59.420 --> 02:42:02.310
in which we offer honor
and blessing to the life

02:42:02.310 --> 02:42:06.240
and promise present and
gathered in this place,

02:42:06.240 --> 02:42:08.880
to the one known by
many names and heard

02:42:08.880 --> 02:42:12.120
through the voices of
wonder and passion,

02:42:12.120 --> 02:42:15.750
through reason and order,
and in all that calls forth

02:42:15.750 --> 02:42:19.390
the best in who
we are and can be,

02:42:19.390 --> 02:42:23.200
we offer our thanks for this
day and invite your blessing

02:42:23.200 --> 02:42:25.870
upon these men and
women graduating

02:42:25.870 --> 02:42:30.140
from the Massachusetts
Institute of Technology.

02:42:30.140 --> 02:42:34.160
We are grateful for the
families, friends, professors,

02:42:34.160 --> 02:42:37.760
staff, and other mentors
whose companionship

02:42:37.760 --> 02:42:41.780
with these graduates has
made this day possible.

02:42:41.780 --> 02:42:46.010
May these guides continue to be
instilled with wisdom and joy

02:42:46.010 --> 02:42:49.520
in teaching, and may they
ever delight in the lives

02:42:49.520 --> 02:42:52.010
they touch.

02:42:52.010 --> 02:42:54.980
For those whom this
day marks a transition

02:42:54.980 --> 02:42:57.420
into a new chapter
of their lives,

02:42:57.420 --> 02:43:01.190
we invite a passion for
justice and a sense of clarity

02:43:01.190 --> 02:43:04.550
through which they may use
their gifts for the betterment

02:43:04.550 --> 02:43:06.580
of the world.

02:43:06.580 --> 02:43:09.550
We invite an eagerness
for learning and an ever

02:43:09.550 --> 02:43:12.220
deepening creativity,
that as they

02:43:12.220 --> 02:43:15.310
grow in years and
experience, there

02:43:15.310 --> 02:43:19.600
is a forever sense of wonder
in the marvels of this planet

02:43:19.600 --> 02:43:22.920
and the universe we inhabit.

02:43:22.920 --> 02:43:26.960
We invite a thirst for truth
and a discerning awareness,

02:43:26.960 --> 02:43:29.810
through the often
complicated and at times

02:43:29.810 --> 02:43:33.420
distressing ways of the world.

02:43:33.420 --> 02:43:37.220
And we wish for them
companions along their way.

02:43:37.220 --> 02:43:40.220
Colleagues as partners
in creativity,

02:43:40.220 --> 02:43:42.560
and to serve as
wise guides as they

02:43:42.560 --> 02:43:45.540
mature into their professions.

02:43:45.540 --> 02:43:48.840
We wish for them friends
to sustain them and laugh

02:43:48.840 --> 02:43:51.510
with them and to
be for them sources

02:43:51.510 --> 02:43:54.226
of strength and flexibility.

02:43:54.226 --> 02:43:58.830
And may they be surrounded by
those with deep love in order

02:43:58.830 --> 02:44:03.390
that they may know more
fully their giftedness.

02:44:03.390 --> 02:44:05.280
And may they go
forth into the world

02:44:05.280 --> 02:44:07.920
full of courage and eagerness.

02:44:07.920 --> 02:44:11.910
May their paths be well-lit
and their hearts open.

02:44:11.910 --> 02:44:16.365
And may their minds and
hands always be full of joy.

02:44:16.365 --> 02:44:16.865
Amen.

02:44:21.855 --> 02:44:26.346
[MUSIC PLAYING]

02:44:46.860 --> 02:44:50.540
MEAD: I am pleased to
welcome to the platform

02:44:50.540 --> 02:44:55.100
the honorable Kenneth E. Reeves,
mayor of the City of Cambridge.

02:44:55.100 --> 02:44:56.080
Welcome, Mayor.

02:45:04.410 --> 02:45:07.980
I am also pleased to
welcome to these exercises

02:45:07.980 --> 02:45:10.620
the distinguished members
of our 50th reunion

02:45:10.620 --> 02:45:24.970
class, the great class of 1957.

02:45:24.970 --> 02:45:28.860
It is my pleasure to introduce
our commencement speaker,

02:45:28.860 --> 02:45:34.200
Dr. Charles M. Vest,
MIT President Emeritus,

02:45:34.200 --> 02:45:36.870
professor of
mechanical engineering,

02:45:36.870 --> 02:45:39.480
and President-elect
of the National

02:45:39.480 --> 02:45:41.980
Academy of Engineering.

02:45:41.980 --> 02:45:44.570
Ladies and gentlemen,
distinguished guests,

02:45:44.570 --> 02:45:48.000
graduates, and
graduates-to-be, Dr. Vest.

02:45:55.690 --> 02:45:58.070
VEST: Thank you, Dana.

02:45:58.070 --> 02:46:01.730
Before I begin, I
must tell all of you

02:46:01.730 --> 02:46:04.610
that I have listened to
more commencement speeches

02:46:04.610 --> 02:46:06.930
than you can imagine.

02:46:06.930 --> 02:46:09.960
I've also invited more
commencement speakers

02:46:09.960 --> 02:46:13.500
to campuses than
you can imagine.

02:46:13.500 --> 02:46:16.320
From this, I learned
that students usually

02:46:16.320 --> 02:46:20.040
feel they were short-changed.

02:46:20.040 --> 02:46:23.790
Hypothetically-- now,
just hypothetically--

02:46:23.790 --> 02:46:29.810
they might say things like,
geez, Harvard got two Bills

02:46:29.810 --> 02:46:33.440
and all we got is one Chuck.

02:46:33.440 --> 02:46:35.240
But it's okay.

02:46:35.240 --> 02:46:40.340
Because I want to speak
to you, not to the world.

02:46:40.340 --> 02:46:43.790
And because this
is MIT, I'm going

02:46:43.790 --> 02:46:47.240
to talk to you about
two big things--

02:46:47.240 --> 02:46:50.870
opportunity and service.

02:46:50.870 --> 02:46:53.930
Here are two things I
know about opportunity.

02:46:53.930 --> 02:46:58.790
First, MIT is the greatest
place on the planet

02:46:58.790 --> 02:47:03.650
when it comes to radiating
education, opportunity,

02:47:03.650 --> 02:47:05.490
and service.

02:47:05.490 --> 02:47:09.510
Second, you never
know when or how

02:47:09.510 --> 02:47:12.480
opportunity will materialize.

02:47:12.480 --> 02:47:15.360
Don't try to plan
it or predict it,

02:47:15.360 --> 02:47:18.910
because you will
undoubtedly be wrong.

02:47:18.910 --> 02:47:23.160
Now, how do I know these two
things about opportunity?

02:47:23.160 --> 02:47:26.520
I know them because
of the two letters

02:47:26.520 --> 02:47:31.880
I received from MIT
during one 22-year period.

02:47:31.880 --> 02:47:36.810
I received the first
letter from MIT in 1968.

02:47:36.810 --> 02:47:40.140
It informed me that
the Institute was not

02:47:40.140 --> 02:47:46.300
interested in my application to
become an assistant professor.

02:47:46.300 --> 02:47:50.350
So in Ann Arbor, I happily
taught, did research,

02:47:50.350 --> 02:47:52.820
wrote a book, painted
the house, helped

02:47:52.820 --> 02:47:56.370
Becky raise our children,
and above all else,

02:47:56.370 --> 02:47:59.180
avoided committee assignments.

02:47:59.180 --> 02:48:05.150
But 22 years later, in 1990,
a second letter came from MIT.

02:48:05.150 --> 02:48:10.100
This one asked me to
serve as MIT'S president.

02:48:10.100 --> 02:48:14.480
Not in my wildest dreams as
a young faculty member could

02:48:14.480 --> 02:48:16.670
I have imagined
that one day I would

02:48:16.670 --> 02:48:23.280
be called to serve as president
of this remarkable institution.

02:48:23.280 --> 02:48:25.835
So always read
your mail from MIT.

02:48:28.730 --> 02:48:31.160
There's an outside
chance that instead

02:48:31.160 --> 02:48:36.890
of asking you for a donation,
it may ask you to be president.

02:48:36.890 --> 02:48:39.850
Or perhaps,
commencement speaker.

02:48:39.850 --> 02:48:42.020
Now, being president
of MIT brought

02:48:42.020 --> 02:48:46.580
me experiences that I could
barely have dreamed of.

02:48:46.580 --> 02:48:49.850
Because of the opportunities
created by education

02:48:49.850 --> 02:48:53.690
and by the place of
MIT on the world stage,

02:48:53.690 --> 02:48:58.820
I would come to meet, know, or
work with the kings, queens,

02:48:58.820 --> 02:49:03.650
presidents, premiers, or prime
ministers of many nations;

02:49:03.650 --> 02:49:07.460
with the first human to
set foot on the moon;

02:49:07.460 --> 02:49:10.400
with great artists
and musicians;

02:49:10.400 --> 02:49:13.130
with the leaders of
huge corporations;

02:49:13.130 --> 02:49:17.010
and with remarkable
young entrepreneurs.

02:49:17.010 --> 02:49:20.910
So what happens when you're
with the powerful and famous?

02:49:20.910 --> 02:49:26.670
Well, in 2001, my wife Becky and
I had tea with Queen Elizabeth

02:49:26.670 --> 02:49:29.130
at Buckingham Palace.

02:49:29.130 --> 02:49:32.220
Would you like to know
what she said to me?

02:49:32.220 --> 02:49:36.960
She asked me what I
thought about Harry Potter.

02:49:36.960 --> 02:49:39.420
Thank goodness I
had heard enough

02:49:39.420 --> 02:49:44.370
that I knew to mumble something
about Muggles and Hogwarts.

02:49:44.370 --> 02:49:47.730
But believe me, it
was a close call.

02:49:47.730 --> 02:49:48.900
Or consider this.

02:49:48.900 --> 02:49:53.670
In 1994, I found myself in the
East Room of the White House

02:49:53.670 --> 02:49:57.330
when all the living Apollo
astronauts came together

02:49:57.330 --> 02:50:01.080
to celebrate the 25th
anniversary of the first lunar

02:50:01.080 --> 02:50:02.870
landing.

02:50:02.870 --> 02:50:06.920
As I was leaving that
amazing event, both Carl

02:50:06.920 --> 02:50:10.580
Sagan and the President
of the United States

02:50:10.580 --> 02:50:14.420
sought me out to shake my
hand and thank me personally

02:50:14.420 --> 02:50:19.510
for a small role I had recently
played in our space program.

02:50:19.510 --> 02:50:21.760
With swelled head
and adrenalin rush

02:50:21.760 --> 02:50:25.450
I floated out of the White
House to catch a taxicab.

02:50:25.450 --> 02:50:29.120
Then I heard someone on
the street call my name.

02:50:29.120 --> 02:50:32.180
I turned around to find
a former MIT graduate

02:50:32.180 --> 02:50:35.210
student who proceeded
to berate me

02:50:35.210 --> 02:50:38.570
with a five-minute litany
of pent-up complaints

02:50:38.570 --> 02:50:41.690
about some aspects of
her experience here.

02:50:41.690 --> 02:50:44.870
She was certain that I had
been personally responsible

02:50:44.870 --> 02:50:46.610
for all of them.

02:50:46.610 --> 02:50:50.360
So there's nothing like
encountering an MIT graduate

02:50:50.360 --> 02:50:52.320
to keep your head
out of the clouds

02:50:52.320 --> 02:50:54.860
and your feet on the ground.

02:50:54.860 --> 02:51:00.410
But my real point here
is that education and MIT

02:51:00.410 --> 02:51:04.260
opened amazing doors of
opportunities to me--

02:51:04.260 --> 02:51:07.490
opportunity to learn,
opportunity to teach,

02:51:07.490 --> 02:51:10.930
opportunity to serve
a greater good.

02:51:10.930 --> 02:51:14.080
Indeed the opportunity
to serve is what

02:51:14.080 --> 02:51:17.560
I have valued above all else.

02:51:17.560 --> 02:51:21.610
I had the opportunity to
serve as a voice for science,

02:51:21.610 --> 02:51:24.460
engineering, and
higher education,

02:51:24.460 --> 02:51:26.830
and for the importance
of our nation being

02:51:26.830 --> 02:51:31.180
open to scholars from all
over the world at a time

02:51:31.180 --> 02:51:35.610
when America sorely needed
to think about these things.

02:51:35.610 --> 02:51:38.550
And I want you to know
that the people I have most

02:51:38.550 --> 02:51:43.410
valued knowing and working with
are not the leaders of nations

02:51:43.410 --> 02:51:45.390
or corporate titans.

02:51:45.390 --> 02:51:50.040
They are my faculty colleagues,
the intellectual leaders,

02:51:50.040 --> 02:51:54.720
the teachers and scholars
who are the essence of MIT,

02:51:54.720 --> 02:51:58.290
and who give you, and
gave me, the opportunity

02:51:58.290 --> 02:52:01.740
to literally change the world.

02:52:01.740 --> 02:52:04.350
The opportunities for
which they, the faculty,

02:52:04.350 --> 02:52:07.500
have prepared you will
be as breathtaking

02:52:07.500 --> 02:52:09.420
as they are formidable.

02:52:09.420 --> 02:52:13.540
And most of them are
beyond today's imagination.

02:52:13.540 --> 02:52:17.070
One reason is that they
are mostly global in scale

02:52:17.070 --> 02:52:19.700
and very complex.

02:52:19.700 --> 02:52:22.910
Another reason is the
continuous acceleration

02:52:22.910 --> 02:52:25.340
of technological progress.

02:52:25.340 --> 02:52:28.940
The future is rushing at
us more quickly every year.

02:52:28.940 --> 02:52:31.040
Just consider this.

02:52:31.040 --> 02:52:33.220
After the automobile
was introduced,

02:52:33.220 --> 02:52:37.340
it took 55 years, in
essence a lifetime,

02:52:37.340 --> 02:52:42.810
for 25% of the US
population to have a car.

02:52:42.810 --> 02:52:46.650
It took 35 years, the
length of a typical career,

02:52:46.650 --> 02:52:51.450
for the telephone to reach
25% of the US population.

02:52:51.450 --> 02:52:54.570
It took 23 years
for radio, 16 years

02:52:54.570 --> 02:52:57.960
for the personal computer,
13 years for cell phones,

02:52:57.960 --> 02:53:01.680
and only seven years
for the world wide web.

02:53:01.680 --> 02:53:06.060
Do you feel the acceleration,
this exponential growth?

02:53:06.060 --> 02:53:08.310
Well, we'd better
get used to it.

02:53:08.310 --> 02:53:11.990
3,000 new books are
published every day,

02:53:11.990 --> 02:53:13.950
and the amount of
technical information

02:53:13.950 --> 02:53:16.440
is doubling every two years.

02:53:16.440 --> 02:53:19.740
But along with all of this
acceleration and exploding

02:53:19.740 --> 02:53:25.650
information, you have
entirely new tools.

02:53:25.650 --> 02:53:29.610
Many of these new tools come
from information technology

02:53:29.610 --> 02:53:33.000
or from 21st century
life science.

02:53:33.000 --> 02:53:36.300
Your generation is
already leading us

02:53:36.300 --> 02:53:40.480
into a new domain of
global interaction.

02:53:40.480 --> 02:53:45.040
I am convinced that your way
of communicating and working--

02:53:45.040 --> 02:53:49.120
Second Life, Wikipedia,
YouTube, social networking,

02:53:49.120 --> 02:53:52.150
social computing,
open innovation--

02:53:52.150 --> 02:53:56.110
these things reflect a
fundamental transformation.

02:53:56.110 --> 02:53:59.770
You can and must guide
this transformation.

02:53:59.770 --> 02:54:02.240
You can use it to make money.

02:54:02.240 --> 02:54:05.200
You can use it to revel in
catching politicians and movie

02:54:05.200 --> 02:54:08.260
stars making stupid mistakes.

02:54:08.260 --> 02:54:13.090
Or you can use it to bring
what James Surowiecki calls

02:54:13.090 --> 02:54:15.550
the wisdom of crowds--

02:54:15.550 --> 02:54:18.850
to work together to
solve important problems

02:54:18.850 --> 02:54:23.980
and to build a more inclusive,
engaged, and more egalitarian

02:54:23.980 --> 02:54:25.660
world society.

02:54:25.660 --> 02:54:28.050
It's your choice.

02:54:28.050 --> 02:54:29.330
That's about information.

02:54:29.330 --> 02:54:31.960
What about the life sciences?

02:54:31.960 --> 02:54:34.670
We're barreling along
three great frontiers

02:54:34.670 --> 02:54:39.690
of 21st century life science
and biotechnology, frontiers

02:54:39.690 --> 02:54:44.440
that present extraordinary
challenges and opportunities.

02:54:44.440 --> 02:54:48.390
The first is to realize the
promise of genomic medicine,

02:54:48.390 --> 02:54:51.480
to put a powerful base
of rational science

02:54:51.480 --> 02:54:54.540
beneath the practice
of human medicine,

02:54:54.540 --> 02:54:59.550
and to tailor medical treatments
to each individual patient.

02:54:59.550 --> 02:55:02.240
The second challenge is
to dramatically advance

02:55:02.240 --> 02:55:05.980
our understanding of the
human brain and mind,

02:55:05.980 --> 02:55:11.040
of memory, learning, and
communication, and to attack

02:55:11.040 --> 02:55:13.980
the mental and emotional
illnesses that today

02:55:13.980 --> 02:55:17.510
wreak havoc on so many lives.

02:55:17.510 --> 02:55:19.840
The third is to
revolutionize much

02:55:19.840 --> 02:55:24.760
of engineering using the
stuff of life as templates,

02:55:24.760 --> 02:55:28.480
and using biological
mechanisms to design and grow

02:55:28.480 --> 02:55:32.470
new materials and to create
new production techniques that

02:55:32.470 --> 02:55:35.470
mimic the efficiency
of nature, thereby

02:55:35.470 --> 02:55:38.710
greatly reducing the
environmental footprint left

02:55:38.710 --> 02:55:40.680
by industry.

02:55:40.680 --> 02:55:43.440
Or to cite a more
prosaic example,

02:55:43.440 --> 02:55:45.690
last year researchers
announced that they

02:55:45.690 --> 02:55:50.880
had cloned pigs that made
their own omega-3 fatty acid.

02:55:50.880 --> 02:55:53.980
Imagine, on the horizon
as health food--

02:55:53.980 --> 02:55:55.560
sausage.

02:55:55.560 --> 02:55:56.940
And there's more.

02:55:56.940 --> 02:55:59.340
Already the term
biohacking is heard

02:55:59.340 --> 02:56:02.130
along our infinite corridor.

02:56:02.130 --> 02:56:08.070
Biohacking-- just think about
the significance of that term.

02:56:08.070 --> 02:56:11.340
It of course heralds the
advent of synthetic biology,

02:56:11.340 --> 02:56:13.620
the fusion of
engineering and biology

02:56:13.620 --> 02:56:17.230
to design and build novel
biological functions

02:56:17.230 --> 02:56:19.050
and systems.

02:56:19.050 --> 02:56:21.030
Will all of these
new capabilities

02:56:21.030 --> 02:56:24.840
of information science,
life science, engineering,

02:56:24.840 --> 02:56:29.980
and their combination be
used for good or for ill?

02:56:29.980 --> 02:56:33.070
How will you grapple with the
ethical and legal questions

02:56:33.070 --> 02:56:36.250
that will come along
with these new powers?

02:56:36.250 --> 02:56:39.310
How will you influence
the public discourse

02:56:39.310 --> 02:56:42.520
and the public action
on these questions?

02:56:42.520 --> 02:56:44.830
These are enormous
challenges, in part

02:56:44.830 --> 02:56:47.470
because technological
advancement usually

02:56:47.470 --> 02:56:50.110
outpaces social advancement.

02:56:50.110 --> 02:56:52.810
For example, the
design of the internet

02:56:52.810 --> 02:56:57.250
that forms the basis of so much
opportunity in the world today

02:56:57.250 --> 02:57:00.430
was predicated on mutual trust--

02:57:00.430 --> 02:57:03.310
trust that you are
who you say you are

02:57:03.310 --> 02:57:06.720
and that you are doing
what you say you are doing.

02:57:06.720 --> 02:57:10.830
But now, of course, we have
viruses, worms, and phishing.

02:57:10.830 --> 02:57:16.140
That's P-H-I-S-H-I-N-G. You
guys will have to explain

02:57:16.140 --> 02:57:18.630
to your parents what that means.

02:57:18.630 --> 02:57:21.570
But these human-developed
technological evils

02:57:21.570 --> 02:57:23.520
can be very serious.

02:57:23.520 --> 02:57:27.840
Just a couple of weeks
ago, hackers virtually

02:57:27.840 --> 02:57:32.640
brought down the cyber-structure
of an entire nation, Estonia.

02:57:32.640 --> 02:57:34.560
This is a highly wired
country, and it's

02:57:34.560 --> 02:57:36.720
government,
business, and economy

02:57:36.720 --> 02:57:39.630
we're very seriously impacted.

02:57:39.630 --> 02:57:42.030
Because of all of
this, colleagues

02:57:42.030 --> 02:57:46.980
tell me that the internet must
now be redesigned with security

02:57:46.980 --> 02:57:50.250
rather than trust at its core.

02:57:50.250 --> 02:57:52.050
And we are all
aware of the debates

02:57:52.050 --> 02:57:56.400
around genetic counseling, stem
cells, and genetic modification

02:57:56.400 --> 02:57:58.620
of crops and food.

02:57:58.620 --> 02:58:02.040
Such redesigns and such
debates are a necessary part

02:58:02.040 --> 02:58:04.530
of life in a free society.

02:58:04.530 --> 02:58:08.580
But you must be prepared to
engage in the public dialogue

02:58:08.580 --> 02:58:12.930
and bring your own moral
compasses and your commitments

02:58:12.930 --> 02:58:16.470
to applying the rationality
of science and engineering

02:58:16.470 --> 02:58:19.950
to improving the
human condition.

02:58:19.950 --> 02:58:24.500
But let me turn the
clock back 50 years.

02:58:24.500 --> 02:58:30.200
Each of us has a few moments
of seemingly random memories

02:58:30.200 --> 02:58:34.670
of the past that from time
to time spring into our minds

02:58:34.670 --> 02:58:39.120
with full clarity of
sight, sound, and feeling.

02:58:39.120 --> 02:58:42.660
One such memory for me is simply
of walking to high school one

02:58:42.660 --> 02:58:45.950
sunny morning in 1957.

02:58:45.950 --> 02:58:49.100
I lived only two houses
away from high school,

02:58:49.100 --> 02:58:52.100
so if I left home when I
heard the first bell ring

02:58:52.100 --> 02:58:55.430
I could make it into my
seat before the second bell.

02:58:55.430 --> 02:58:57.410
But be that as it
may, I remember

02:58:57.410 --> 02:59:02.720
on that day a feeling of
absolute joy, well-being,

02:59:02.720 --> 02:59:05.590
good fortune, and optimism.

02:59:05.590 --> 02:59:08.050
The air was warm and clear.

02:59:08.050 --> 02:59:10.870
The American flag was fluttering
in front of the school.

02:59:10.870 --> 02:59:14.350
The sky was blue, and
the sky was the limit.

02:59:14.350 --> 02:59:16.300
Life was good.

02:59:16.300 --> 02:59:19.330
The fact is that joy,
well-being, good fortune

02:59:19.330 --> 02:59:22.690
and optimism were the right
things for me to feel.

02:59:22.690 --> 02:59:26.530
Life indeed was good if
you were a boy growing up

02:59:26.530 --> 02:59:29.290
middle-class and white
in an American college

02:59:29.290 --> 02:59:32.020
town in the 1950s.

02:59:32.020 --> 02:59:35.530
Now, much water has flowed over
the proverbial dam in the years

02:59:35.530 --> 02:59:36.910
since then.

02:59:36.910 --> 02:59:38.200
But you know what?

02:59:38.200 --> 02:59:42.940
I still believe that optimism
was very well founded.

02:59:42.940 --> 02:59:46.870
Now, you may say, yes,
but when you were young,

02:59:46.870 --> 02:59:50.680
your world was much simpler,
and certainly more insular

02:59:50.680 --> 02:59:52.100
than ours.

02:59:52.100 --> 02:59:56.350
Today, we face incredible
global challenges.

02:59:56.350 --> 03:00:00.220
We have to improve the world's
health, economies, security,

03:00:00.220 --> 03:00:02.480
and quality of life.

03:00:02.480 --> 03:00:05.740
We have to provide water
and food for the burgeoning

03:00:05.740 --> 03:00:07.780
peoples of the Earth.

03:00:07.780 --> 03:00:12.130
We have to give them clean air
to breathe and clear blue skies

03:00:12.130 --> 03:00:14.650
to make their spirits soar.

03:00:14.650 --> 03:00:19.030
We have to provide the energy
that will lessen their burdens,

03:00:19.030 --> 03:00:21.550
integrate them into
the world community,

03:00:21.550 --> 03:00:25.130
and give them
economic opportunity.

03:00:25.130 --> 03:00:29.560
We have to cure their ills and
safeguard their well-being.

03:00:29.560 --> 03:00:30.760
Come on, now.

03:00:30.760 --> 03:00:33.730
We have a much harder
road than you did.

03:00:33.730 --> 03:00:36.520
Why should we be optimistic?

03:00:36.520 --> 03:00:39.300
Well, in some ways
you are right.

03:00:39.300 --> 03:00:43.720
But I want to leave you
with another perspective.

03:00:43.720 --> 03:00:46.360
When I was young, I sat
in our comfortable home

03:00:46.360 --> 03:00:49.240
in front of a
black-and-white television

03:00:49.240 --> 03:00:53.280
and watched an interview
with Dr. Tom Dooley,

03:00:53.280 --> 03:00:57.210
an American medical doctor
serving people in Asia

03:00:57.210 --> 03:01:00.660
in the midst of unfathomable
poverty and dire living

03:01:00.660 --> 03:01:03.240
conditions.

03:01:03.240 --> 03:01:06.960
Dr. Dooley held up in
front of the camera

03:01:06.960 --> 03:01:14.610
a tiny, ill, starving
child with distended belly.

03:01:14.610 --> 03:01:19.530
Now, I have to explain that in
1950s, such sights were never

03:01:19.530 --> 03:01:23.580
seen on television
or even in magazines.

03:01:23.580 --> 03:01:28.250
It was shocking, and I
recoiled emotionally.

03:01:28.250 --> 03:01:34.110
Then he calmly said in essence,
when you look at this child,

03:01:34.110 --> 03:01:37.190
you see something horrifying.

03:01:37.190 --> 03:01:39.170
But I look at this
child and know

03:01:39.170 --> 03:01:45.460
that I have the knowledge and
the skill to make him well.

03:01:45.460 --> 03:01:48.310
I believe that
simple statement is

03:01:48.310 --> 03:01:50.260
a metaphor for
what the graduates

03:01:50.260 --> 03:01:54.190
of a great university,
especially one with the focus

03:01:54.190 --> 03:01:57.940
of MIT, can and must do--

03:01:57.940 --> 03:02:01.200
make the world well.

03:02:01.200 --> 03:02:04.680
Yes, you do grasp the
complexity of our world,

03:02:04.680 --> 03:02:08.110
and you understand the
enormity of its challenges.

03:02:08.110 --> 03:02:12.980
But you also have new
tools to resolve them.

03:02:12.980 --> 03:02:16.010
In the end, I believe that
knowledge and skill trump

03:02:16.010 --> 03:02:19.970
ignorance, and that
optimism trumps pessimism.

03:02:19.970 --> 03:02:24.230
If you believe this, and if
you embrace the opportunity

03:02:24.230 --> 03:02:29.210
to serve, you will find personal
happiness and fulfillment

03:02:29.210 --> 03:02:31.580
beyond expectation.

03:02:31.580 --> 03:02:36.320
And you will benefit our world
and your fellow men and women

03:02:36.320 --> 03:02:37.922
beyond measure.

03:02:37.922 --> 03:02:39.866
Good luck and godspeed.

03:02:54.960 --> 03:02:57.240
MEAD: Thank you, Dr. Vest.

03:02:57.240 --> 03:03:00.900
And I should say thank you for
that thoughtful and challenging

03:03:00.900 --> 03:03:01.530
address.

03:03:01.530 --> 03:03:04.260
But even more so,
thank you again

03:03:04.260 --> 03:03:08.820
for your 14 years of leadership,
and your dedication, wisdom,

03:03:08.820 --> 03:03:12.279
and hard work on behalf of MIT.

03:03:12.279 --> 03:03:13.758
And we wish you--

03:03:13.758 --> 03:03:16.716
[APPLAUSE]

03:03:24.130 --> 03:03:28.110
And we wish you the
best and godspeed

03:03:28.110 --> 03:03:32.490
as you assume leadership
of the Academy.

03:03:32.490 --> 03:03:36.090
Now, Mr. Eric Weese, president
of the Graduate Student

03:03:36.090 --> 03:03:39.330
Council, will give
a salute to MIT

03:03:39.330 --> 03:03:42.180
from the graduate student body.

03:03:42.180 --> 03:03:47.390
Following this, Miss Susan Shin,
president of the senior class,

03:03:47.390 --> 03:03:51.210
will present the class gift
to President Hockfield,

03:03:51.210 --> 03:03:53.544
after which the
president will deliver

03:03:53.544 --> 03:03:56.034
her charge to the graduates.

03:04:06.010 --> 03:04:07.130
WEESE: Thank you.

03:04:07.130 --> 03:04:09.230
One of the great
things about MIT

03:04:09.230 --> 03:04:12.380
is how it draws students
from around the world

03:04:12.380 --> 03:04:14.880
and from many
different backgrounds.

03:04:14.880 --> 03:04:16.680
You wouldn't be
here today, though,

03:04:16.680 --> 03:04:19.420
if you didn't have
some things in common--

03:04:19.420 --> 03:04:23.140
a willingness to work
hard, to persevere,

03:04:23.140 --> 03:04:27.760
to consume distinctly unhealthy
quantities of caffeine--

03:04:27.760 --> 03:04:31.347
in short, you've set
a very high standard.

03:04:31.347 --> 03:04:33.180
And it's one that I've
been trying to reach.

03:04:33.180 --> 03:04:35.430
But the other day
as I was attempting

03:04:35.430 --> 03:04:40.480
to do thesis research, I was
waylaid again by the internet.

03:04:40.480 --> 03:04:43.650
You might note that I'm
not graduating with you.

03:04:43.650 --> 03:04:47.250
I didn't completely
waste my time, though.

03:04:47.250 --> 03:04:51.360
Because I stumbled upon
an interesting complaint

03:04:51.360 --> 03:04:55.070
from someone with
a $150,000 job.

03:04:55.070 --> 03:04:55.920
It reads as follows.

03:04:59.140 --> 03:05:03.954
"We never seem to have enough
money to do anything fun."

03:05:08.360 --> 03:05:14.120
It may well be that
none of us will ever

03:05:14.120 --> 03:05:18.490
have all that we want.

03:05:18.490 --> 03:05:24.300
But it's also likely that
we will all have much more

03:05:24.300 --> 03:05:26.880
than we need.

03:05:26.880 --> 03:05:31.370
So as you contemplate your
possibly imminent ascent

03:05:31.370 --> 03:05:36.200
up the pay scale,
please keep in mind

03:05:36.200 --> 03:05:42.520
that we are among the
most fortunate ever.

03:05:42.520 --> 03:05:46.810
And we certainly ought to
have enough to have some fun

03:05:46.810 --> 03:05:49.380
and to share.

03:05:49.380 --> 03:05:54.675
Congratulations on your
hard-earned degrees, and best

03:05:54.675 --> 03:05:56.390
of luck with the future.

03:05:56.390 --> 03:05:57.370
Thank you.

03:06:11.090 --> 03:06:12.070
SHIN: Thank you.

03:06:17.000 --> 03:06:19.970
My friend Melvin
introduced me to Heroes,

03:06:19.970 --> 03:06:23.630
a TV show that tells the
story of ordinary people

03:06:23.630 --> 03:06:27.145
who discover that they
possess extraordinary powers.

03:06:27.145 --> 03:06:28.520
When they find
out that they have

03:06:28.520 --> 03:06:32.220
these remarkable abilities, they
begin to accept their gifts,

03:06:32.220 --> 03:06:34.970
learn to control their
powers, and discover others

03:06:34.970 --> 03:06:37.580
who are as special as they are.

03:06:37.580 --> 03:06:39.720
As these strangers from
across the globe come

03:06:39.720 --> 03:06:41.850
to meet each other, they
find that they are all

03:06:41.850 --> 03:06:43.740
united by a common goal--

03:06:43.740 --> 03:06:46.140
to save the world.

03:06:46.140 --> 03:06:49.080
Hiro Nakamura is one of
the show's main characters,

03:06:49.080 --> 03:06:52.550
and he has the ability
to bend space and time.

03:06:52.550 --> 03:06:56.090
At first, Nakamura uses
his abilities for fun,

03:06:56.090 --> 03:06:58.610
showing off to his friends
and taking little vacations

03:06:58.610 --> 03:06:59.870
through time.

03:06:59.870 --> 03:07:01.850
But he quickly realizes
that because he

03:07:01.850 --> 03:07:04.700
has this power he has
a great responsibility

03:07:04.700 --> 03:07:07.560
to the rest of the world.

03:07:07.560 --> 03:07:09.800
On a trip into the
future, Nakamura

03:07:09.800 --> 03:07:13.200
witnesses a horrific crime
that wipes out an entire city,

03:07:13.200 --> 03:07:15.000
killing millions.

03:07:15.000 --> 03:07:17.400
Once he returns to the
present, his doubts

03:07:17.400 --> 03:07:19.760
cause him to lose
faith in his abilities.

03:07:19.760 --> 03:07:21.900
And because of these
doubts, he is suddenly

03:07:21.900 --> 03:07:24.810
unable to travel through time.

03:07:24.810 --> 03:07:27.060
He begins to worry if his
powers will be strong enough

03:07:27.060 --> 03:07:30.180
to defeat his enemies,
to save his friends,

03:07:30.180 --> 03:07:34.590
and to build the future that
he wants to see for himself.

03:07:34.590 --> 03:07:37.930
What Nakamura finally
realizes is this--

03:07:37.930 --> 03:07:43.580
everyone has their fears,
but heroes overcome them.

03:07:43.580 --> 03:07:45.860
When I look out into
the audience today,

03:07:45.860 --> 03:07:49.820
I see my friends, my
classmates, my heroes,

03:07:49.820 --> 03:07:53.060
and I recognize their
extraordinary gifts.

03:07:53.060 --> 03:07:55.520
There are students here who
have published research,

03:07:55.520 --> 03:07:59.600
patented new technology, and
started their own companies.

03:07:59.600 --> 03:08:01.730
Others have won
national championships

03:08:01.730 --> 03:08:05.509
or even courageously battled
a life-threatening illness.

03:08:05.509 --> 03:08:07.800
All of us have persevered
through the hardships of life

03:08:07.800 --> 03:08:11.880
at MIT and achieved greatness,
because when it came time

03:08:11.880 --> 03:08:14.280
to choose between
giving up and hoping,

03:08:14.280 --> 03:08:16.890
we made the right choice.

03:08:16.890 --> 03:08:20.160
When that opportunity to
be a hero comes to you,

03:08:20.160 --> 03:08:22.160
what will you do?

03:08:22.160 --> 03:08:24.380
When a moment of
consequence arrives,

03:08:24.380 --> 03:08:27.415
it is my hope that we
will relish that moment,

03:08:27.415 --> 03:08:30.335
rise to the occasion,
and inspire by example.

03:08:33.510 --> 03:08:36.360
We, the graduating
class of 2007,

03:08:36.360 --> 03:08:39.150
may not be able to fly
or teleport ourselves.

03:08:39.150 --> 03:08:42.610
But we don't need these special
powers to save the world.

03:08:42.610 --> 03:08:46.320
In fact, we have something
much more important--

03:08:46.320 --> 03:08:50.110
the hope that we can change
the future for the better.

03:08:50.110 --> 03:08:52.210
All of us set to
graduate have struggled

03:08:52.210 --> 03:08:55.870
to hold onto this hope despite
all that MIT has thrown at us.

03:08:55.870 --> 03:08:57.940
But it's because of
the others here today--

03:08:57.940 --> 03:09:00.940
our family, friends,
and teachers--

03:09:00.940 --> 03:09:03.100
that it has remained strong.

03:09:03.100 --> 03:09:06.280
And it's because of where we
are today, the place to which we

03:09:06.280 --> 03:09:09.340
are now saying goodbye, that we
have the chance of fulfilling

03:09:09.340 --> 03:09:12.480
our hopes and dreams.

03:09:12.480 --> 03:09:15.030
When we give ourselves
something to look forward to,

03:09:15.030 --> 03:09:17.700
no matter how great or small
the event, we are giving

03:09:17.700 --> 03:09:19.660
ourselves hope.

03:09:19.660 --> 03:09:21.940
When there is hope
built into tomorrow,

03:09:21.940 --> 03:09:24.610
there are all kinds of
opportunities to overcome

03:09:24.610 --> 03:09:27.840
the obstacles we face today.

03:09:27.840 --> 03:09:30.990
In a short while, we will
walk across this stage

03:09:30.990 --> 03:09:35.040
as MIT graduates, having
conquered our fears and doubts,

03:09:35.040 --> 03:09:37.530
ready to save the world.

03:09:37.530 --> 03:09:40.110
Today is a day of
great happiness.

03:09:40.110 --> 03:09:43.269
We've survived the Institute,
and we have so much

03:09:43.269 --> 03:09:44.736
to look forward to after today.

03:09:44.736 --> 03:09:45.714
Thank you.

03:09:56.000 --> 03:09:59.441
MEESE: And now, the moment
we've all been waiting for.

03:09:59.441 --> 03:10:01.760
SHIN: For years we
have worn the Brass Rat

03:10:01.760 --> 03:10:05.500
with the beaver sitting on
us, representing the hardships

03:10:05.500 --> 03:10:07.780
imposed on MIT students.

03:10:07.780 --> 03:10:10.720
Take one last look at
the Boston skyline, which

03:10:10.720 --> 03:10:13.104
has symbolized the
outside world awaiting us,

03:10:13.104 --> 03:10:16.026
and join me in turning
around your Brass Rat.

03:10:27.250 --> 03:10:30.100
May the Cambridge skyline be a
constant reminder of your time

03:10:30.100 --> 03:10:33.020
spent at MIT.

03:10:33.020 --> 03:10:36.680
And now for the presentation
of the senior gift.

03:10:36.680 --> 03:10:39.590
The average class participation
rate for four years

03:10:39.590 --> 03:10:41.870
has been 33%.

03:10:41.870 --> 03:10:44.090
I am proud to announce
that our class has

03:10:44.090 --> 03:10:48.804
broken MIT's record with an
astounding 52% participation

03:10:48.804 --> 03:10:49.304
rate.

03:10:58.210 --> 03:11:01.120
A portion of the class gift
will go towards the class

03:11:01.120 --> 03:11:03.610
of 2007's study abroad fund.

03:11:03.610 --> 03:11:05.320
We hope that this fund
will help students

03:11:05.320 --> 03:11:07.360
broaden their
educations and give them

03:11:07.360 --> 03:11:10.940
a well-deserved break from MIT.

03:11:10.940 --> 03:11:12.920
On behalf of the
senior class, I would

03:11:12.920 --> 03:11:16.100
like to thank MIT Alumni
Association President Martin

03:11:16.100 --> 03:11:20.868
Tang for matching our gift
with a generous $15,000.

03:11:20.868 --> 03:11:23.263
Now I would like to ask
President Hockfield to join me

03:11:23.263 --> 03:11:24.221
at the podium.

03:11:26.900 --> 03:11:31.810
I'm honored to present
this check for $26,861

03:11:31.810 --> 03:11:34.891
from the class of 2007 to you.

03:11:34.891 --> 03:11:37.376
Congratulations, Class of 2007.

03:11:37.376 --> 03:11:38.370
We made it!

03:11:51.300 --> 03:11:57.180
HOCKFIELD: Thank you
very much, Class of 2007,

03:11:57.180 --> 03:12:00.280
for the enthusiasm
that 52% represents.

03:12:00.280 --> 03:12:03.150
Congratulations.

03:12:03.150 --> 03:12:07.880
Graduates of MIT,
this is your day.

03:12:07.880 --> 03:12:10.460
We have gathered
here in Killian Court

03:12:10.460 --> 03:12:14.360
to celebrate your successful
completion of demanding courses

03:12:14.360 --> 03:12:18.230
of study, and you have our
deepest respect for all

03:12:18.230 --> 03:12:20.820
that you have accomplished.

03:12:20.820 --> 03:12:23.797
But today is not yours alone.

03:12:23.797 --> 03:12:25.380
None of you would
be here this morning

03:12:25.380 --> 03:12:27.900
were it not for the
families and friends

03:12:27.900 --> 03:12:29.460
who have nurtured
and supported you

03:12:29.460 --> 03:12:34.300
since your childhood, who
have embraced your dreams

03:12:34.300 --> 03:12:36.970
and lighted your path.

03:12:36.970 --> 03:12:39.480
This is their day, too.

03:12:39.480 --> 03:12:42.420
Graduates, I invite you
please to rise and to join me

03:12:42.420 --> 03:12:44.895
in thanking your
families and friends.

03:13:11.660 --> 03:13:15.740
Now, I want to speak to
those of you graduating today

03:13:15.740 --> 03:13:19.010
about your path here at
MIT, and the path that

03:13:19.010 --> 03:13:22.890
leads from MIT into the world.

03:13:22.890 --> 03:13:25.530
Of course, before you arrived
here at MIT, each of you

03:13:25.530 --> 03:13:29.300
had already demonstrated
significant talents.

03:13:29.300 --> 03:13:32.390
That's why we invited you
to join our community.

03:13:32.390 --> 03:13:35.630
Once you arrived here, you
took up MIT's challenges.

03:13:35.630 --> 03:13:38.600
And working, I am certain,
harder than you ever

03:13:38.600 --> 03:13:42.590
had before, you've taken
your academic accomplishments

03:13:42.590 --> 03:13:44.990
to entirely new levels.

03:13:44.990 --> 03:13:47.270
Today's ceremony is our
community's expression

03:13:47.270 --> 03:13:51.340
of our pride and what
you have achieved.

03:13:51.340 --> 03:13:53.290
Our appreciation of
your accomplishments

03:13:53.290 --> 03:13:56.530
would be far too
narrow if included

03:13:56.530 --> 03:13:59.090
only your academic successes.

03:13:59.090 --> 03:14:02.330
Beyond the classroom
and the laboratory,

03:14:02.330 --> 03:14:06.010
you have excelled on the stage
and on the playing field,

03:14:06.010 --> 03:14:09.860
in service projects and
in entrepreneurship.

03:14:09.860 --> 03:14:12.590
And what is perhaps
most important,

03:14:12.590 --> 03:14:15.440
you have also begun to
distinguish yourselves

03:14:15.440 --> 03:14:17.570
as leaders.

03:14:17.570 --> 03:14:21.230
MIT itself has a deep
commitment to leadership,

03:14:21.230 --> 03:14:26.080
demonstrated time and
again in myriad ways.

03:14:26.080 --> 03:14:29.740
In the foundational science that
led to the new targeted cancer

03:14:29.740 --> 03:14:31.090
therapies--

03:14:31.090 --> 03:14:35.930
Gleevec for leukemia and
Herceptin for breast cancer.

03:14:35.930 --> 03:14:39.420
In innovative plans to
rebuild devastated New Orleans

03:14:39.420 --> 03:14:41.370
neighborhoods.

03:14:41.370 --> 03:14:43.710
In countless businesses
from startups

03:14:43.710 --> 03:14:47.610
in Kendall Square to industry
giants across the technology

03:14:47.610 --> 03:14:49.940
landscape.

03:14:49.940 --> 03:14:52.550
In fresh approaches to
meeting the world's energy

03:14:52.550 --> 03:14:56.600
needs, including new
technologies for energy storage

03:14:56.600 --> 03:14:59.800
and for solar energy conversion.

03:14:59.800 --> 03:15:04.420
And in novel uses of technology
to convey the immortal truths

03:15:04.420 --> 03:15:06.706
of the humanities.

03:15:06.706 --> 03:15:11.810
And in many different ways, from
the UPOP program to the $100K

03:15:11.810 --> 03:15:18.390
competition, from [? 2-007 ?]
to the Public Service Center,

03:15:18.390 --> 03:15:22.530
our goal has been to teach
you how to become leaders

03:15:22.530 --> 03:15:24.750
yourselves.

03:15:24.750 --> 03:15:28.560
We have done this,
graduates of MIT,

03:15:28.560 --> 03:15:32.920
because the world today
needs your leadership.

03:15:32.920 --> 03:15:34.600
We need your
leadership as we face

03:15:34.600 --> 03:15:36.790
the challenges of
an increasingly

03:15:36.790 --> 03:15:40.070
complex and
interdependent world.

03:15:40.070 --> 03:15:43.400
And we need your leadership
to develop new ways

03:15:43.400 --> 03:15:48.480
to bridge old divides, not only
between peoples and nations,

03:15:48.480 --> 03:15:52.570
but also between
technology and policy.

03:15:52.570 --> 03:16:00.430
MIT's enduring motto, "Mens
et manus," "Mind and hand,"

03:16:00.430 --> 03:16:02.710
is a reminder that leadership
in the modern world

03:16:02.710 --> 03:16:05.020
depends critically
on integrating

03:16:05.020 --> 03:16:10.070
across different perspectives
for the common good.

03:16:10.070 --> 03:16:12.680
From one point of
view, the leadership

03:16:12.680 --> 03:16:15.230
we call you to
assume today might

03:16:15.230 --> 03:16:20.290
appear to be an obligation,
perhaps a burden.

03:16:20.290 --> 03:16:23.340
But that would be
a grave mistake.

03:16:23.340 --> 03:16:27.780
Leadership is a privilege
and it is a joy,

03:16:27.780 --> 03:16:30.390
and I can assure you that in
using your talents to serve

03:16:30.390 --> 03:16:33.540
others you will find
the most enduring

03:16:33.540 --> 03:16:37.260
of personal satisfactions.

03:16:37.260 --> 03:16:40.470
Even as you leave this place
to become your generation's

03:16:40.470 --> 03:16:43.980
leaders, you will remain
members of this community.

03:16:43.980 --> 03:16:45.690
At the close of this
morning's ceremony,

03:16:45.690 --> 03:16:48.990
Martin Tang, the president
of the Alumni Association,

03:16:48.990 --> 03:16:52.482
will formally welcome you into
the Association's membership.

03:16:52.482 --> 03:16:53.940
And we hope that
your lives will be

03:16:53.940 --> 03:16:58.890
enriched by an ongoing
connection to the Institute.

03:16:58.890 --> 03:17:03.300
And it's my fervent hope that
as you join new communities,

03:17:03.300 --> 03:17:07.230
you will transmit to them the
values that define the MIT

03:17:07.230 --> 03:17:11.090
community; that you
will make integrity

03:17:11.090 --> 03:17:14.180
the touchstone of
your judgments;

03:17:14.180 --> 03:17:17.600
that you will exemplify
the pursuit of truth

03:17:17.600 --> 03:17:22.070
and an unwavering
drive for excellence.

03:17:22.070 --> 03:17:24.350
And that you will
continue to demonstrate

03:17:24.350 --> 03:17:29.360
the value of good old
fashioned hard work.

03:17:29.360 --> 03:17:32.310
Beyond these great
aspirations for you,

03:17:32.310 --> 03:17:37.470
I make a more pressing request.

03:17:37.470 --> 03:17:41.910
I ask of you to inspire your own
generation and the generations

03:17:41.910 --> 03:17:47.970
to come with a renewed sense
of optimism for the future.

03:17:47.970 --> 03:17:52.410
MIT's founding vision was
both practical and idealistic

03:17:52.410 --> 03:17:54.810
in its insistence
that our work must,

03:17:54.810 --> 03:17:57.810
as engraved across
the frieze of Lobby 7,

03:17:57.810 --> 03:18:03.380
advance industry, the arts,
agriculture, and commerce.

03:18:03.380 --> 03:18:06.260
That optimism for
a better future

03:18:06.260 --> 03:18:11.610
has made MIT a beacon
visible the world over.

03:18:11.610 --> 03:18:16.590
This weekend we will celebrate
MIT's optimism for the future

03:18:16.590 --> 03:18:20.890
by re-igniting one of our
most powerful symbols.

03:18:20.890 --> 03:18:22.600
After the late summer
sunset tomorrow

03:18:22.600 --> 03:18:26.380
evening, for the first time
in more than three decades

03:18:26.380 --> 03:18:31.070
we will fully relight
the great dome.

03:18:31.070 --> 03:18:35.270
The lighting of the dome signals
the importance of what we do.

03:18:35.270 --> 03:18:38.390
Here at MIT, we see
up close every day

03:18:38.390 --> 03:18:40.820
the countless ways that
science and technology

03:18:40.820 --> 03:18:42.920
benefit humankind.

03:18:42.920 --> 03:18:46.100
But if we are to
realize our optimism,

03:18:46.100 --> 03:18:49.430
we need to rekindle in others
the same love and passion

03:18:49.430 --> 03:18:53.750
for truth and discovery, for
creativity and problem solving,

03:18:53.750 --> 03:18:56.330
that brought us all here.

03:18:56.330 --> 03:18:59.120
I hope that each of you
will embrace this challenge

03:18:59.120 --> 03:19:03.400
as your own, that you will
let your light shine out

03:19:03.400 --> 03:19:07.240
to illuminate the
paths for others.

03:19:07.240 --> 03:19:09.250
Now, I would not set
you this challenge

03:19:09.250 --> 03:19:12.550
if I did not think and
truly believe that you

03:19:12.550 --> 03:19:14.530
could and would meet it.

03:19:14.530 --> 03:19:16.540
I have tremendous faith in you.

03:19:16.540 --> 03:19:21.010
Your intelligence, your
dedication, and your creativity

03:19:21.010 --> 03:19:23.680
have inspired us
during your time here.

03:19:23.680 --> 03:19:27.520
And I know that in the years
to come you will do even more,

03:19:27.520 --> 03:19:31.450
and surprise and delight us
with your further achievements.

03:19:31.450 --> 03:19:34.300
For your accomplishments
here at MIT,

03:19:34.300 --> 03:19:38.320
I offer you my heartfelt
congratulations graduates

03:19:38.320 --> 03:19:39.292
of MIT.

03:19:51.940 --> 03:19:54.540
MEAD: Thank you,
President Hockfield.

03:19:54.540 --> 03:20:00.440
And I should say, on behalf of
the Corporation , thank you.

03:20:00.440 --> 03:20:06.820
Thank your faculty, your staff,
and the entire MIT community

03:20:06.820 --> 03:20:10.940
for first, producing this
wonderful group of graduates,

03:20:10.940 --> 03:20:16.101
and secondly, for a very
successful year of leadership

03:20:16.101 --> 03:20:18.506
and excellence at MIT.

03:20:18.506 --> 03:20:19.468
Congratulations.

03:20:27.740 --> 03:20:30.240
We are finally there.

03:20:30.240 --> 03:20:33.150
By virtue of the authority
delegated to them

03:20:33.150 --> 03:20:35.250
by the Corporation
of the Massachusetts

03:20:35.250 --> 03:20:38.830
Institute of Technology,
and on the recommendation

03:20:38.830 --> 03:20:41.780
of the faculty,
President Hockfield

03:20:41.780 --> 03:20:45.440
will now present the
following degrees.

03:20:45.440 --> 03:20:52.620
Bachelor of Science, Bachelor
of Science, Master of Science.

03:20:52.620 --> 03:20:57.450
Bachelor of Science,
Master of Engineering.

03:20:57.450 --> 03:21:01.770
And the advanced degrees
for the School of Science

03:21:01.770 --> 03:21:05.980
and Whitaker College of Health,
Sciences, and Technology.

